      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>Mu DO UG A. LI:8 'PATENT SE IF-P AIS LNG FLOUR 217AIPMD. LADIES' LErIIER. I WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>Mu DO UG A. LI:8 'PATENT SE IF-P AIS LNG FLOUR 217AIPMD. LADIES' LErIIER. I WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>BIY DEAR NELLIE, F -.tile is a good material for doubtful weather. wear 4,3 the seaside and elsewhere. It is much Lighter than cloth, and wearaisqually well. Three. flounce skirts have returned by fashion, and a beige fonle was made in this three-decker style, but with a plain front breadth, covered with black guipure, in a enrved and foliateddesign. The flounces were edged and headed with three or four rows of black mackvine.stitching, and above each flounce was a band of-black glace silk., much stitched. Narrower bands of the stitched glace were continued above The lop flounce to the waist, separated by their own width, so that-the-beige fettle appeared to be striped with black. The bodice was quite plain at the- back , but had-long bolero fronts, bordered with ;Amid of the stitched black glac6. A bertha- shaped collar of black guipure finished the top of the bodice over a vest, chemisette, and collar of beigwookarred chiffon, with narrow bands of pastel. blue rsillion velvet run in and out through spaces Arranged for that purpose. The blue could be re. „t)laCsd by Wack if the dress were desired to be of a. half-mourning character. Worn with a large black hat and a black ruffle, it would be rather a distinguished-looking costume.</p>
        <p>For hot and stump days there will be the white serge or alpaca and the holland snit, with some- thing dainty and coot for afternoons. For doubtful weather there must be a provision of tailor-made cnatand.strirt, and tor a rim to the post on a wet clef there will be a waterproof ands, pair of stout boots. For the evening walk and listening to tlie band brmoontight a comfortable wrap will be necessary, and, as a rule, the winter coat itself is not too warm for this agreeable, but somewhat chilly amusement. When the weather is very hot and airless in towns it is very difficult for the dwellers therein to imagine how needful it is to provide against all these contingencies, and as a 'coosequence about eight persons in every ten are obliged to send home tor mara garments when they have been a coupe of days at the seaside; and the shops do a lively trade in warm capes, "clouds," and knitted or croetreted• shawls, to say nothing of ruffles and fur boas.</p>
        <p>In spite of the lifting of the half mourning cloud which has hung persistently over the public raiment this season, the general tone of yachting costumes is black and white; mauve or grey. cream serge, trimmed with black and white braid, is chosen for the generality of frocks, and black and white muslitts will constitute the majority of garden party frocks. Simple little muslin blouses are being made for wear underneath coats and boieros ; the latter are going out of favour, how- ever, the newest coat being more of a jacket, with short basque behind and elongated points in front, which give length to the figure. Most of these coats are made with roll collar and revers fronts in one, extenc ing to the bottom of the coat:</p>
        <p>Linen dresses are being greatly worn this season, and some of them are beautifully embroidered. The favourite colours are blue in navy and corn- flower shades, old rose pink, cream-colour, grey, and mime. The coat and skirt style is the usual me&amp; of making, but the coat is of the least obtru- sive sort, very different from the wide, loose shape that was worn three seasons since. The basque is now quite short. The fronts are generally cut away to show a dainty blouse, and sometimes there is not even a basque, the Eton shape or bolero heing preferred. A favourite way of Emishing off these linen coats 18 by adding a tucked or pleated muslin collar, or one in deep cream or stri• - coloured guipure.</p>
        <p>To transfer the patterned portion of a lace or embroidered collar to a new net or muslin ground has often to be done, as the old grounds are fre- quently quite worn away, and this is ntatraged by esiosiirefly tanking-the old collar on the new ground, sewing down the design to the latter with the aeritest arra smallest stitches (taking special care wit&amp; theaectgal, and then clipping the old ragg: ground away. I may here hint that if the edge of a worked musfin or net collar is fonnd to be very imsch frayed it is often an improvement to add a narrow frill of lace to it, taking care, of course' to choose a kind of lace that goes well with the watt on the cellar. If any portions of the paStern are worn away they must be worked in again on the new ground_ In-the case of a collar which in in too-frail a condition to bear transferring to a fresh froandation, or if the worker thinks her skid =equal to the task, it is beet simply to back the elld nat with a piece of sound, sewing the two torther very carefully with fine running stitches roved every part of the-pattern, All ragged bits of thoold nes most be-cfortionaly pulled ant fiat and sewn dawn vitt iniMble stitches. )nritrring 41, At Greenwich, James Bolton, thirty-eight, miner ii dealer, ot 21, Baildon-street, was committed for trial for the murder of his wife Kate, by striking her on the head With a hammer. In answer to the Bench be said, " 1 bays got nothing to say whatever." News was received at Hawarden on Wednesday morn tug of Mr Herbert Gladstone's engagement to Miss Paget, daughter of Sir James Paget, A telegram was sent from the townspeople of Hawarden crogratulating Mr Gladetene, and a peal of hells was rung at the parish church.</p>
        <p>The fact that life is sometimes infinitely sadder than death, is amply illustrated in the case of the Empress Frederick, e whose release from a long drawn-out agony, all wh-, loved her must rejoice. Her Majesty leaves two sons and four daughters, the Kaiser and Prince Henry of Prussia, the Princess of Saxe bleiningen, the Princess Adolphe of Schaumburg-Lippe, the Crown Princess of Greece, and the Prinaess Frederick of Hesse-Cassel. All were devoted to their illustrious mother, and that she should at length have entered into her longed for rest must, despite their great grief at losing her, be a matter of great thankfulness among them. Within a few short months, both our; King and the Kaiser have been called upon to take a long farewell of those very dear to them, but on each occasion the one has hastened to the other, supporting alli around, by sympathy and helpfulness, to the utmost of their power. Of all our King's sisters,the Empress Frederick was unquestionably the best beloved by him, and her long illness has been a great sorrow to His Majesty. Once again the Cowes week has brought bereavement to our Royal Family. Two years ago Queen Alexandra—then Princess of Wales—was sum- moned from the Solent to her mother's deathbed ; on July 30th last year, the Duke of Saxe-Coburg passed away ; and from Cowes again, it happened to be, that the King was summoned to Friedriehshof, where the shadow of death was deepening so swiftly and surely. And once again the country is in mourning—mourning for our Princess Royal. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The new appointments in the Royal Household are now all but complete, and though the changes have been numerous, many old members of the Court have been retained. No change has been made in the offices of the Lord Chamberlain, or the Lord Steward, but Lord Farquhar succeeds LordEd ward Pelham Clinton as master of the household.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Ellis now becomes Comptroller of Accounts in the Mace of Sir Spencer Ponsonby-Fane, who is in his seventy-eighth year. Viscount Bridport, whole nearly eighty-seven, also retires on account of age,and his place as Lord-in-Waiting is taken by Lord Sheffield. It was understood that the office of Governor and Constable of Windsor Castle, was to be abolished, but it is still re- tained by the Duke of Argyll. The Lord Chamberlain's department is principally concerned with the holding of Drawing Rooms, and is therefore more or less enjoying a holiday until next year. Some effort is to be made to curtail the number of presentations,and there is to be a more rigid scrutiny of the lists. As the Drawing Rooms are to be held in the evening in future, they will partake more of the nature of Royal parties, and will be more exclusive. I Every season, it A not unusual to see in the papers some such announcement as this "An American desires to find a lady of title, who will chaperone his daughter for three months, for an honorarium of £5,000." The I new rules for the holding of the Drawing Rooms, under which, not only will an invi`ation be given to the debutante, bat also to the lady entitled to present her, will however put a check upon these methods of ob. taming a preseotation at Court, Lady Londonderry, who was recently taken seriously ill, with a fainting fit, whilst riding in the park, is one of the leading women of Society, and is a most charm- ing conversationalist. She is the daughter of Lord Shrewsbury and married Lord Londonderry, then Lord Castlereagh, in 1875. Her leading position in the social world has been of great assistance to Lord Londonderry since he became a member of the Government, bu, despite her social duties, Lady Londonderry always finds time for outdoor amusements, of which she is exceedingly fond. Riding, cycling, boating. yachting, are among her favourite diversions, and in ordinary seasons, she is usually to be seen at the Cowes Regatti steering and sailing her own boat, with the no,st per- fect ease. . . .</p>
        <p>A fashion was started at a recent dinner, which, later may be considerably developed. The guest of the even. log was requested to sign his name upon the blank back of his menu card, and then to pass this on to the next diner, that he might do the same thing. In this way all the menus on the table circulated, and each guest rose with a complete set of autographs in Ws possession. On the occasion in question, an unusual number of dis- tinguished persons were present, and each knew the value of such a souvenir of the meeting of himself and colleagues. At an ordinary social dinner, the autograph menu would of course be merely interesting as a relic of a friendly evening. It was instructive to note the different ways in which the guests had signed them selves, and the traits which their writing exhibited even to unpractised graphologists In summer boots are little worn, save by enthusiastic walkers, but early autumn, Causes US to look dubiously at thin out-of-door shoes. Certainly a light well-made well-fitting boot, net too new, affords most comfortable support, and for rough wear is absolutely necessary. Visitors to Switzerland—and other rocky realms—are I apt to be much puzzled about this question of boots and shoes, SOMA recommending the thinnest, others the heaviest make,. As a matter of fact, thin boots and oboes for wear in the tewns do well enough, while for climbing, especially Made boots are essential to safety. Soft but strong leather, laced up if possible, and ctith the sole well projecting all round, even these do not afford sufficient foothold, and the village cobbler, or a guide, must be induced to put in nails increasing in size according to the prowess of the climber. When no longer necessary, they are easily taken out, and the boots are then fit for their erdinary winter work. When autumn comes the storage of summer sun- shadee, deemed worth keeping for another season, is rather a problem: Drawers, wardrobes and boxes sr. perchance full, but a good holder for them is a bolster. shaped case of white batiste or muslin, with a rounded end of cardboard covered. A pretty cretenne is made up to shape and drawn over the lining foundation, a second round card covered and with a deep frill falline from it, is partly sewn, and partly tied on with ribbons to form a lid. In this case, if made large enough, the daintiest parasols rest in peace, free from dust, and stand in a spare corner unreproached for untidiness. The warm snmrners with which we have lately been favoured, have caused a revolution in the sphere of waterproofs which formerly' were of heavy "shiny material" or cloth. The up-to-date cloak or coat is light and silky; it is gay and frivolous in colour, and in make is orzamental instead of disfiguring. These water- proofs are not costly and they serve also as dust cloaks, which hitherto were represented by separate garments. For most of us, a pretty and showerproof coat or cape is ample pretection, and the shot fawn, grey, and dove ground', with pearly shades blended with them, are as appropriate for dressy out-door functions, as the daily constitutional. - - -</p>
        <p>The apprehension which so many people feel in a thunderstorm is accountable for many superstitions about lightning. In some parts, there is a belief that the ringing of church bells, will avert all danger, and from time immemorial, it has been thought that similar ' immunity can be secured by the wearing of laurel and bay, or the growing of the houseleek on dwellings. There is more justification for the belief that some trees attract lightning more than others. If caught by a thunderstorm in the woods, one should certainly avoid the oak and fir trees, and seek the beech trees, which therm is reliablo evidence to show, are much less fre- quently struck by lightning. If caught out of doors, everyone is taught to seek the open country, if possible, and avoid the shelter of trees, but as a matter of fact, the open country is not so safe a place se. a position of about 25 to 30 feet from tall trees. Indoors we should avoid the chimney places, or being under a chandelier, and above all, that of stand- ing. or sitting in a draught, which would tend to carry the lightning in ore's direction' An unrigh• or" cottage " pianm is an awkward piece of furniture when stood out in the room. Curtains over it rruiTl the sound, and "draperies' have long been baniOied. A new idea is to fasten a light, rather shal- low cabinet, 6speoially made for the purpose over the piano tack, and to stand bric-a- brac on the shelves of it. It necessary a thin ail curtain or some Japanese paper stretched across the back of tb3 shelves conceal 4 all trace of the instrument behind them. If the cabinet be fixed am as just not to touch the piano, there is little fear of the instrument calling forth a jangling accom- paniment musing the ornaments placed thereon. " MADGE."</p>
        <p>LARGE STOCK -- MANDOLINES, BANJOES, GUITARS, VIOLINS, AC, Special Offer.—Guaranteed ITALIAN MADIDOLINE, MAIM'S name inside, Rose- wood. ornamented ; lb. 6d. Carriage paid. ARCHER AND SONS. ST GEORGES' CRESCENT, TOP ON LORD- THEM. EUPOOL. Established 1848.</p>
