        <head>EX MINER, FPID Y, AUGUST 9, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EX MINER, FPID Y, AUGUST 9, 1901</p>
        <p>would De ror a nne iyay use -er. 6ne s 'most ready to 'old 'er nose as it is." My mother's thin face flushed angrily, and I felt the blood rush to mine. Until Fan's taunt it would have a tneared unbearable to stay in such surroundings, crowding the occupants, who must already be cramped for breathing space. But her words raised in me a curious antagonism, a desire to contradict. "It is my mother's house," I said, stiffly. "If she says that she will have me I will stay," "There!" cried my mother. "Well, I do say it." "Can you really put up with me?" I asked, pretending not to hear Fan's rude laughter. . "Fan can come in here to this bed," my mother returned. "You'd have to bunk in the back room with Totsey." "I'll send the cab away," I said, drearily. Then, suddenly I remembered a new embarrassment. "My luggage!" I exclaimed. "What shall Ido with it ?' "Lor !" groaned my mother, ruefully eyeing the proportions of my boxes through the one small window of the bedroom-kitchen. "Oh, 'ave 'em in, do!" entreated Totsey, almo-t dancing in excitement at the prospect. "We'll manage some'ow. I'm jest dyin' to see 'er things." Half the street was ready to help the driver carry my belongings into the little house. The big trunks were taken to a tiny bare back room, furnished only with a narrow bed and shabby chest of drawers, the latter having a tin basin and a cracked yellow jug on its top. Small as the room was, the lack of furniture made accommodation for</p>
        <p>the boxes possible, and they were piled one on top of another against the wall. The cabman, evidently very curious, was paid, and sent away; And during the process of finding the right change I heard Totsey whispering to a slatternly woman from next door that her "sister had come 'ome to stop—quite a lydy, with no end of smart clothes, and 'ad been livin' with a pirress." Ten minutes later, no doubt, all Easel-street was in possession of this savoury morsel. Mother had been engaged in replenishing the firs for "tea" when I had arrived. Now Totsey was sent out with a few coppers, and shortly after returned with something brown wrapped in newspaper. This something resolved itself into four bloaters, a delicacy with which my nose and eyes for the first time made acquaintance, as I was informed that it had been provided in honour of my coming. While the bloaters frizzled pungently on the fire, and the tea brewed in a brown earthen pot with half a spout, I was invited by Totsey into the next room, which I was to share with her. Fan followed, with none too good a grace to remove . .</p>
        <p>, her belongings from two or three hooks on the wall, and the top drawers of the dilapidated chest. But her big black eyes sparkled as they roved over</p>
        <p>my boxes. - "I aye, you must open them after tea," she said, with a commanding air which was characteristic of her. "It'll be fun to see what you've got." "Wouldn't it be rather useless ? " I asked, tone- lessly. "I only brought so much luggage because I could not leave anything at ho—at Arrish Mell Court, and didn't know where to store the boxes. But, of course, I shall not need the things while l'm here, and they might as well stay packed as they are." ' "Well, you will be a pig if you don't let us 'ave a look in!" exclaimed Fan, her red cheeks growing redder. "But it's jest what I egspected." "Oh, if you really care, you shall see everything, of course!" I sighed. And so it was settled. We were to come back directly after tea for the entertainment. We sat down at the deal table, which had no cloth, and my mother hewed thick, repulsive rounds of bread off the strangest, most hump- backed loaf I had ever seen. The girls spread this with the grease from the newly-cooked bloaters, while I turned away, with a spasm of the throat. The tea was bitter as lye, and we drank it without milk; Fan and Totsey, with their elbows on the table, stirring in yellow sugar, which they took from a paper bag, with their own pewter spoons. I had eaten no lunch, and suddenly discovered that I was faint with hunger; but.the.bread.choked</p>
        <p>me, and though I grieved-to 'run the risk of hurting my mother's feelings, I could not touch the terrible bloater.</p>
        <p>Tea was a serious meal in Easel-street, and my three newly-acquired relatives laid in rations to last for several hours, asking me questions with their mouths full, about the doings of the aristocracy. I answered kindly, though as briefly as I could, but they hung upon my words with curious greediness, which I did not understand. Then, pushing back their well-polished plates at last, Fan and Totsey sprang up, reminding me of my promise. Then followed a strange scene, I knew not whether more comic or tragic. But my sense of humour swooned; and, sick at heart, I took from my boxes, exquisitely packed by Swift, my smart frocks, and underlinen of cambric or silk, .and</p>
        <p>lace, sweet with the scent of orris root and violets. -Each garment reminded me of something in the past. The girls snatched every article from my hands as it left the box, turning it about and exclaiming over it in almost incredulous admira- tion. " Why, " said Totsey, "I didn't 'spose any- body but the Queen 'd 'ave things like these!" Many treasures which I had loved for one reason or another I found myself bestowing upon these strange half-sisters of mine ; things utterly unsuit- able to them, yet delighting their hearts with a joy such as, perhaps. they had never known. After the first gift or two, Totsey capitulated entirely, smothering me with moist kisses, which I tried not to find repulsive. But Fan was still curiously on the defensive. She accepted what I gave with a stiff "thank you," but she eyed me as a house-cat eyes another suddenly imported to share its rug. I caught her watching me sometimes with a sullen, jealous stare ; and her face lighted Ivith a vivid gleam when she heard our mother appraising my possessions, exclaiming that when my six pounds were exhausted I should have something substantial to fall back upon. Never shall I forget my first night in Easel- street. Used to a big, deliciously springy bed, spread with lavender-scented sheets, blankets light and white as thistle-down, and a satin coverlet that matched the colouring of my room, the narrow, prison bunk which had to be shared with Totsey was almost unbearable in its discomfort and squalor. I had half fearfully expected a letter from Roger Cope in the morning, but there was nothing for me; and after a breakfast of bitter tea and bread (eaten early that Fan might hurry off to the factory) I went out to try my luck at several employment agencies. Atone or two I had to pay a small, booking fee in advance, and I was alarmed as I found my money (Swift's money!) dwindling. My require- ments and capabilities were written down, and towards noon I left the last establishment which I meant to visit that day, faint with hunger and weary for want of sleep. As I came out into the street and climbed adventurously to the outside of an omnibus which I had hailed, something made me suddenly turn my Lead over my shoulder. A man was looking up at lie with intent, narrowed eyes, and an expression eoth sarcastic and threatening. As he met my estonished glance, his face became a blank. He pretended to be hailing another omnibus close behind the one I had taken, but, though it was bard to believe that ,those dull, commonplace features were the same I had seen sparkling with inalice an instant ago, I could not forget the shock I had received.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER X. THE SPIDER IN THE GLASS.</p>
        <p>Within the next few days, I had occasion to notice some,hing strange in connection with the agencies to which I applied for a situation as companion or governess in a school or family. The manageress would be cordial at first, and, when she had heard the list of my accomplish- ments, hold out high hopes of speedily obtaining 4-ie a desirable situation. But the next time that I went to make inquiries, all would be changed. She would be stiff, reserved in manner, almost for- bidding, telling me that there was a very little chance of my finding anything of the sort I wanted. If this had been so only in one Case, I should have thought little of it; but, acting upon my mother's advice that it was well to have numerous "strings to one's bow," I had laid my claims before several agencies. And it certainly was remarkable, I thought, that in every instance it should be the lame.</p>
        <p>My half-sister Fan was now sixteen, and yet, as she informed me one day in a moment of con- Edence, she had no lover. But one evening she came home from the factory looking mysterious, with eyes that advertised a secret. The next night she did not appear till bedtime. We had been sitting up for her, and she swaggered in, ostenta- tiously shewinv, a ring on the third linger of her left hand. This was a present, she excitedly announced, from a young man, a "real swell," who had been trvin2 to make her acauaintance for</p>
        <p>some time, and who had taken ner to tne tneatre. He wanted to "know her family," and she was going to bring him home to the noon dinner next day, which would be Sunday. I did not wish to meet my future brother-in-law, and besides, I thought that the family gatherin2 would be happier without me, so I dined at a particularly early hour on a piece of bread and disappeared before Mr. Tom Stephens was expected. I would take a long walk, I thought, and not return until he was certain to have escorted the lady of his choice to some Sunday afternoon entertainment.</p>
        <p>Hardly had I reached the end of the street, turning into another which led towards Commercial- road, when I almost ran into the arms of the man who had stared after me one day as I left an employment agency. He gave me a queer look, and passed on, while I hurried away, fearing that he might follow. Presently, I glanced back, and was relieved to find that he was not in sight ; but sauntering after me, with his hands in the pockets of his Sundav coat, was a young man who lived in the "upper flat" of the house opposite my mother's in Easel-street.</p>
        <p>His name, I also knew from Tot sey, was Welcome, his business was a mystery, his money plentiful; and my own experience on the two Sundays I had spent in Peckham had proved h of a friendly disposition. In our street, when a young man wished to make the acquaintance of a young woman, he helped himself to it, so to speak ; no introduction was necessary. And Mr. Welcome—inaptly named —had twice attempted to force his claims upon my notice. Both times I had eluded him by hurrying home, and raising a shout of laughter from Totsey, who had observed the manoeuvres from the window. But to-day I could not bear to go back. I was vexed that I had glanced over my shoulder, lest the man should think I intended to encourage him, and I hastened on, as if I did not know of his existence.</p>
        <p>He could walk faster than I could, however, and in two or three minutes he was at my side. "Look 'ere," said he, breathing fast, and suggesting lately imbibed spirit, "you needn't be in such a dashed 'urry." "I don't know you, sir," I said, with diznity. "Be good enough--" "Then it's your own fault," Mr. We-mot:, 6) in with a cackle. "I want to know you bad enough," he went on, keeping pace with me. "And I'm in earnest this time. Anybody in the street can tell you you've made a dead mash of me. In two weeks you've done what your sister's bin trying to do for twelve months. Sye, don't look as if I was dirt under your feet, y'know 1 I mean business. Honest, I do!"</p>
        <p>"You can have no business with me," I said, icily. "Please leave me. If you don't you will force me to go home." "I wouldn't stand much of that kind o' rottin' from any other gell I know," returned my com- panion. "But you're different. You can chaff me all you like. It makes me see you're worth 'avin'. I sye, it's just the dye for Richmond. Was you ever there? Wouldn't you like t' go ?"</p>
        <p>I turned round abruptly, and began walking back town rds Easel-street. "I would not like to go to Ric',mond, thank you," I said. "Perhaps you mean well ; but if you wish me to think so, you will leave me at once."</p>
        <p>"That ain't my idea of making a gell think well o' me—to leave 'er alone," chuckled Mr. Welcome. "And what's more, things 'avP gone too far with me fur that, I can tell you. W'ere you go, I go—and that sort o' thing. It's Scripture, ain't it ?" and he laughed again.</p>
        <p>I thought that he would leave me on the threshold; but, availing himself, no doubt, of his acquaintance with my family, he strode into the passage, knocking loudly on the door of the front room before I could disappear into my own. The knock was instantly answered, the door thrown open, and the face of the man I had passed on leaving the house appeared in the aperture. It seemed an odd coincidence, but the man viho had stared at me with such a meaning, sinister gaze, and the man who posed as Fan's lover were one and the same.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tom Stephens and Mr. Welcome looked hard at each other, but I did not wait to see or hear what should follow. After the first moment of indecision, I darted into the room which I shared with Totsey, and locked the door. Somehow—l could scarcely have defined why— after this Sunday, things began to be different with me, and even less pleasant, if possible, than before. Though Fan no longer coveted the eligible Mr. Welcome for herself, she resented the fact that he chose to advertise himself as a conquest of mine. She was sarcastic, often insulting, taunting me because, with all my boasted accomplishments and my fine lady airs, I could find no work, while she was "earning good money." Then, worse than all, Mr. Stephens, who now came to the house every evening to take Fan for a walk, or to some entertainment, expressed a desire for me to turn the duet into a trio.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile, poor Swift's money was fast dis- appearing. I paid my mother twelve shillings each week for board and lodging, which sum Fan sharply hinted was insufficient, as my wants Were so much more extravagant than those of the others. Pennies for omnibuses and trams soon mounted into shillings; I was considered by the family to be criminally reckless in the matter of washing bills, and my purchase of a cheap bath tub was almost the reason of a quarrel. Several times Fan and Totsey asked for a trifling loan, and I could not bear to refuse, but at last came a day when, with work as far off as ever, I found myself penniless. My heart sank at the thought of parting with the simple jewellery given me in happier days, each ornament a memory; but there was no other way. I could not be a burden on' my relatives or on society at large. So I went to the box where my most valuable possessions were stored. I had not unlocked it since the night of my wretched home-coming, when, after the family inspection, I had returned my possessions to their place; but I remembered well in which tray the jewel-case had been laid. I lifted the pretty dresses, still folded with sachets of orris-root and violets, but—the case was not to be seen. With my heart beating quickly, I took out the tray, and searched through another and another, then going over all again for the second time. The jewel-case was gone! Still I would not believe in my loss. It was impossible that anyone should have stolen it, I told myself. I must, after all, have put it away in one of the other boxes. Trembling, my hands growing cold, I ransacked each trunk, but in the end I had to acknowledge defeat. I had been robbed; and I dared not let my mind dwell on the hateful solution of the mystery which would come • up before it. Fan had indulged in a great deal of new finery of late, and, when questioned by her mother, had flushed, saying it was nobody's business how she had saved up the money. She had, and that was enough. It was a pity if a girl who worked as hard as she did shouldn't have something a little decent, especially now that she had a "young man" of her own. She had bought two new dresses and as many hats, to say nothing of a huge feather boa like a variegated snake ; and she had even a little gaudy jewellery which might have been bestowed by Mr. Stephens or might not. Sometimes I had left my keys where Fan might have taken them for her own purposes. I could not help remembering this, but I tried not to think of it, and I would not for all the world have accused her. I spoke hesitatingly to my mother of what had happened, suggesting no explanation, but she, poor woman, always nervous, worn, and weary, flew into a passion, demanding to know at once whether I isuspected her or one of my sisters as the thief. Afterwards, she was sorry for her anger, and for some harsh words she had uttered in haste, even offering to call in the police, though it would be sure to give great offence "in the street." But I refused. I was certain that I should never again see my jewels unless a thing should happen which I would give more than their value to prevent. The only hope I had left lay in selling or pawning some of my pretty clothes and books, for —I presently discovered—all the silver toilet things not in use had vanished with the jewel-case. That evening my mother and I went out with large parcels under our arms. I was hot, and tingling with self-consciousness, feeling as if everyone we met must know that we were going to the pawnbroker's. It was easy enough to say to one's self: "What does it matter? Who thinks anything about you ? or, if anyone should, of what importance is that in your life ? " But it did matter ; and it.was of importance just the same. The pawnbroker would not have my things. If they had been men's clothing it would have been different, or if I had any old jewellery or silver, be would take that. But he bad no call for ladies' dresses, and could give so little advance that it would not be worth while to part with my possessions. I went home and cried bitterly, with my face turned to the wall in the little lard bed when Totaey and I lay (I can hardly say slept) together.</p>
        <p>Best morning, tor tne tirst time since my art Five in Easel-street, there was a letter for me. Fan/had gone to the factory when it came, but my m6ther and Totsey, who was not yet strong enough to gs back to work, watched me excitedly as I broke the envelope. What they expected, I did not knots Perhaps they could scarcely have told themselteti but the envelope was of thick, creamy paper, sealr, with a crest, and it must haTe been evident them that my correspondent was not of their world' but of that remote and dazzling one which I hsd I knew the instant my eyes fell upon the cleat:, precise handwriting, with the affected Greek "e 's, that the letter was from Roger ; and 1 felt rs 9 colour rise as I broke it open. Often I had thought it strange, since he 120 professed such a passionate regard for me, that I,e. had never come or written. But, as 1 read on, began to fancy I understood the reason. For the letter renewed the old offer.</p>
        <p>He had waited, it said, controlling his grest, longing for a sight of me or a word from me, until I should have plenty of time to be sure whether or not I could make up my mind to reconsider 1111 decision. Every moment he had thought of 11le' Nothing that was now his was of the slightest vall to him, since I was not there to share it. Wash' I not give him something to live for by scrawling one word ; that word only of four letters, "C-o-m-e " ? In a few hours after he would be with me. I need commit myself to nothing; need make no decision. He would come if I wool(' let him, and we would talk together. What a tslt it would be I And then, if I were tired of the nei life I had chosen for the sake of avoiding the on° who loved me best, I had but to put my hand hie. He would be my servant until I was ready call him "lover." But we would be married, od we would see many lands together, if I wished. I-1° a week it might all be arranged, and I could choo°, where I would travel. Should it be Switzerlanol the Engadine, or the Black Forest? Or should Ir° dream away the dayston a yacht? He would hard', exist until my answer; but I must at least give bl° credit for patience in the past. I gave him credit for policy; and I thought gust I could read his thoughts between the lines, almost, as if I had seen a skeleton through flesh by aid 0 the Röntgen rays. He had waited until he imagined that I must be sick to death of my intolerable environment. Pea years,he had kept my mother always in sight, an had finally given me her address with exactly till contingency in his mind. Everything had worel; out according to his plans, except that the bribe ° food he offered to me when I was starving was not' after all, tempting enough. He was very cleverf, but he was not sufficiently clever to understand tluo all the money and all the luxuries and pleasure° which might be mine through him would lose their value because they could not be separated from hilt It would be like offering to a thirsty man a glasi; of exquisite champagne with .the black, crumple'', body of a great spider at the bottom. I could se. conceive being thirsty enough to drink that chaW pogne ; and my heart was bitter against Roger. t I would have liked not to answer the letter; fearing lest, in that case, he might come to Ease,' street, I sent a few words written on the con:110T blue-lined paper with which I wrote almost dai,T, offering my services to various advertisers in tu" newspapers. 11 When the envelope was addressed to Arrish Court, where it seemed that Roger was living novri I remembered that I had no more stamps, and rltire a penny to buy one. I must go out and try "va sell the things refused by the pawnbroker, to second-hand clothes dealer. I had expected to obtain at least twenty pouncli for what I had taken, for I knew that the thint,gos must originally have cost fully three times t' sum, and they had been scarcely worn before went into mourning. But I escaped at last, brovr: beaten, tongue-tied, and thankful to have wrestes four pounds from the second-hand clothes dealer' Her husband had been called in to her assistance'd and I was assured by both that I had been cheste, by my dressmakers, that fashions had suddepa, changed, the season was almost over, and it was favour to take my things at all. Still, I had something to chew for my journeil I was at least able to post my letter, and to back in a tram which would take me three-qusrtle of the way to Easel-street. I rested after _, battle, and shut my eyes, only to open again with a start when the conductor camos demand the fare. I slipped my hand int t rbit pocket for the purse lately replenished. 1311 gra, purse was gone.</p>
        <p>(To (le continued.)</p>
        <p>- TEMPERANCE EXPERIMENT AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A Refreshment Bar at Bewsey Work - The Vicar of St. Paul's Church, Warrington, the following interesting announcement in the Si. Parish Magazine for this month "One more organiz.tion is to b. added to the long list in cone( ction with our church. By the ness and hearty co-eperation of the directors of Pearson and K ,owles Company, Limited, a refres room for the employees is to be built within the Works. The room will be built by the firm, andf then be furnished by the Vicar from the proceeds Magazine Fund. It has long been in the mind o'.l Vicar to do something of this sort for the men and v at the forge, whose work is at all times of the year tot extremely trying one. But " hot drinks "in W and "iced " drinks in summer, with the best of Fos food, may do something to prove that these workens9V sympathiz-ci with at St. Paul's. There is to be ronizing, and the • St. Paul's Refreshment Bar ' tiff° conducted on sound business principles. It is a ofttll a serious undertaking. Let it be understood froffte first that all profits after paying off capital tiaileil%, will be spent on extending the premises or estabwpir new bars in other portions of the works. Tile P are being prep-red by our good friend of" John Bate, and we hope to be able to eel; for business about the middle of Svter° In the meantime, applications for the lAsts/(4, barman may be sent in to the Vicar., The woe will be to commence with fifteen shillings a strPe'neirt4 meals a day, and 25 per cent of net profit. The p.oli will ba 7 a.m. to? p.m.. Saturdays 7 a.m. to 20°; No Sunday work. The duties will include the so tt°, food and drinks, the cookine of one sort of Pia ~sio", dinner each day (gas stove provided), the prePaef of first-class tea and coffee, lemonade, &amp;c., aefrp. keeping of the room and its furniture, utensils, 'liter clean as elbow grease can make them. Either tbeit joi; or one of the committee will take stock, &amp;C.. ete„6,llei The firm have promised not to charge rent en'',lloo the use of the room or land, and not to evict tis competitors on the ground for throe yearS.kiedl grateful to Mr Bunch and also to Mr Burgess f?r,tD prectieal assistance, and to the directors itlVc‘i encouragement. We may say that smoking ifflpOio allowed, and the only duty the Vicar wishes t° r'rite r on the men and lads i, that they Eti,.ll co-oFefor Ceo keeping the place as clean a, d comfortable raftii tomers and workers as possible, but of course tuotodio is not to he considered a " place" for gamblinfiThe any one like to furnish the room either part ? The furniture will include crockery. nirreot;gpj board, large gas stove, sink, dominoes stld vo spittoons, pictures, gas fittin a, gas jets for .000 coffee, urns, icechest, &amp;c., &amp;c. We shall also veoioP least one daily paper delivered m,•rniog and e „1, 10'3 t so TRUSS.—New washable waterproof Is the Illostiols, for tropical climates ; it le light, clean, cool, CO durable ; can be worn whilst bathing. WHIT/1 Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw-strO, ' tap of Bold-street, Liverpool, Advt.</p>
