        <p>Home.</p>
        <p>'HE RETURN OF THE 3rd SOUTH LANCASHIRE MILITIA REGIMENT. Warrington's Welcome</p>
        <p>fk hearty reception awaited the 3rd South Lapcashile (unilitia) Regiment on arriving at Warrington on Satur- ..eY morning from the seat of war, after seventeen , 'll9nths' active service It wa• fitting that Warrington I , toil v.,'" which the Battalion has long been connected,should its appreciation of the patriotism of the militia- •,,en , •,,,,. • As in the days of the Crimean War, PO, again, 1 silien the war in South Africa ',quires that others be I e.es the regular army should come forward for the . t;zetice of the Empire, the 3rd South Lancashire Regi- rpt was One of the first of the Militia Battalions to ?ter its services. The battalion was etc bodied at it.gshoo on 13th December, 1899, but only 400 of ; tumber were called up. Few days, however, were , 84°Wed to pass before the battalion volunteered for ' ac:"°e. and before the end of the year its offer was ;',.'Ll'ed, and orders received for the remainder of the pa-Lslion to be called up, with a view to an early de- for South Africa. The time was short but by tOet of hard work all necessary preparations were made, let whole battalion was assembled at Preston, and on ty bil lalluary, 1900, it embarked on the transport City ~.'‘oote, at Liverprol, for South Africa. %Stine to their departure, his Worship the Mayor of 14:hington (Alderman Roberts) was requested by th'u,teaant-Colonel Blackburne, the officer commanding 41:battalion, to take charge of the colours during the is„etee of the men. The regiment itself cannot now be 4,%k01ed as purely a local one, hut it was formerly the Itical Lancashire Militia, one of the oldest institu- q: of Warrington. For many years the men were tsie`ed in the town and the head quarters and perma- the "aff were in the Old Barracks at Bank gJaV,WhiiSt ihe rteete mess was in Bold-street Colonel Blackburn., ts °Meer commanding, is the son of the late Colonel 44',Ptburne who was for years in charge of the battalion 4,' one of the officers is also identified with Warrington ' ni rt.telY. Lieotenant Lyon, who is a nephew of Mr Lyon ,i Appleton Rail. On Saturday, the Mayor took occa- i ._oon to return the colours to the Regiment .nil Ms c-re- u3 ov added not a little to the interest f the official welcome home. 17 was expected that the men would arrive from Bouthampton /it 'seven o'clock, and at that ear' heur , the Mayor, Alderman Roberts, members of the Cor- , 'potation, military officers, and prominent townspeople I..s.sernbled at the Towi. Flan, waiting to receive Colonel Tackburne and his khaki clad men from South Africa. detachnaent of the Ist Volunteer Battalion, under the 'tetproand of Captain Appleton, paraded in review 4.,'.., an formed a guard of honour. The other efficers .duty were Lieutenants Avery and Roberts. The re- tale "bed militiamen travelled in two trains, and the first arrived at W ireifigtell Central Station in good hut unfortunately the second train VMS consider- huiY delayed, and caused a long and weary wait of two en°,llrs• At the Central:Station, there was a large of spectators near the sidings and in Win wick- many many of those present being wives, relatives and ::4s of the men who had journeyed from Liverpoo/, ldelens, Widnes, and other places. Wherever it was ,rsible there were hearty hand-shakings and enthu- illutic welcomes. The men who had journeyed in the „e.st train were drawn up alonz the carriage drive of " station, and here they ha u to wait until nearly a 11,111'4bn. to nine. To while away the time the Barracks Volunteer Bands played a lively selection of music. Z.l7l3.°°est those who were early in attendance were oinnel Fitzherbert, commanding officer of the district slid other townspeople. 1, The crowd outside the station greatly increased as ,ttle wore on, and when the secnnd train steamed in, ‘'lle attendance was almost as large as that which Isssrobleel on the arrival of the Warrington Volunteers 'eoln the front. The streets through which the men were to pass were 'thickly lined with spectators. and when at length the Itilitiamen, numbering over 600, appeared, headed by their bugle band, they were greeted with rounds of theering. The procession proceeded to the Town Rail, Ind took op a position on the lawn in front of the hall. Ihe procession to the Town Ball was headed by the L'olunteer and Barracks Bands, and the band of the 3rd uattalion, and the reception of the returned soldiers tetelved along the route was of the heartiest possible "%meter. The Volunteer Band struck up the now isscillar air" Soldiers of the Queen " in a very spirited lill4,llner, and when they ceased to play on their arrival ~ntinkey-street the Barracks Band supplied the music ue Mayor, accompanied by the Mayoress. Colonel I,mlwall Legit and Mrs Legh, of nigh Legh, Mrs Black- V,,„h Ps and others occupied an improvised platform. The , a.:`hriteer officers present were Colonel Ridgway (corn- Ceding the lat V.B Prince of Wales Volunteer (South ''W,scashire Regiment), Major Buckton (acting adjutant absence of Major RN-me), Captains Edelsten and • I,hDleton and Lieutenants Robetts, Avery and Broad- Excellent order was kept in the streets by the 11)1,1,2e, under the direction of Chief Inspector Phillips. tagraphe of the Ceremony at the Town Hall were Tea b Mr hirtles. ,tlia,,tx formally welcoming the men home, the MAYOR , :—Colonel Ireland Blacithorne, officers, non-com- -I„'"2loned officers and men of the 3rd Battalion South , el'hash i ire Regiment, it is with feelings of nten:e 1 -,'essure that I join with my fellow towns: enple in 4-bettne you a most cordial welcome hatne. We shall al "fle: forget the patriotism which prompted each and to offer yourselves for active service when the in South Africa brke out. Neither will it be for- si);" that this is not the first time that the battalion ;tips° distinguished itself, veing tbat your predecessors animated with the same spirit. when they ~ Th„,"selY volunteered in the days of the Crimean War. Vh;totinection between this battalion and our town is is ~ ',' elnfle one, not only because of the personality.of its a°Anding officer. whose name is a household word 110:nttst us, and whose family have for so long and en lat'eurably been associated with us, but also because is a ge Proportion of your numbers ere Warrington men, . be 'se have, therefore, from the date of your departure th,ahlsary last rear, until your return to•day, never viiitlerd our interest in your movements, nor ceased to atoe your StlcCeSs and for your speedy return. The bai„,,u4B been prolonged fa, beyond what was antici- ')l t'is". and this has dr mauled from you a continuance . stly"at spirit of dogged de'ermination not to be beaten i, lth is not only one of our national characteristics.hut Far, Pecially developed in Lancashire men, and which taiienahlefi you to face hardships and dangers with a ti,,,iirrls cheerfutnefs which has called forth the admire- ', pi of all observers. The splendid reputation of the sod Rhhine Fortieth "has been Well maintared by you tti., (7oen the history of this war comes to he written Drjdeede of the South Lancashires will be read with teat," and gratitude. We are deeply that- kful that so end4;.,(4 You have been brought in rafety home !gain. the •••-•:e P"' our tribute 4 affectionate admiration to ~.• •usin I ()Ty of those o': your gallant comrades who have N A teld" "D their lives in the service of the Empire. liolthj,our long and tedious journey it would be most "" °f me to keep you waiting longer, but I cannot</p>
        <p>conclude without expressing an earnest hope that these of yin who desire CO return to your civilian duties may do so as speedily as possible, as I feel cure your old employers will be glad to see you once more filling your accustomed places. And, now,Colonel rilackburne.maY I with great pleasure, return to you the cJours nf the battalion, which were entrusted to me when ) ou lett Us for the Front ? I shall never farget the houour yin then cooferred upon me, and I am proud to hand the colours back again to you to-day, becatHe I know that they will be carried at the head ot a battalion whose officers and men have alike helped to increase the splendid reputa- tion of the regiment to which you belong. (applause.) The National Anthem was then placed by the Volun- teer Band, which was under the conductorship of Band- master Richardson. _</p>
        <p>Colonel BLACHBURNE replied as follows:—Mr Mayor,— On behalf of the 3rd Snail Lancashire Regiment, which I have the honour to command, allow me sincerely to thank you tor the very hearty reception you have given us to-day, and at the same time let me, tt rough you, thank sit the inhabitants of the ancient borough of Warrington for turning out as they have done this morning at such an unearthly hour, I am afraid, to bid ' U s welcome. I thank you on behalf of the regiment for t.iking care of the colours while we were on duty in South Africa. I dare say you don't quite know what the battalion did while it was away. During the last sixteen or seventeen months we have been guarding the finest from Edenberg in the North to Norvals Pont and Bethune in the South, and have been in charge of 30 different !outposts with headquarters at Springfon- tein. I think the regiment has done its duty, at any rate lam sure it has tried to do so. There are some,as the Mayor nas very kindly remarked, who have been left behind. We have had two gallant officers killed in action and six or seven men killed and wounded, and in addition to that disease has worked havr.c amongst us. No fewer than 35 have been left b.,hind, and in addition we have had 157 men invalided home, so that the regi- ment has borne the brunt and hardships of the war. I can tell you, Mr Mayor, that no commanding officer, could have commanded a regiment of men, who were so willing, notwithstanding all the hardships and hard work from kopjo to guard and guard to kopje day after day and night after night, without grumbling. I beg to thank vou extremely, Mr Mayor, on behalf of the regiment, for the kind reception you have given us to- day. (Applause.) At the request of the Colonel three hearty cheers were given by the battalion "for the Mayor. Corporation and inhabitants," and "one cheer more," the men, taking off their helmets while the 3 did so. It was a hearty ringing cheer and the sight will be long remembered by these who witnessed it. Orders were then given to the men to pile arms on the lawn, after which they marched to the Drill Hall for breakfrst to the strains of suitable music provided by the Volunteer Band. The tables were laid down the whole length of the hall, and contained% substantial fare of meat pies, ham and beef sandwiches, buns, scones, coffee, &amp;o. The wants of the men were attended to by a email army of workers, composed of men from all departments at the Town Hall, Bank House and by the gardeners from the Park. The whole of the arrangements were under the personal supervision of Mr Powys, caretaker at the Town Hall, whose catering was inert creditable to him. During the breakfast Colonel Blackburne and other Facers as well as many well known townspeople, were present and watched the novel scene with evideat pleasure. The number who arrived at Warrington was eleven officers and 654 men, one of the company being left at Queenstown suffering from scarlet lever, and another at Netley with a fractured arm. At the conclusion of the repast the men left for Orford Barracks.</p>
        <p>..........-0- BOWLING. i -- CEMETERY BOWLING CLUB'. LIBERAL CLUB. These teams met on the green attached to the Ceme- tery Hotel, Manchester-road, on Saturday, and the ga meg resulted as follows . CEMETERY CLUB. LIBERAL CLUB. B. D. B. D. G Ball. captain ll 112 T Locker captain ... 7 I n 3 Harrison 7J " 0 H Hibbert . , ll f " T Bolton 9 8 T Billion ...„. ............ ... 11 j'' J Jennings— 4 1 H Williams — 11 , R Fogg J Boardman ll f ' F Rigby R Young W Haigh B Butterworth 1 5 J Ashton 3 Wilson W Barton 11 A J Jones ll 1,2 B A bander. 1 4 T Forster W Wood ll Lt., J Middleton , 0 1 T Bridge J. Willett }13 W Patrick W Hughes Vir Butterworth ... ...... -. IDi• 1 F Baker ---' 11 J Savage 5 113 F Tongue 7 Tata! 213 112 Total-..... ..... 113 67 Majority last match at i iberal Club Majority for Cemetery 145 Cemetery on both nu majority BO Newton Division Challenge Cup Competition</p>
