      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>111 E EXAMINER, FRIOAY, AUGUST 9 1901</head>
        <p>111 E EXAMINER, FRIOAY, AUGUST 9 1901</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>IT STATUS.—LittIe Elmer: "Papa, what Is kleptomania?" Professor Broadhead: "The most lucrative form of insanity, my son." Miss Passay : I dread to think of my ortieth birthday." Miss Pert: " Why ? Did something unpleasant happen then?" THE BEST HE COULD Do. —Johnny : "What is IL contralto, papa?" Papa: " Utn—l can't defiae a contralto, Johnny, except as the mortal enemy of the soprano." AFTER THE MASS MEETING.—" Colonel Shouter had an impediment in his speech." "Is that so?" "Yes. It was interrupted by eggs and potatoes" A FLASH OF SILENCE.—He: " Why so pensive?' She: ".1?" He: "Yes. You haven't said a word for 40 seconds."</p>
        <p>AT THE GAS OFFICE—First Official: "Bray nes is working on an iuvention to enable us to sell gas at 25 cents a thousand feet without reducing dividends." Second Official: "H'in I A new meter?"</p>
        <p>TnomsoN : "Is your daughter improving in her piano. playing?" French: " Well, she's either improving or else we're getting used to it. I don't know which."</p>
        <p>Aunt Mudacres (seeing a lady in bathing cos- tume it la Francais for the first time): "So that there's one o' them bloomer' hussies, is it ? I wonder what she's a-doin' in the water without her bishickle."</p>
        <p>A Norm:E.—The Caller (soarply): "Just ask Mrs. Jones i she'll see me." The Maid : " Well, "I see if she'll see you; but I hope, for your own sake, it ain't for subscriptions or collections or such !"</p>
        <p>WICKWIRE : "Say, I have been carrying these I 0 U's of yours until they are almost worn out." Mudge: "Don't t hey make a nizserable quality of paper ni.iwadays ?" IR. URBAN : " How do V 311 like your new city house?" Mr. RusLie : "Oh l ever so much. The people are so sociab/e. Wily, we've only been here a week, and the butcher, the giocer, and the coal dealer have all left cards."</p>
        <p>TEACHER (to class): "In this stanza, what is meant by the line, The shades of night were falling fast ?'" Bright Scholar: "The people were pulling down the blinds." FLASHER ! "I saw you out horseback riding this morning, old man.", Dumbleton : "Don't Pay I Well, was lin it, so to speak ?' Flasher: "The saddle ? „Weil, at very rare int ervals." "IT is claimed that thieves are almost un- known in Norway." They must have the same police system there that we have in this country." OLD FRIEND: "Your bride looks somewhat familiar to me. Does she remind you of any- body?" Muchwed "Oh yes; her first hus- band."</p>
        <p>BIEGS : "Where is your family going to spend the holidays?" Boggs: "I don't know yet. My wife hasn't found out which place is most expensive." WHEN GREEK MEETS GREEK.—" What makes you so anx.uus to introduce Higby and Digby ? "Higby tells war stories, and Dighy tells tells fish stories.</p>
        <p>- COUNT (to his son, who is engaged to an actress): "I'm sorry you are going to bring an actress into our family." Son: "Oh, that's all right. She isn't much of an actress."</p>
        <p>PENSMITH (a callow bard): "My poem is re- turned with the editor's thanks. Huh! I don't see what he has to thank me for?" Grimshaw : "For what you d'dn't send him, probably."</p>
        <p>- MRS. JOHNSON: "So yo' fink ole Mrs. Jackson's husband and five able-bodied sons hab enlisted and gone to de war? What makes yo' fink dist?" Mrs Raleigh: " Well, she's stopped takin' in washin' l"</p>
        <p>..—... . THE FIRST MATE: "How clear and bright it h in the west I" The Second Mate: "Why notl The captain has been sweeping the horizon with his glass."</p>
        <p>RYMAN : "I hove several fine poems at home that I wrote myself." Shopman : Why don't you send them to some of the newspapers?" Hyman: "I did." DoesoN : "Did you know your wife's first hus- band?" Hobson (with a sigh) : "Yes, but he never put me on to anything about his domestic affairs, confound him. THE other day we saw a fellow fishing. Upon inquiring if he had caught any, we received the following reply: "Caught any? No, you fool, I've not been at it only about four hours yet." PUNISHED.—" Melville says he thinks platonio friendship is the thing, and that he will never marry." "I used to know a fellow who said that, too." "Where is he now?" "Upstairs playing horse with baby." SMITH: "Those two Thompson girls are twins, area" they?" Brown: "Yes." Smith: "Well, theme something about them I can't make out." Brown: "What's that?" Smith: "The one that was married last week owns to being five year. older than her sister."</p>
        <p>IRRESISTIELE.—" And so that's George Ridge. leg's wife? I'm surprised. I shouldn't think such a woman as she would have any attractions for him." "That's because you don't know it all. She has a hundred thouaand big, round attractions for him, and all well invested." OPENING THE CONVERSATION.—YOting Mr. Bash. by and Miss Spritely were at dinner together. Two courses had been finished, and still the bash. I fill youth bid not said a word, Suddenly the young lady spoke. "I'll tell you what let's do," , she said; let's talk." The young man's corn- Flexion uttered several bright things in reply, but he spoke not a word. She was a pretty young thing fresh from a fashionable "finishing school." She was bidding the " happitst man on earth " a fond adieu in the porch of the old country house. "Now, dearest, just before I go," he said, commencing all over again, " what kind of a ring shall it be?" "Oh, Jack !" she arswered, rapturously, "do get me a solitaire with elle diamond in it." JOINED TuGhTHER.— Teacher : "Thomas, will you tell me wnat a conjunction is, and compose • sentence containing a conjunction?" Thomas (after long and solemn reflection): "A conjunction is a word connecting anything, such as, The horse is hitched to the fence by his halter.' Halter is a conjunction, because it connects the horse and the fence !" HER HoME-MAD3 BREAD. Young Man: "Doctor, you nave been a; ',ending me for a week. and I am worse than I was at the start." pay,aeian " I ,sill be frank with you, sir. Being tumble to discover what was the matter with you, and beine unwilling to risk interfering with the cure rive power of nature. I have given you no niedieine at all. In fact, my treatment has not commenced yet." "But you have given me_pills right along." " They were only a sham. They were made of bread.' "Where did you get the bread?" "Your young and charming wife made it." "No weeder I'm worse." A Sean FL NOT A t:•iiovs,L. —Sir Frank Lockwood used to tell a story vvolen happened in his own ex- perience, as showing the dulness of certain men who are on the magisterial bench. A man had stolen a spade, and was tried before a well-meaning and conscientious magistrate. His worship care- fully looked up Areaibald's "Criminal Law," to find a precedent n 'which he could convict and pewee the man. "I can't find anything under toe word spade,'" he said at length, "although I see that a man \vas convicted and severely pun- iehed for etealing a shovel." Then, looking at the culprit severely over his spectacles, he added, " You have had a very narrow escape, but you may go I SZEING ANI; KNOWiNG.—An eminent judge' who Was trying a ri 1,11 -A- way ease, had before him a witness—an old tanner—who was telling the jury• that he had " knewed the path for 60 yeer, and my reyther tould I as he heerd my grandfeyther say----' "Stop !'' said the judge ; "we can't have any hearsay evidence here." "Not," ex- claimed Farmer Giles. "Then how dust knew who thy feyther was 'cept by hearsay." Judge : In courts of law we can only be guided with what you have seen with your own eyes, and nothing more orless. " Oh, that be blowed fora tale," re- plied the farmer. " I ha' a bile on the back of my neck, and I never seed on, but I be prepared to swear that he's there, I do 1" This second triumph on the part of the witness let in a torrent of hear- say evidence about the footpath which obtained weight with the jury, albeit the judge told them it was not testimony of any value, and the farmer's party won.</p>
        <p>Temporary Authority. At the Warrington Police Court, on Sathrdny, Thomas B. Malkin was granted temporary authority to sell at the " Royal Exchange " Inn, in Len of James Rowlinson Bolton.</p>
        <p>W. T. WILLIAMS' SALE is now proceeding. See Windows for Bargains in 3dILLINERY, NIA.NTL S. COATS, DRESSES, BLOUSES &amp;C., &amp;C.—Sankey-street and Legh-atreet, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WARRINGTON FAMILY DISPUTE SETTLED.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FAMILY DISPUTE SETTLED.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Assizes on Monday betore Mr Justice Ridley, a biagular family :aw dispute came into court, when L.,uchlan. Miller, of 27, Wilson-Put en-street, Warrington, was the plamtiff, and the defendant, James Stobie, of Parr Hous Farna,Ashton-in-Maker The action was brought to recover £305 alleged to be due for wages and work done. The deleuce was a denial of the claim. Mr Horridge, K C., and Mr Rigby Swift (instructed by M s-r B!OWZIP and Crcrnpton, Warring- ton) app ared for the pl.intiff, and Mr Pickforo, K C.. and Mr Greer (instructed by Mr Jackson, of Wigan) were for the defendant.</p>
        <p>Mr Horridge xplained that the plaintiff, Lauchlan Miller, as a stepson of he defebelant, Mr Stowe. 'roe plaintiff's own father diea when he was three years ot age, and his mother married the defendant shortly afterwards. The plaintiff's mother owned a farm near Warrington, and shortly alter the second mat riage the name of the defendant pre are.d n even- thing con- nected with the farm. Le 1883 the plaintiff, who was then sixteen years of age, returned from school and was put to work on the farm. There was no real agreement as to the amount of wages the defendant and his wife would pay plaintiff, bat they made it perfectly clear to him that they would remunerate him in one way or other for his services. About thiee years ago plaintiff intended to go to Afllf tied with his brother,but his mother, in the prest nce of the defendant, persuaded them to stay behind, ea} leg that they would have a division of tbe farm with the rest ot them. Counsel sub.. mitted that from that time the plaintiff was not work- ing for nothing. In February of tbie year his mother died, and the stepfather said to the plaintiff, •• You stop on with me, Don't leave me, and you will have half ot the whole thing." It was only in May of this year that plaintiff, finding he could not get aria thing out of his stepfather, left him. He now claimed (or pound a week for the past six years, which counsel con- ended ,was a reasonable pay for a man in the plaintiff's position. Plaintiff in his evidence, said ne had had charge of part of the farm, and had done most of the boring and selling. Be said his mother bac made no will, and the farm and stock came into Stobie's possession, He had been offered 14s a week by the defendant, but he bad refused to accept this RM. In cross-examination, plaintiff admitted that he had been living with the de- fendant. and had been kept by him and his wife. The defendant gave evidence, and denied having beard any promise made by his wife to the plaintiff. After his wile's death he promised plaintiff 143 a week if he stayed on the farm, but plaintiff did not stay. Witness said it was the custom amongst farmers not to pay their sons anything in wages, but to feed sod keep them.</p>
        <p>Tha jury returned a verdict for th plaintiff for £125.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning, Mr Horridre app fed for jude- me; t to be entered for the plaintiff for the amount found by the jury. His lordship thereupon entered judgment. Mr Pickford. for the defendant, applieo for a stay of execution as it was the intention of the defendant to appeal against the decision of the jury. Mr Horridge consented if the usual terms were com- plied witb, to which Mr Pickford at first objected hut subfrequently his lordship decided that he would not grant stay unless the sum of £lOO was p.id into Court withiu 14 days and notice of appe4l given within the same period, costs to be taxed and paid with an under- taking by the plaintiff's solicitor to refund them in tee event of the appeal being successful. This was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WATER POLO. – Liverpool and District League– --</head>
        <p>WATER POLO. - Liverpool and District League- - --</p>
        <p>Warrington and Liverpool City Draw.</p>
        <p>There was again a crowded attendance at the War- rington Corporation Baths on Tuesday evening, and a splendidly contested match was witnessed. The visitors were Liverpool City, a team of very good reputation. The referee was Mr C E Snowdoo,and the teams entered the bath as follows :—Warrington : Goal,Robertson; full backs,Boacow and Lowe: half back.W Ashton; forwards, T Locker, C Ashton and Su,ton Liverpool City : Goal, , Turner; full backs,McLachlan and Gardbam ; half back, Sherri:irk; forwards, H Mosley,F Hayes and W Willisms. During the first half Warrington defended the s'lallow end, and early on W Ashton sent in a good shot, which Turner just saved. Warrington continued to attack and W Ashton getting possession passed to his brother " Charlie," who opened the scoring for Warlington. The Liverpool men played up with vigour, and shots were aimed at the borne goal in quick succession Rarely has the local custodian been seen to better advan- tage, though for some time he was kept very busily occupied. Half-time arrived with Warrington leading by a goal to nil. On changing ends Liverpool invaded the home territory and F Hayes equalised. Matters actin took a different turn, however, and Locker with a fine shot placed Warrington in the lead. For a time play was of an even character, but ultimately the Liverpool forwards made a rush into the Warrington half and Stiarrocks equalted. There was no further scoring and the game resulted in a draw of Lae, goals each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SAO DROWNING CASE.</head>
        <p>SAO DROWNING CASE.</p>
        <p>A Returned Militiaman's Bereavement. Narrow Escape of a Corporation Employee.</p>
        <p>A sad drowning fatality occurred at Warrington on Monday afternoon. It appears that about four o clock, a number of children were playing on the river side near the Corporation wharf at Howley, when one of them, a boy named Michael Tansey, aged 8 years. and residing in Haydock-street, was observed to fall into the eater. He was got out by a constatle assisted by a Corporation employee, but by that time life was extinct. The inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Tuesday evening by Mr S Brighouse, County Coroner. Mr Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk) watched the proceedings on behalf of the Corporation. Bridget Tansey, the mother of the dec,ased, stated that her son left home on Monday afternoon, so he told her, to go to the sports. She sat up the whole of the night nursing a sick child. She expected the deceased to return borne every minute. At eleven o'clock on Tuesday morning, as he had not turned up, she went to the Police Statioe to inquire after him. She was then told that a boy had been drowned and that the body wss lying at the Lower Seven Stars. She went there , and identified the deceased as her son. I John Forster, a little bey, stated that at about four 1 o'clock be and several other boys were PlaYloC on the river side near the Co:potation wharf,w hen the deceased lay on hie stomach and commenced to push a boat with his hands. The boat was tied. The deceased then slipped into the water. Thomas Brown,' pavior in the service of the Corpora- tine, stated that he was ongaped in unloading a boat alongside the wharf when he noticed five or six children playing about. He had left the wharf to fetch the turtles when he heard a shout. He at once returned and Was in time to Fee the deceased sink. The police soon arrived and gr t a boat and he assisted them to grapple for the body. Witness, howevec, overbalanced himself and fell into the water and was pulled out by the police. He had to go home,and he bad been unable to work that day. I In reply to the Coroner witness said he could not swim. Irspector Taylor: He had a narrow shave, be was .almost drowned. Brown : I tried my utmost to find the body. P.C. Monks gave evidence as to the finding of the body. Brown overbalanced himself and fell into the water, and the police Mid some difficulty in getting him out. His place was taken by a man named William Talbot. Mr Wilson said he wished to express his regret with the parents of the child in the sad loss they had sty,- tairwd. Ile was sure that all who had given evidence that evening did their hest to save the child's life. The Coroner : I always appreciate these expressions. feel hurt when companies concerned do not seed some One to represent them at the inquest. Inspector Taylor : The boy's father is a militiaman, and he only returned on Saturday. i The C ironer : Ir is a very sad accident. I A verdict of" Accidental Death " was then returned:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Funeral of Mrs. H. R. Dodd.</head>
        <p>Funeral of Mrs. H. R. Dodd.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mrs H Dodd were in- terred yesterday week in Burbage churchyard, beautifully situated among the Derbyshire hills, The funeral service was conducted by the Rev E C Carleton, of Warrington. The coffin bore the in- scription, " Augusta Jane Ayseough Dodd,died 29th of July, 1901, aged 60 year,' and was followed to the grave by the husband and .children of the de- ceased, Dr Russell Dodd and Miss Bloxham. In the church were Mr Vere and Mr Leonard Davies, I Miss Constance and Mr Fletcher (Whitley). Beau- tiful floral tributes were sent by the following:— Husband and children, Mrs W ykeh am Perry (sister) Miss Dodd (London) Mrs Edward Dodd, Mr and Mrs Charles Dodd, Colonel and Mrs Dodd, Dr and Mrs Russell Dodd, Miss Bloxham, Mrs Bolden (Bur- I bap), Mrs Broadbent and ramify, Miss Constable, I Mr and Mrs Veto Davies, Mr Leonard Davies, Mrs Ellison (tztretton),Mrs Rrnest Fletcher, Mr and Mrs ()metier, The Misses Harvey, Mr and Mrs Logan Wright, Mr, Mrs and Miss Lyon, Colonel and Mrs IRidgway, Rev and Mrs Gordon Robertson, Mr and ;Mrs Sewart (Lancaster), Mr a and Miss Sumner,Miss 1 Wigram,Rev S and Mrs Wilkinson, and some neigh- bours at Burbage. As a,mark of respect to the deceased, the bells at Stretton Church were muffled on Sunday, and the Dead March in" Saul "played at the services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Annual Prize Meeting at Altcar. Oa Monday, the members of the Ist V.B. Sou Lan- cashire Regiment (P.W.V ) proceeded to Altcar Rifle Range for the annual prize meeting. A good number paraded a, 8 30, under the command of Colonel J C Ridgway, V.D. The men proceeded by special train from Bank Quay station a. 9.20, arriving at Altcar about 10.30. On arrival the men marched down to the range and the teams were selected iur the various team , competitions- A b..filing wino was blowing from the , sea and this mitigated against good shooting. The rules of the meeting were :—Uniform—drill order with one pouch, (Lo be worn in all competitions.) Unless other- wise stated, all shooting at 200 yards to be kneeling, 500 yards lying down ; 600 yards, any military ; end all competitions to be fired with one sighting shot and seven rounds at each distance, except where otherwise stated. The meetings to beheld undet Musketry Regu• lations, 1898, and Bisley Rules 1901 in i•ci tar as tney apply. Lbç decision of the senior Officer present to be fiat in any disputed point. Pull off:' minimum Gibs ammunition : That issued by the Ba;talion only. During the a ay, the men were provided with a substantial dinner and tea. The following were the results of the different com- petitions :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Team Competitions. TfiE BATTALION CHALLENGE SHIELD.</head>
        <p>Team Competitions. TfiE BATTALION CHALLENGE SHIELD.</p>
        <p>Kindly presented by the late Lieutenant Colonel W Mac-Carthy O'Leary. when cJminanding the 18t Bat- talion P. W.V. (South Lancashire Regiment) to be best Company of this Battalion at Practicai Field Musketry. To be competed for annually and to be held by the winning Company far the year, under the conditions of the donor. let Prize, ;the Shield and £l2 ; 2nd Prize, £4; 3rd Prize, £2. This Competition was open to teams 01 16 men from each Company, each team to be com- manded by an officer or sergeant. The teams fired five volleys at 1,000 yards distance from "Heads and shoul- deis" targets and advacced by 50 yards at a time up to within 250 yards of the target, halting and firing one round, independent, at each advance. Result;-1, E Company (70 points).</p>
        <p>THE LADIES' TROPHY (VOLLEY FIRING). Open to teams of eight men from each company,com- manded by an officer or sergeant of the company. First prize, handsome challenge trophy presented by the ladies of Warrington, to be held for the year by the winning company under the conditions of the donors, and £5 added money ; and prizes of £2, and £1 if five or more companies enter. Distance-500 yards, single rank, kneeling. Rounds, ten. Loading from the pouch onl7. Time allowed—two minutes from the command "Ready." Targets—volley. Result i-1, B Company ; 2 and 3, E Company and Mounted Infantry Company (a tie).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>GENERAL ROOME'S PRIZE.</head>
        <p>GENERAL ROOME'S PRIZE.</p>
        <p>Open to teams of eight men from each company, commanded by an officer or a sergeant of the company. Ist prize, a handsome challenge trophy presented by General F Roome, C. 8., to be held for the year by the winning company, under the conditions of the ootior, and £5 added money ; and prizes of £2 and £l, if five or more companies enter. Distance-600 to 200 yards. Extended order. Targets—heads and shoulders. The teams fired a volley at 600 yards, advanced about 35 paces, halted and tired a volley, advancing again in a similar manner to the 200 yards firing 12 volleys on the way. At 200 yards three rJunds rapid indi pendenr fire were delivered. Result :-1, .Mounted Infantry Com— pany.</p>
      </div>
