      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ACTION AGAINST MESSRS</head>
        <p>ACTION AGAINST MESSRS</p>
        <p>WALKER.</p>
        <p>A:Conviction Depreciates a Licence.</p>
        <p>Important Verdict.</p>
        <p>Before Mr Justice Ridley and a special jury on Wed- nesday in the Nisi Prius Court, an action upon an indenture of lease for breach of civenant was brought by George Wright Mumford, brewer. Liverpool, against Peter Carlaw Walker and others. The defendants, who denied the breach, were represented by Mr PiAfr.rd K.C., and.Mr F E Smi h.</p>
        <p>_ Horridge, LC., who along with Mr Ls lie Scott, appeared for the plaintiff, stated that Messrs Walker were the well-known local firm of brewers, whilst, toe plaintiff was also a brewer, though in a smaller war of business. He was the owner ot the Old Abbey Inn, Leicester-stree. Birkenhead, and under a feurteen 5 ears' lease,dated 1886,he let the premises to Messrs Walker. In the lease a protective Covenant had htet, inserted to the effect that the lessee would so conduct and maurlge the house as to afford no reasonable or lawful ground or pre- tence for the justices refusing to renew, endoise, or object to the renewal of the iicence. Messrs Walker let these premises to a tenant by agret ment, in which there was a clause sto the conduct of 'he 11 use in accor- dance with the original covenant ti,tteeen plaioiff and defendants, On the Ist, 2nd, and 3rd August of last year the tenant was convicted for opening his Louse be- fore six o'clock in the moiling, but nst, ithstanding these:rconvictions the magis.rates renewed the licence at the next sessions. Cnunsel argued that this did not t rid of the danger to the licence, for if a further con- , viction took place the previous conviction would be taken into consideratiou,anel the magistrates would say that the license must go. Mr Horridge pointed cut that , during the last few years, since the case of Sharpe v. 1 Wakefield, licensed houses, as regards renewals, had been very much more sensitive property than they were before, and toe fact of a house getting convictions against it was a much more serious thing now than it was formerly. This was more particularly the case in places like Liverpool and Birkenhead, where tne feeling of the magistrates was that there were too many licensed houses. Consequently it Was extremely impor- tant that the premises under consideratim should not have a conviction recorded against them. Plaintiff in the course of his evidence stated that before the convictions Me house was worth £3,500, but , since the convictions it was net worth more than £2,000. , Other evidence as to the depreciation of the value of the property was given. For the defendants,expert evidence was called to show that as a selling property in the market the house was worth as much to-day as it was befere the convictions. Mr P.ckford urged that the question for consideration was not so much that these convictions would_ lessen</p>
        <p>the price of The -house or make people shy of taking it, but the jury must Sek themselves whether the conduct which led to the convictions was such as to fford reasonable ground for the justices refusing to renew the licence, which was not the case. Counsel maintained that there had been no depreciation in value. Mr Horridge insisted that £5OO was not an exorbitant figure at which the jury should assess the diminution in the value of the licence. The. jury awarded the plaintiff £375 damages. His L Irdship reserved judgment, pending the settlement of a legal point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>TOWAGE OF A VESSEL AT RUNCORN. Action against the Ship Canal Company.</head>
        <p>THE TOWAGE OF A VESSEL AT RUNCORN. Action against the Ship Canal Company.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Wednesday, refore Mr Justice Buckalli and a special jury in the Nisi Prius Court, the case of Busy and Palmer v Manchester Ship Canal Company was heard. In this action the plaintiffs of Yarmouth, owners of the brigantine Bare Bell,songht to recover from the defendant company damages for injury to their vessel, caused by alleged negligence in towing.. The defendants denied the negligence, and al'eged that they were protected by an agreement. Mr. Horridge, BC, and Mr Leslie Scott were for the plaintiffs, and Mr Ptokford, i C, and Mr Hill for the defendants.</p>
        <p>Tee case for the plaintiffs was that the Hare Bell, which had been loaded with a cargo of coals at Runcorn, for Falmouth, was being towed out of the 8 ip Canal by the company's tug Brackley. Somebody told Captain Palmer, who was in charge of the brigantine, that be had to go ant sign his clearance. The captain went to an office, and be was then asked to sign a document headed " towage contract." Thetug took charge of the Hate Bell on the 16th May, and towed it down to Easthana Lock. It was alleged that the tug was handled in such a way that the brigantine first had tier rail , damaged,aiad then struck against a dolphin and serious- ly damaged her stern. On the .17.11 May, or immedi- ately after the accident, the plaintiffs wrote to the defendant company holding them responsible for the ' damage, whereupon the Snip Canal Company replied that they would hold the plaintiffs responsible for the damage to the dolphin. Captain Palmer, in giving evidence in corroboration of this statement, saia that when he signed the paper , givers to hlm by the Ship Canal official, he had not got his glasses, and he did net know that it was a towage voucher or dortket. Mr Pickford said be did not think be could say that the tug did properly tow the brigantine, but the ques- tion was whether they were protected by the contract signed by the master of the vessel. The document was , headed "Towage docket" in very large print, which could be read, he should have thought, without glasses. His Lordship said he would put to the jury two ques- tions—" Was there in fact a contract made as in the language of the document signed by the captain ?'' and " Was the captain negligent in placing his signature on the paper be did ?" Did he put it there carelessly or in a reasonable or ordinary way considering the circum- stances ? His Lordship pointed out that the towake docket signed by the captain absolved the Ship Canal , Company from responsibility in regard to towage even in case of wilful negligence.</p>
        <p>The jury found that there was no contract, but that the captain bad been negligent. Judgtnent wmt deferred.</p>
        <p>The Bth Annual Conference of Churches of Christ in Great Britain and Ireland, co-operating for evangelistic purposes, met on Tuesday, and following days +it Nottingham, under the chairmanship of Mr James Flisher, of Manchester. A very interesting paper was read by Mr John Straiton, of Londonderry, entitled " The Christian's duty in relation to the use of intoxi- cating Liquors." Mr C L Bothers (Nottingham City Coroner) gave some striking extracts from the recent speech of the most eminent scientist of the day at the recent conference in London on Tuberculosis, showing that Alcoholism is closely allied to that fearful disease. This question of chink is the great social question of the day and the only solution of it is total abstinence. He could not bring himself to believe that our Lord evair I sanctioned the use, even moderately, of intoxicating drink. Mr George Collin, of Carlisle stated that in the day when the drink traffic was established a Cecil was to the front,so now in a government that will do nothing for the cause of Temperance.</p>
        <p>WANTED COOR-GSNRRAL.—AppIy BELVOIR VILLAS, 38,Upper Cher Mon-road, Whalley Range, Manchester.</p>
