        <p>AUGUST 9th 1901.</p>
        <p>It will be a matter of The Late Empress. great consolation to our Sovereign to find h° warm and unaffected is the tribute of admiration</p>
        <p>paid to the late EMPRESS FREDERIcx by the German Press. There was a period when thig august lady was misunderstood in the Fatherland although her rare talents never lacked appreCia- tion. To-day the Courts of Germar y and Great Britain are mourning together in perfect ,ympattlY and the German people are earticularly anxious to evince their sense of the splendid virtues and noble courage of the dead EMPRESS —the more so he; cause at one period she did not receive her rrgedhas° praise. The event, though not unexpected, plunged the nation into grief. Six months when her Royal mother was stricken with her 15-1 illness, it seemed hard to tell which of the rega</p>
        <p>ladies would first suCc-c;m-b—to..;Ce-feTl d'e-s-troYer. The case of the EMPRESS was known to be danger' ous, and appeared even so hopeless that it was thought she would be the first to pass within tile veil. Yet she rallied for a time, and she has since been engaged, away in the seclusion of her Pig° at Kronberg, in a determined encounter with tll9, Last Enemy, which she has sustained with all the strong courage and firm fortitude Which have merked her Royal family and race. Peace t° her ashes; her memory will live in the hearts of, many Englishmen, and many more of the sons 01 her adopted country, whose good she loyallif sought ever after she trod its soil as the wife 0c FREDERICK WILL/AM. As the Crown Princess °I England she was a favourite with the English people, and the womanly devotion which she menl. fasted to her husband was equalled by that aoe/r. dad to her son; when a momentary estrangen3eet occurred on the accession of WILLIAM 11. it WaSt° perhaps, among the hardest trials of I.ler life. ICe: her extraordinary tact, supplementing an ardent maternal affection, found a way of smoothing over difficulties, and restoring the family relations t° their original tone. Such a victory had El°4 enduring and beneficial effects on the career of ber impulsive, though distinguished, son ; and if Ilifb subsequent policy has been marked by model- ate and reasonable tendencies, and a ra°,,a,re amiable attitude to this country, it maY '- traced to the influence of his Royal mother' During the obsequies of Queen Vicrome EDWARD set an excellent precedent in the sPect consideration which he displayed towards German EMPEROR, the effect of which was We marked on our Teutonic cousins. A reciprocati° of courtesy and sentiment cannot fail to telt; place, which ought to strengthen that effect a% further increase the growing friendship between PA two nations. Thus out:of evil often cometh P°o; and bereavements among Royal personages, Ili° more than matrimonial alliances between croWned heads, may do much, through the tenderness en regard which they inspire, to increase maul! respect and esteem. This, though purely Sent;l; mental, has its value, and counts for more tball t`o generally supposed among the factors that go it making or unmaking the peace of the world. 0 will count for much in the continued adveric.e which Great Britain is making, and will contiol`l, to make, in the van of nations to have Gerwa°o. with us in sympathy with our ideals and prejee'ro There is no other Power with which we could ino,o naturally seek an alliance. Of the same rSC.:O, stock, the same religion. the same industrial,phlia sophic and literary parsuits, we have natle4 cow:nor, and we ought to be friends.</p>
