      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>Impany got in the House of Commons a widely spread and ever-growing opinion te sooner the national means of transport are the hands of the nation the better it will be for all parties. With a sharp falling off in dividends, ranging from 11 to II per cent., even the share- Ilaiders may begin to think they might do worse than fall in with some national arrangement, and It ought certainly to lessen the terror with which Conservative politicians have been in the habit of regarding the question. Of course,there have always been slack times, and they have been followed by tunes of prosperity; but, though unremunera- tive, the present time has not been a slack one, as the overcrowded state of many of the trains run by the chief• offenders testifies. The companies</p>
        <p>athaniCoi</p>
        <p>that.i thi</p>
        <p>- ---...--_ have, as a matter of fact, the maximum in bulk of traffic which they can handle; but the manage- alent is so bungled that they cannot turn it out at a profit. On their part they complain, as they have so often complained before, of an exceptional Coal bill, an increased wages bill which there is no h°Pe of reducing, increased capital charges, and fresh demands by the Board ofTrade for more men With shorter hours and improved appliances for the prevention of accidents. Add to this the con- etant pressure from traders for concessions in sates, many of which are not unreasonable,though apparently out of the power of directors to grant and it will be seen that the life of a railway meg- tate is not a happy one, and the prospects of the stockholder not very rosy. If the companies decide —and there seems to be no alternative—on bolding on for better times, of which there is Certainly no appearance in the near future, but rather the reverse, it means raising more Capital on preference or debenture, which will ,absorb the net earnings. When the transport, easiness only serves to pay fixed charges and Working expenses the lines will have been eationalised, perhaps by a gradual and painless Process, but by a nrocess the reverse of satisfac- tory. It therefore becomes a question of wide in- terest whether some legitimate step should not be taken to nationalise our transport in view of the Veeess which has attended municipal enterprise. eaaY be that the undertaking may seem too im- -2488 ; but at least an experiment might be "made with one of the worst London railways, or With the Ti ish railways,the working of which would</p>
        <p>4Upply the experience necessary for the successful</p>
        <p>nParating and gradual absorption of the entire Uational system. It is useless to look to the future with a vague hope of relief. One million tees in dividends will be distributed this half- Year by the twelve principal English lines, and it cierns far from certain that the worst has been ex - Perienced. Even though a wave of prosperity flay come, as it has come before, after long and weary waiting, there is reason to believe that in the case of railways each returning wave of pros- Parity has reached a lower point than its pre- decessor, and anything like permanent improve- Inent is not to be looked for. It is therefore evi- dent that no vested interest of an overwhelming nature exists to act as a hindrance to what is be- abundantly proved to be a much-needed national reform.</p>
        <p>With bronzed faces and weather-stained khaki Ztfits, the 3rd South Lancashire (Militia) Regi- Zeht, arrived in Warrington on Saturday morn- after an absence of eighteen months on active ;I rvine. The best laid plans sometimes go agly as e Scotsman would say, and Saturday's welcome home to the Militiamen did not pass off without a fosappointment. There were many who for the lionee left their beds earlier than usual in the tiTs men at hope of welcoming Colonel Ir3land Blackburne and n seven o'clock in the morning. One half of the reigment arrived at the Central Station in good time but the train conveying the remainder did not steam into Warrington until after nine o'clock. The disappointment Of those who had experienced the long and weary Wait of over two hours in the early morning can be bAter imagined than described. The short- eftllngs of the railway company were, however, °oon forgotten when the returned warriors filed .through the central arch and took up a position M front of the Town Hall. The official welcome Waa conveyed to the officers and men in fitting terms by his Worship the Mayor, who at the close turned the colours of the regiment, knowing that they will be carried at the head of a battalion Whose officers and men have alike helped to in- crease the splendid reputation of the regiment." The men greatly appreciated the welcome and Were still more gratified with the substantial breakfast which had been thoughtfully providied 10r them by the inhabitants. Contrary to expgt- rAtion there was little to complain of in their ondact later in the day and it was a pleasant sur- prise on Monday to find that only one Militiaman had been locked up for drunkenness.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Corporation have applied to the Power as Corporation Limited for a license to manufacture and supply Mond gas for heating and power purposes within the Borough. Such an application shows at least that the members of the Corporation are alive to the importance of keeping pace with the times in the manufacture and distribution of gas for all purposes. Tue liond gas, as it is called, takes its name from the Patentee, Dr Mond, of Messrs Brunner Mond and Company, and it is claimed on its behalf that for manufacturing purposes it is considerably cheaper than most other forms of motive power. Where steam is not a necessity in manufactories, Mond as can be used with advantage, and already one weal firm we hear, is laying down plant for the Manufacture of this new gas. Provided that lia tisfactory terms can be arrived at with the licensees, we see no reason wny the Warrington vorporation should not be in a position to place his cheap gas at the disposal of all manufacturers M the Borough. Cheapneas in production is 1148 entiai if we are to maintain our numerous ',llthistries and in endeavouring to supply menu- Lecturers with cheaper power,the Corporation will acting in the best interests of the community. ------ , The proposed establishment of a Temperance „nttafreshment Bar at the Bewsey Works of Messrs. ,"erson and Knowles Company, Limited, of War- "b.ngton, is a step in the right direction. Tile A 'ear of St. Paul's Church and his organising Committee are to be congratulated upon having ~., i 'um courage to take this step. It s another idence that the work of the Church for the sPiritual, moral and social well-being of the corn- nltznity is practically unlimited, and that it is by no means of a parochial character. There seems no reason why this venture of catering for the Physical wants of workmen should not be . financial success, if conducted upon sound business lines. But the financial aspect is , 110 t the most important. The provision of good food and drink in the midst of such a large 9-lics should do something to minimise the drink . evil. The exacting work of a forge, especially in h °t weather,creates an almost unquenchable thirst, ?nd it is not to be wondered at that bottles 'are umeessantly being conveyed from the works to the , I;arest drink shop with the object of obtaining some ...quid refreshment. It is more than probable that 4 well -kept refreshment bar on the spot would be ehl e to meet the want and reduce the demand for 814t_exicating liquor. In these days when we hear 0 whmuch about soulless limited liability companies pe,°take no interest whatever in their work- pe-Pte, it is at lesst gratifying to find that Messrs pe:narsio: and Knowles have not only given their : rae„. ion for the proposed St. Paul's Refresh- ‘' the'"' Bar, but have generously provided a room in Works for the purpose. It is to be hoped that ;</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>/• t ti 4ft sets 011 tr-i</p>
