a''''"Z':'''-'''''?''''-:4'-';'''''"'l:'''''-':'''''''V'i•;'i',A',Vi,!lifPS,;.::.-,,,i:.:Af,i,.',..,f,;;•,;,it':;..*;.,,,P.Z.'VF...Y.*..4.a.titk?i1-'.'•••.i.4,,j,•',erV,J,T:":k'=:,:;..;,,.:,4 -....1,,f,t117.!!„,•:::&amp;?"2,f.,..14;;;;;,M4E.7,;-.l..th.;f'i:-.C.1,,,,,,•!'..:,,,,,t&lt;,',,',..•.747::' -„,---- .. •••.--,,,•'.,'•••,... ', : , . -.. • ~ - -.-... :-,::.,,„:„ ...,::. •, • ',. . ~.....:, ~ .-, , • , , . „.....,- '</p>
        <p>the experiment will be appreciated by the work- people, and that its success will lead to a further development of the scheme in other works in the town.</p>
        <p>Very timely, the National Reform Union has issued a book dealing with the war against the Dutch republics in South Africa. It has been compiled by Mr H J Ogden and consists for the most part of a condensed selection of interesting and important printed references to the causes, conduct and results of the South African War. It is a bulky volume of over 300 pages and teems with facts and opinions bearing on the war. To those who have not the time and opportunities necessary tor informing themselves in detail of the full story of the quarrel, this book is invalu- able and presents the essential points in a clear and concise form, whilst to speakers and writers it is a veritable vade mecum. The carefully compiled in- dex will make the book a still more handy and convenient form of reference. To thcse who may be crying out in the words of Carlyle "Give me facts, feed me on facts," this book can be con- fidently recommended, for here is something sub- stantial relative to South Africa which should appease the most prodigious appetite.</p>
        <p>What has been the effect of the late rains on the harvest prospects of this year Oa the whole, we gather from the " Times " reports from differ- ent parts of the country, the optimistic view has been justified. The immediate damage done by torrential downpours has proved in few places really considerable, while in the warm days which closed last week vegetation shot up in an atmosphere sometimes suggestive of that of a greenhouse itself. Naturally enough, the improve- ment is most seen in the market gardens, where, perhaps, rain bad been most badly needed. There is little sign of potato blight, except in one limited area in the North. Runner beans look so promising that their yield may be a corn- ' pensation for the comparative failure of the green pea, a crop which came to an untimely end through the premature dying away of the haulm. Fieid beans, however, have suffered very greatly from an attack of the collier fly, which has wrought great ravages over half England. With regard to the cereal crops,wheat-cutting is now in progress—indeed, on some farms is over, though there is no disposition to hurry opeifttions, the present market pricebeing anything but tempting. The yield:is expected to be on the:whole a fair one. Barley differs very greatly in quality in different parts of the country, but the doleful view seems to predominate, the colour of the grain being gener- ally anything but good; while the " Times " , anticipates that the oat crop will be the worst of the three chief cereals. The impovement in pas- ture is the nopeful feature, the prospect of good autumn keep coming opportunely for sheep- breeders on the eve of the principal sheep fairs.</p>
        <p>--- The position with regard to the great steel strike in America, which appeared to have become more favourable, has lately undergone a change for the worse, and there is now every prospect of a desperate struggle, accompanied by incidents perhaps more deplorable than have ever occurred in conflicts: between capital and labour. From this distance it is hardly possible to decide the rights and wrongs of the dispute, but, apart from sympathies with either side, it certainly does seem that the conciliation is at present with the amal- gamated unions and the belligerency on the part of the Steel Trust. Mr Schaffar, who had been practically deposed from the leadership of the men, owing to differences of opinion in the Execu- tive, has been restored to power, with the full authority of a dictator—so much the firm front of Mr Morgan and his friends has done in consolidat- ing the opposition. Mr Schaffer, however, if a dictator, seems to be one of the most moderate men ever burdened with such responsibilities, and his policy of delay, giving the Trust an opportunity of taking up a loss implacable attitude without loss of dignity, is appreciated by the American press, which shows a tendency, after having severely blamed the originators of the strike, to veer round in their favour, or at least, while opposing, to be- come more'sympathetic. The steel magnates,how- ever, seem to have no intention of retreating, but rather an eagerness for the fray. The mills are,we are told, being turned into miniature fortresses, provisioned and armed, and capable of withstand- ing long sieges and determined assaults. Non- union men are being pressed into service, those preferred who know how to hatuile a gun. There may be a good deal of bluff in all this, but it is a dangerous game to play, and one in which the stakes are larger than the players know.</p>
        <p>-- -- The success which has attended the promotion of the Mono-Rail Bill in the House of Commons has come as a great surprise even to many in the engineering world. This great experiment of an electric high speed railway has been considered in certain quarters to be a scheme not sufficiently matured to gain the sanction of a committee of the House of Commons or to deserve the investment of large sums which it will undoubtedly require. " Feilden's Magazine" for this month states that the failure of the Bill in the House of Commons would not distress them "since the final defeat of the scheme would be in the best interests of the public at large." With such a prognostica- tion we do not agree, and the fact that the com- mittee of the House of Commons has approved of the Bill shows that they have some confidence in the practicability of the scheme. There are many engineering difficulties to be overcome before Mr. Behr can give us a mono-rail system with a speed of from 110 to 150 miles an hour which shall be safe to the passengers, but we believe that the dis- trict which enabled Mr. Stevenson to place England in the van in railway enterprise may yet enable Mr. Behr to achieve a like success in his great electric traction experiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>By the death of the late Queen's eldest daughter and the King's eister, the itmpresa Frederick, the "hole cnuntry has again been thrown into mourn- ing,indications of which are seen on every hand. It was the slow martyrdom of the Empress that gave so pathetic a Figniticance to the appeal made by King Edward to the scientist, at the recent conference on tuberculosis in London, to search diligently for the cause of cancer, that mysterims disease which attacks all classes of society, and before whose advances the highest medical and surgical skill is confessedly helpless. From whom the late Emperor Frederick contracted the fell disease that wrecked his splendid congtitutirn and brought to an end a reign eo rich in promise, will never be known perhaps, but it is not u, reason- able to surmise that the germs of cancer were transferred to the Empress during those long days and nights through which she tenderly nursed her stricken husband. There was much in her career that appeal. to English people. When she wedded the Crown Prince of Prussia she did not for a long time find multi favour in the eyes of a people who are habitually antagonistic to the influance of woman in public affair'. Bit more or legs of a change came eventually, and thera cannot be th. e i slightest doubt that sympathy now s as genuine in Germany as it has been, at the decease of the illus- trious lady, in England</p>
        <p>---- " The sweet security of the streets "is another of those generally accented sayings which now and again appear to be flatly contradicted by solid fact. The father of the House of Commons is dela, and the manner of his death causes serious refl •ction. The Right Hon CC' B Beach was driving to the House of Commons the other evening when the horse fell at a point where the street was "up." The vener- able occupant of the carriage was precipitat,d into the roadway and subsequently succumbed to his injuries. It is noteworthy that no member of Parliament has been overtaken by death in such a way for many years; aod the acciden• should assuredly convey some " sort of lesson to local authorities who have charge of the regulation of traffic in our public thoroughfares, especially those under repair or otherwise disturbed. Mr Beach had been a Parliamentary representative continu- ously sinc3 1858, and the distinction of 'father of the House of Commons " descended to him on the death of Sir John Mowbray, to whom it reverted on the demise of 'Mr Pelham Villiers. He witnes;ed duriog his career some historic scenes in the House</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>rHE EXA.MINER: FRIOAY AUGUST 9 1901. RUNCORN AND WIDNES</head>
        <p>rHE EXA.MINER: FRIOAY AUGUST 9 1901. RUNCORN AND WIDNES</p>
        <p>and was the contemporary of many brilliant and distinguished pa,diamentarians.</p>
        <p>School Boards would do well to observe that in a memorandum recently issued by the Board of Edu- cation relating to the regulations for evening schools and classes, the latter state that this year relaxation will be made in regard to the require- ment that application for the recognition of even- ing schools must be sent in before ugust Ist, but that the application should be forwarded as soon as possible to the secretary of the Board of Educa- tion. The form of application issued by the Board must be returned, according to tho instructions on it, on or before TAursday next, August 15th.</p>
        <p>Local celebrations of the millenary of the death of King Alfred the Great have at last actually be- gun, but one cannot help thinking tha interest therein has been largely discounted by so many re- petitions of the leading events of King Alfred's life and times, and of the nature and object of the com- memoration itself, in the daily and weekly press for the last two years or more. Everybody may not feel the same, of course, and as evidence that they do not, it may be mentioned that many great and influential persons have travelled almost fabulous distances to take part in certain of the district celebrations of the last few days. Some people are less sceptical than others as to the occurrences of a ' thousand years or so ago, as related by historians and impressed upon puthful minds through the unerring(?) media of school books.</p>
        <p>To electors of every political persuasion, the customary three-fold signal, "Register, Register, Register ! "has been given. All persons who be- lieve they are qualified to v3te,or are already voters at Parlamental y elections, are everywhere in the provinces being called upon to observe whether their names are on the new list of voters now being exhibited, in the old-fashioned way, on the church and chapel doors, and if not there to see one of the party agents and have the name and address enter- ed correctly. If incorrect, or if the would-be voter is inaccurately described in any way, the result is the same, and the name had better not be there at all. Lodgers desirous of being placed on the register are particularly invited to see to this matter, and let the agents assist them ' • so that these people have only themselves to blame if, on the eventful day of polling, they find they had before the 20th of August, glne wrong. Each political thinker owes it to his party, to say nothing of the country at large, to see that he is duly registered and qualified to vote.</p>
        <p>Large consumers of coal in particular will be interested in an announcement which has just been made on behalf of the Government, to the effect that the latter have in contemplation the appoint- ment of another Royal Commission to inquire into the probable duration of the coal supply, and to generally take stock of the coal measures in this country. The announcement will scarcely surprise those who have observed the importance attached by Ministers to the Government reports on mineral statistics signed by dMr. Le Nave Foster, who has repeatedly asserted that the exhaustion of the coal- fields is a matter of the utmost urgency. Some writers will persist in taking a very gloomy view of the future of Great Britain They are greatly concerned at the 'commercial success of Germany and the United States, and, undeterred by the ridi- cule that was poured en the late Professor Jevons, and others who from time to time have endorsed his opinion, they predict that the coal resources of this country will in the near future practically , come to an end. The latest of these Cassandras is I Dr. R H. Thurston, who contributes a well written article to " Caseier's Magazine" for the current month. It is just about two Nears since Mr Walter Dixon had a similarly pessimistic article 4n the same work. Dr Thurston now opines that before the end of the twentieth century is reached the fossilised fuel which to-day represents the vital energy of the world's industries will have become so expensive as to be practically unavail- able for the multitude of purposes to which it is now applied. He appears to go further into doleful prophecy than did Mr Dixon two years since. It is quite true that to the present period coal has I been used and exported as though the supplies were inexhaustible; bu no one in his proper senses would seriously propose that the present genera- tion should leave the coalfields unworked. or but partially worked,simply for the benefit of posterity. "Necessity is the mother of invention" still, and it is more than probable that the present century will witness some scientific discovery which may go far to reduce the demand for coal as an article of fuel. So that common-sense would assuredly suggest making the most of her natural resources by Great Britain before coal gives place to a large extent to electricity or some other force. Dr Thurston's jeremiad notwithstanding, there is no lack of evidence that we are not yet within measurable distance of the exhaustion of our coal- fields, and that even if we were the English people would know very well how to adapt themselves to a changed condition of things..</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL TREATS. On Wednesday, the Bethesda Sunday school, and the Greenway-road Primitive Methodist Sunday school scholars and trac:hen, to the number of about 500 had their annual outing to the nodsham Hills. The usual tea and amusements were pro vided.</p>
        <p>THE REV J A B MALVERN AT RUNCORN. The Rev J A B Malvern, formerly of Camden Chapel, will on Sunday next conduct the Nelson- street anniversary services at Camden Chapel, In the afternoon he will address a meet- ing presided over by Mr F J Norman, jun. See advt.</p>
        <p>THE SONS OF TEMPERANCE. This society advertise particulars of a demon- stration which will take place on Saturday, August 17th. The members will meet at 2 o'clock, at the Greenway-road schoolroom, parade the streets, sit down to tea at 5 o'clock, and open the doors for an entertainment at 7 o'clock. Mr H Baker, will pre- side, and addresses will be delivered by Messrs E Haworth, P.M.W.P., and W Davies, M.W.P. See advt.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF CAPTAIN RIGBY. • The death is announced of Captain J C H Rigby, Royal Marine Light Infantry, who during the last four years had been serving with the North Nigeria Regiment in the West African Frontier force. He joined the Marine eight years ago, and died in London at the residence of his cousin, Lord Justice I Rigby, in his 28th year. Captain Rigby is well- known in Runcorn, and is a near relative of Captain Rigby, of the Castle Line. The news of his death was received with profound regret.</p>
        <p>THE EORTHCOMING REGATTA We would call our readers attention to the Swimming Gala and Boating Rogatta, which will take place at Rocksavage, near Runcorn, on Satur- day next. The fete is the first of its kind attempted by the club, and promises to be really unique. Amongst many other attractions the Runcorn public will be aff.)rded an opportunity of seeing a water polo match and some excellent swimming and boating races. The entries for all events indicate an excellent programme.</p>
        <p>HOLIDAY AMUSEMENTS. As usual business was suspended throughout the town and docks on Monday, and the town presented an empty appearance. This was accounted for by the inhabitants indulging in the inexpensive but always enjoyable visit to Frodsham and Helsby Hills—Runcorn Park, in the afternoon, was w4ll patronised, and considerable in'erest was taken in the Model Yacht Races on the Lake. Mrs F J Norman presented valuable prizes to the winners. The races, which were sailed in heats, resulted as follows: Second class boats: First heat: 1, Mr Hill's Novelty. Second heat : 1, Mr Hail- wood's Rose. Third heat: 1, Mr Lightfoot's Bat. Fourth heat, 1, Mr Lightfoot's Thistle. Final: 1, Mr Lightfoo's Bat: 2, Mr Hailwood's Rose; 3, Mr Lightfoot's Thistle Third class boats.—First heat: 1, Mr Hoxvorth's Jackie. Second he it; I, Mr Nor- man's Nellie. Third heat: I, Mr Rand's Alice. Fourth heat : Mr Baker's Mary. Fifth heat, Mr Corker's Thistle. Final; 1, Mr Corker's Thistle; 2, Mr Hoxworth'sJackie; 3, Mr Rand's Alice.</p>
        <p>A DAUGHTER'S TERRIBLE DISCOVERY. At the Runcorn Police Court, on Tuesday evening I Mr Robert Dobson, coroner, held an inquest, on the body of Hag fbeth Jermyn, widow of the late I Edward Jermyn, master mariner. of Padstow., Cornwall.—Edward Jermyn, the son, testified that deceased was his mother, and was 46 years of age. She died on Sunday momma about 10.30. Deceased was always in failing health and suffered from 'heart complaint—The daughter Daisy agedl2 years said she was in the house on Sunday with her mlther, but left her for abut half an hour. When she returned she found her mother lying on her side in the passage upstairs. Going to her she said" Mother I have had 3d given me. You have gone to sleep in the passage. Shall I make a cup of tea for you." But getting no answer witness screamed and some neighbours came.—ltda Gleave, wife of Henry Gleave, 34 Egerton-street, said she went into the house on hearing the last witness scream, and found deceased as described. She was quite deal —Samuel Gregory, a police sergeant at Runcorn, went to the house about 11 30 and found things just as the last witness bad described —Dr 1 A McLennan, of Runcorn said he was called in to the house, mid male an external examination. There was a considerable external evidence of dropsy aboet the legs and death was probably due to sudden failure of the heart's action.—A verdict</p>
        <p>in accord with the medical evidence was returned. DEATII OF A RUNCORN MAN IN A DOUGLAS Criuncw. While service was proc teding in the Lech Parade Primitive Church, Douglas. on Sunday night, a gentleman in the congregation was seized with apoplexy and on being carried into the vestibule died immediately. Re turned out to be Mr Broadhurst of Northwich,who went to Douglas on Saturday for the purpose of spending a few days in the Isle of Man. When the seizure took place the sermon, which was being preached by the Rev Mr Lock- hart, of Birmingham, was in progress. The sad event created a profound sensation amongst the congregation. The mournful intelligence reached Northwich on Monday morning, and naturally created no small amount of excitement among the many friends if the be-eaved family The deceased. who was a coal merchant, and was 28 years of age, was widely known in the town and district, and highly respected. The deceased is a Runcorn man, and the son-in-law of Mr Thomas Reynolds, It seems he had gone to the Isle of Man with Mr Reynolds for a holiday, and was "camping out " there with some other friends He was well known •in Runcorn, and the news of his painfully Sudden death created quite a sensation in the town.—On , Monday an inqr.est was held at Douglas. Medical evidence was that death was due to failure of the heart's action, and a verdict to that effect was accordingly returned, EXHIBITION AT THB W/DNES TECHNICAL SCHOOLS, Last Friday afternoon, at the Widnes Technical Schoils, an art exhibition, promoted by the Library and Technical Instruction Committees and the Widnes Photographic Society, was formally opened by Mr F Niel. Previous to the opening ceremony the roems where the different exhibits were dis- played were thrown open to the public, and during the day there was a very large attendance of visi- tors. Valuable paintings were lent by Mr Holbrook Gaskell, Colonel W Hall Walker, M.P. Mr F Gossage, Mr T Sutton Tiromis, and other private gentlemen. The trustees of the Walker Art Gallen', Liverpool, sent a number of fine works from their collection. On the walls were canvasses of Sir E Poynter. P.R.A , Peter Graham, HA.. Thomas Faed, R Fowler, R.I. Kr-kin Nicol, R A.. Adrian Jones and other well-known artists. The Members of the Widnes Photographic Society, St Helena Photographic society, Li vetrol Amateur Photo- graphic Society, the St Helens Sketching Club, and Widnes artiste also contributed specimens of their work. The exhibits have been hung to the best advantage by Messrs R Jack,on and Sons, Liverpool and the Joint Committee are deserving of great credit for the manner in which the arrangements have been carried out. The exhibition remains open until the 170 inst.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Circuit- Appointments The followmg is the final draft of stations for this district :—Warringtou—Theodore Bishop, Charles 11 Hocken, William C J Neville ; sup, John Gunnell.— Runeorn—Alfred Wells, mphrey Idorrison,James B Foster.—Fr3dsham ( Warrington)—Thomas II Bailer, who shall change on two Sundays in every quart.r with ths mini4ers of 'lie Runcorn Circuit; sup, Frederick F Rdmunds,—Widnes—G Beamish Saul, Henry Barraclough.—St ['piens and Prescot— George Parker (a), Charles Fl Dove (Earlestown, Newton-le-Willows, Lancashire), John W Seller (Prescot), R Nloff it Gantrey.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[Br A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>There was what Judge Rowlands wai pleased to Cs!!" a light day" at the Runcorn County Court,on Thursday, there being no business of public inter- est before the Court.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the full Board of Directors of the Widnes and Runcorn Bridge Company was held at Liverpoo', on Thursday, when the contracts and the date of completion of the erection were considered. Runcorn's interests were represented by Mr RII Posnett, J.P , chairman of the Urban Council.</p>
        <p>At the next Annual Llc,?nsing Sessions,to be held at Runcorn, there will be two applications for licenses to sell intoxicants in Higher Runcorn. Mr George Partington again gives notice of an appli- cation, whilst the new applicant is Mr Richard Abel. I understand both applications will meet with very strong opposition from the several tem- perance bodies in the town. Apart from any objection on the part of Temperance Organisations, it should be transparent,considering that the Bench has persistently refused to grant Mr Partington's application year after year, that there is little need for additiohal licensed premises. Indeed taking a general view of the question, the town is over- stocked with public-houses, and if it were possible the licensing Bench would be mora justified in reducing rather than increasing them:</p>
        <p>I hear, though not through an ofthial source, that the dispute at Runcorn Docks has been satis- factorily settled, and that the Company's represen- tatives have now been satisfied that the subject matter of the men's contention was the basis of a conference some three years ago, and that the Company have conceded the point. If it is so the question of "macadam" will always be a conten- tious one, and the subject of a fresh contract for every vessel discharged. The men get 211 a on at Rresent. and at niece work contend they can but earn 2s 6d to 2s 9d per day under good conditions. At other work they earn con-iderably more And argue that "macadam" should be increased a little BO as to level itself with other work.</p>
        <p>It should be thoroughly understood by the pub- lic that there was no strike. One gang simply wished to exercise their right of option, whPreon the manager suspended all other work until the diqcharge of the "macadam" was undertaken. How the difficulty was got over is detailod elsewhere. I make this explanation on account ef reports of a strike which appeared els vvhere.</p>
        <p>-- The Runcorn footballers have all signed on at last, and a much mr,re satisfactory tone prevails in the town. The chances in the new League are being freely discussed, and the redtsils are confluent of a high place at the close of the first year.</p>
        <p>DISPUTE AT THE RUNCORN DOCKS A Temporary Settlement.</p>
        <p>Fr a considerable time a state of dissatisfaction be- tween the dock labourers end the Manchestnr Ship Canal authorities has existed at Runcorn, and on Tars day things were brcught to a bead by the action of a single gang of men, with the result that the wttole of the dock labourers and warehousemen struck work. Oa e Wedwaday, however. Mr J Billam (the president a th Waterman and Porters Association), succeeded in effecting a temporary settlement, and work was resumed. On making inquiries, as to how the matter really stood at headquarters. considerable diffi• denc° and reticence was exhibited by those best able to give the necessary information. Our ' representative, however. has been able to ascertain that the case for the men, as stated by Mr Bliley', is to the effect that some little time ago there was a conference on various matters rebating to the men employed at the Docks. At this conference there were present Messrs Oldfinid. Meadowcroft, and Digges (Manchester Ship Canal Company), and Messrs J Billam and E Getley (Upper Mersey Waterman and Porters Association) and several other interested gentlemen. As stated, several matters were discussed, and amongst them the question of discharging minerals and macadam. The men asked for an increased rate for discharging macadam,to which the Company's representatives replied it was impossible to give the men an increased rate. It was then stated that unless the mnn got an increased rate for macadam, It would be left an open qrestion for the merchants to find men and gangs to do the work at their option for the present rate. On Friday when there was very little work in the Docks, there was a vessel there loaded with macadam. An order was given to the first gang to do the work and it would have been done without demur had there been boats into which to discharge it. In con- sequenoe it stood over until Tunsday morning. Or Tuesday morning there was a lot of work in the drcks The first gang were again told to discharge the vessel with macadam, but they declined saying they were not compelled to work on the macadam when there was other work. The manager replied that they would have to go to the macadam. The men absolutely refused. The manager would not allow other work to proceed unioaa the gang went to the macadam, and a 'dead lock" resulted. Subsequently the whole of the dock leheurers and members of the Uppnr Mersey Waternaen'e sad Porters' Association struck work. Mr Bittern had an interview with Mr J Meadowcrift on Wednesday morning, and it was decided to allow this dispute in regard to the macadam discharging to remain in abeyance,and for the other men to go on with the other work, vending sown arrangement with Mr Oldfield (the manager of the Bridgewater Canal under- taking. Mr Meadowcroft, interviewed, maintains there is no arrangement whereby it is optional for the men to unload macadam or anything else ; and quote i the fact that 13 vtulaela out of 17 which came to the Dock con- tained macectana, and were discharged recently without dispute.</p>
        <p>- . Pending the sArthment work h now proceeding se usual at the Docks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
