      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RAILWAY MYSTERY AT ST. HELENS• Gentleman found Unconscious in a Carriage.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY MYSTERY AT ST. HELENS• Gentleman found Unconscious in a Carriage.</p>
        <p>-- A sensational discovery was mada in a first-olams com- partment of an naoreas enrridor train from the North to Liverpool, at St. Helens Railway Station, on Wednesday nipht.. . _</p>
        <p>The train in question, which is not timed to stop al St, Helens but rung through direct to Efigehill, where it is due :bout 7 30, drew up at the platform at St. Helens, however, and the officiate were informed that a gentleman in efirst-eass(compartment was seriously ill, and medical aid was wanted. The gentleman in ques- tion wae removed into one of the waiting rooms,and Dr Jackson was summoned. That gentleman found the passenger to be suffering from the effects of poison, and as he was in a very critical condition le was removed to the Providence Hospital, where both Dr Jackson and his assistant, De Brydon,attended him. It appears that on the stoppage of the train at Wigan, Mr Bromilow, of Radcliffe, was about to enter the com- partment in question, on a journey to Liverpool, when he noticed the gentleman lying either ill or asleep on one of the seats. He drew the attention of the station master to the fact, and the stationmaster tried to aabroouustethoisrota.rtA, atntdhatthemwomigeannt,sthiotwthenvmear,detrhegatvraeinorder gs 1 for the train to be stopped at St Helens, and this was I done The gentleman, who appeared to be between 35 and 40 years of age, was well dressed in a cycling coatume• In the compartment was found a lemonade or other mineral water bottle, and a small glass or tumbler which had contained some liquor and a little sediment was left therein. These articles were taken possession of by the police officers who were informed about the occurrence. The unfortunate man was in a completely unconscious condition. An examination of his pockets pointed to the fact that the unknown man was Dr Vincent Wigziesworth, who belonged to Wesham, Kirkham, near Preston, where his brother is believed to reside. It also seems highly probable that Dr Wigglesworth has for some time been confined in die Crichton Royal Institute or Asylum, Dumfries, and that he suffered from a most distressing form of heart disease Br letters in his pockets, Dr Wiegleeworth was evidently in the institution as late as Sunday last, and it is thought he may have been travel- ling from Dumfries to Preston. and on the way been seized with illness, and had taken a powder in order to obtain relief. He is &amp;bent thirty-four or thirty-five years of age and a M R.O S., and L.R.C.P. After being admitted to the hospital, he was in a most serious state, and for two or three hours had to be kept alive by art4ficial respiration and the most strenuous exertions on the part of Dr Jackson. Dr Wigglesworth, died at balf.past one on Thursday morning. He was a native af Farnworth, near Bolton, but left there several years ago, and subsequently wasin practice at Southport and Poulton, as a qualified medical practition.o. For some time past he has had no settled residence. Four years ago be had an attack of religious mania, and was reeently in an hospital at Nice for about six rnonths.suffering was allowed parole. from the same complaint leaving there about three mTnths ago. Since then he has been in the Royal Institution at Dumfries for treatment. He was sunposed to be getting better, and I: appears that he escaped from the institution on Wednesday and the next that Nr3S seen of him was his being found at Wigan.On Thursday morning his brother, Dr Sidney Wiggleswortb. of Weehano, Kirkham, visited St Helens and identified the hody.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>A remarkably good record is that of which the Ley- land team can boast. During the last four years they have not sustained a single defeat and it has long been the ambition of many teams to put a check to their triumphant career. Last Saturday the Leyland con- tingent had Warrington for their opponents and once more came out victorious. From this it must not be inferred that the Wdrringtonians made a poor exhibition. Qoite the contrary. Although the Warrington team was far from being a repre- sentative one, the players acquitted themselves unusually well and had it not been for an accident which befel W U Podmore the chances are that the match would have been drawn.</p>
        <p>Warrington batted first and were all dismissed for the aggregate of 161. The captain J W Bibhy, once more demonstrated his skill with the bat, his innings realiz'ng 46. He went to the wickets at the fall of the fourth stump, and woe the ninth batsman to leave, not having made a single faulty stroke. E A Connor was also in first rate form, and compiled an aonptable 25. Other uselu, contributions were those Of J Callon (19), J A Bziggs (16), E Woods (16) and P B Hawtin (11).</p>
        <p>Levland had two hours in which to complete their task and they set about it with ccnfidence. The score had reached 56, when Kershaw was taken in the slips. At this stage W El PAmore, who had relieved Gibson, met with an accident which incapacitated him from taking any further part in the match. In fielding a return from Aar J Stan- ning, he unfortunately sprained his ankle and was carried from the field. Four wickets went down in quick succession and half the side were out for 99. H Dewburst and J E Staining were now partners and they hit out with a will. &amp;arming was eventually cauglit hy Bibby after knocking up 32 J I' Anson filled his place and three minutes from time made the stroke which gave Leyland the victory.</p>
        <p>For the winners J Stanning piled on the largest individual score, planing 37 co his credit. J E Stan-ing was responsibin for 32 whilst H Tinsley contributed 29 and H Dewburst 27.</p>
        <p>The engagement between Warrington and L,yland second elevens resulted in a draw, but the draw was greatly in favour of the L yland eleven. The match took place on the Arpley enclosure, and Leyland winning the toss, decided to have first knock. They sent in A Calderbank and W K.rirman, and singular ?r, relate these two batsmen were responsible for ths whole of the runs placed to the credit of the visiting contingent. In vain did the War- rington howlers adopt their most " tricky " tactics, and the Leyland captain declared the innings closed wh-n the score had reached 218. Kirkham was responsible for the substantial score of 111, whilst Calderbink rattled up 103 The Warrington eleven had compiled 110 for the loss of 6 wickets when play ceased for the day, B Silcock being the most useful batsman with 46.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills are still " going strong." On Saturday they met St Thomas's, St Helens eleven, and after a well contested game, succeeded in defeating them by the margin of six runs. Cook WA, top score for the Wire Mills, his innings being productive of 26 runs. Taylor knocked up a creditable 19. For St Thomas's Ratcliffe (13),Anders (12) and Smith (10) were the most .useful contributors.</p>
        <p>It was a peculiar coincidence that 13) the same major- it) that the first eleven seeured a victory Sankey Wire Mills 2nd suffered defeat. Their opponents were Moore and the villagers succeeded in raising 56 runs agsinqt the Wire Mills 50 For they winners W Antrobus (12) was the only placer to reach double figures, whilsc Burton (17 not out) was the chief support of the Wire Mills.</p>
        <p>Richmond's received quite a surprise on Saturday when they encountered the Appleton and Stretton joint eleven. The match was played at Appleton Hall, and the country team proved themselves more than equal to their friends from the town. Batting first, Rich- mond's were all dismissed for the aggregate of 32, and to this the home team responded with 75 J Houghton (21) was rhe top scores for the victors whilst Heath (11) was the only player for Richmond's to enter into double figures.</p>
        <p>Angling. The other day twn Warrington clubs, styling themselves Sawdust and Flags, and having their headt (platters at the Ring o' Bells Inn, had a fishing match a Vale Royal. A large number of money and other prins were offered for competition and were awarded to the following :—Alf Shaw, Thomas Leigh, Charles Mather. J Lowton, Harry Ashurst, Joe Sheppard, A Rosclw, J Gsrnett. J W Richardson, Edward Lnwton, L. Spencer, James Whitfield, Frank Parry,W Whitfield, J Taylor, W Oakes, W Mather, S Foulkes, W Bailey. J Duckworth, J Oakes and E Gibson. After the match a Blanking concert was held, Mr S Foulkes presiding. Songs were contributed by the Chairman,E W Cook,Cbarles Mather, J Buckley, and recitations by James Bowcock, James Ashurst, Henry Ashurst, James Garnett and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Coming Events • The annual picnic in connection with the Women's Liberal Association, will take place next Thursday. when a visit will be paid to Bollington, Dunham Park. The members of the Association and friends will start from the Liberal Club at 12 noon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Music in Victoria Park.</head>
        <p>Music in Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>The Latchford Band played a—sele—ctiOn of music in tbe above Park on Wednesday evening, under the conductorship of Mr W Makes.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCRros 888 I' BOOTS IN LANCA SHIBB.—Advt Accident in Dallam-lane.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, a: blacksmith named John Cheadle, 63 years of age, living at 16, Beek Dallatn- lane, was admitted into the Infirmary suffering from a broken leg. The injury was caused by a fall in the back-yard. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt Will of Miss Ellen CrOsfield. _</p>
        <p>Miss Ellen Croatiald, of MotTad-al-e-14-Ouse, Aig- burth, daughter of the late Mr Henry Crosfleld, who I died on the 11th June last, left personal estate of the net value of £37,496 16s 9d. The executrix and ' executor of her will, dated the 28th October, 1895, are her sister Miss Henrietta Margaret Crosfield and her brother-in-law Mr Thomas Wakefield Binyon, iof Bewdley, Worcester, to the latter of whom the 'testatrix bequeathed £265e, to her sister Mrs Edith lElizabeth Binyon £3lO. to Phcebe and Richenda Bett £2OO each, to Cicely, Margaret, and Elizabeth Muriel Binyon £lOO each, to her cousin Fanny Georgina Wilson .£l,OOO, in trust for her nephew and nieces Georße Henry Croslield and Beatrice Katharine Mabel and Monica:Crosfield £l,OOO each, and to Jane Fletcher and Elizabeth Bolland, and Jane Rad and Eleanor Jane Low £2O each. The late Miss Crosfleld left her household effects and the residue of her property to her said sister Henri- etta.</p>
        <p>KITCREN 13ANG119.' Tile Grates, Marble and Wood Chimney pieces. -J.b. Odstann.sltoss. Br!ride-et—Adv. Friendly Society Picnic to Chester. The local agents employed by the Royal London Friendly Society had their annual picnic yesterday week, the venue selected being Chester. Together with wives and friends, the party numbered 37,and a start was made at 7 30, one ot Messrs Maginn's brakes having being chartered for the occasion. Mr TII Ashcroft, the superintendent of the district, avompanied the trippers and the drive proved thoroughly enjoyable. A halt was made at Prod- sham, and after a ramble through the village an impromptu concert was held,at which Messrs Bridge, I T Ashcroft, jun, Campbell (late of Egremontl, and Macklin (Widnes) contributed reversl songs, the I humorous items by Mr Campbell creating much laughter. A start was afterwards made for Chester and the headquarters for the day,the Queen's Hotel were reached at 12 30 Here the host,Hr Topping. served up a repast, which it is needless to add was much enjoyed. and afterwards a sail on the Dee to Bccleston Ferry was one of the most agreeable fea- tures of the outing Chester was reached at six o'clock, and tea was served. The return journes was made at seven o'clock, and it was generally voted that the picnic was one of the most success- ful yet 134 1. The arrangements were ably carried out by Messrs T 11 Ashcroft, ten, J Bailey, and H Dooley. GLEN-SPEY Dismty.s.r. Situate in the eneit Whiskv-producing District of Scotland. is the property of W. &amp;A. Gilbey. This Whisky is made entirely from home-grown Barley and is kept absolutely unblended in his Majesty's Bonded Warehouses to mature, and sold bv W. &amp; A. Cillbey's agents in every town at 3s. 6d per bottle</p>
        <p>LOCAL LICENSING CHANOES• At Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday, the magistrates (Colonel Wood and Mr J W Emmett) granted the following licensing transfers :—The Oak free Hotel, Newton, from Mary Ann Gorse, to Louisa Martha Gorse; Broad Oak, Sankey, from William Appleton to Ann Appleton; Legh Arms Hotel, Newton, from Seth Hutchinson to William Willan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Bolci-street]Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>At this place of worship on Sunday the Rev T Bishop preached in the morning, and the Rev J tiunnell in the evening. Miss L )uie Davies sang the solos " Angels ever bright and fair " ,Handel) and " Lead kindly light " (E Pugh Evans.) Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>Owing, to the holidays there was not as large an ' etendance as u-ual at the meeting of this society on Sunday. Mr R Atkinson presided and in referring to the traits in Abraham's ckaracter, commended his spirit of thankfulness as one which might well be followed more Closely in the present day.—Mr Wiili,rns or Manchester, delivered an interesting address. The service was also taken part in by Mr Hawthorn and Mr Simcock. Mr T Wallington, jun, presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY AT 'EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON. Except by the tradesmen of iLarlestown and Newton, Monday was not observed as a general holiday. Messrs. McCorquodale and Co., of Newton, not being parties to tb e general holiday movement for the work, closei their establishment, but none of the other works were generally closed, although several of the workmen could not resist the temp- tation of jo,ning with others who required a rest from their labour. There was no attraction what- ever in tha town, although a large number of people journeyed as far as Buttonwood to join in the festival in connection with the Dahlia Queen Coronation. The weather was not of a very en- joyable chartcter, being cold and threatening'</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO THE RACES-CRUELTY TO HORSES.</p>
        <p>Alfred Baldwin was charged at the Newton Petty Sessions, on Thursday, befcre Colonel Wood and Mr W Ilmmett, with cruelly ill-treating a horse. Detendant pleaded guilty, and Inspector Robinson. a the Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals, said that on Friday. July 19 he met Baldwin in High-street, Newton, !miring a black mare most cruelly. It was completely cover-d with weals, and wag in an exhausted state. Be had been to the Hay •ock Park Races, an wanted to get back.—The Bench infl,cted a flue of 10s and costs. —Robert Workman also appeared to answer a similar offence. A plea ot not guilty was put in. Inspector Print said that on July 19 be saw the defendant at Haydrck Racecourse driving a horse attached to a waggonette. He was thrashing the horse most brutally with the whip, and the skin was broken in .everal plac-s and it was bleeding. Detendaw, said he had had to hurry to the races, and was afraid of getting behind. Inspector Robinson corroborated, and ‘4aid that there was no excuse for the defendant to beat the animal in such a manner. The bench fined ,vork man 10 and costs.</p>
      </div>
