      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>OUR FOOD.</head>
        <p>OUR FOOD.</p>
        <p>Constant overworking of the stomach must produce rlisease of that organ. More nutrimeett is furnished to the body by a small quantity of food well digested than by a large quantity half digested. The only trustwolthy and permanent remedy is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, which, acting upon the digestive organs, strengthens them and stimulates them to healthy action and gives tone to the whole stomach, imparting new life, hope, strength, and happiness. Overwork ought to be overpaid. Nature insists upon payment of her dues. Intense devotion to business gives the digestive organs only a bare chance to perform their work. He who comes in a hurry from ac...ive work, rapidly swallows hi.; food and hurries rut again, is piling up a debt wh,cn Nature makes him pay in a painful dyspeptic experience. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters will help you to pay, and sooner you take it the better. Delicate patients often complain that they are unable to take quinine, as it produces headaches, LI, but it has been established beyond doubt that Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has overcome this difficulty, containing, as it does other medicinal herbs of great therapeutic value,such as sarsa- parilla, saffron, dandelion, gentian, and lavenelf.r, scien- tifically prepared in happy proportions. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, by all chemists and stores. Beware of imitations. The name " Gwilym vans"E must be found on the label, SUMO, and bottle, to insure its being genuine. When you find the n tme, then you are sure there is no mistake. The Sole Proprietors are The Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
