      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT MILITIAMAN.—PREPARING FOR THE HOME COMING OF THE 3RD SOLITE{ LANCASHIRES.-•••LENIENT MAGISTRATES. Michael Scully, 20, Town-hill. appeared at the War- rington Police Court, on Friday morning, ct arged with being drunk and disorderley in Horsemarket-street at 8.5 on the previous evening and assaulting P.O Redding ton.—P.o Reddington said that at the time in question he was on duty in florsemarket-street and saw the prisoner who was drunk and acting in a very disorderly manner. Prisoner went inside the British Workman's Refreshment Rooms and commenced eparrelling with a respectable young man. The young man went into the street to avoid the prisoner. Scully followed him and challenged him to fight. Witness requested the prisoner to go away several timer, but he refused and witness was obliged to take him into cust,dy. He became very violent, threw himself on the ground, and kicked out right and left. Witness was forced to put the " snaps" on and called a young man to his azsistance. They succeeded in getting the prisoner down Scotland-road when he threw himself down again, and kinked out. He tried to "top " the young man in the face. Ultimately they gonthe assistance of a 'bus driver and another young man and they had to give the prisoner the " frog march " from Buttermarket-street.to near the police station. Prisoner damaged the shoulder strap on witness's uniform.—Arthur Booth, a porter in the employ of the Cheshire Linea Committee, who assisted the officer corroborated.—Pri4oner stated that he was a member of the 3rd South Lancashire Militia Battalion and had been invalided home. On hearing that the regiment was re turning home on Saturday he got drunk. He now expressed sorrow for the offence.—The Mayor said the magistrates were inclined to take a lenient view of the case entirely owing to the fact That the prisoner had been at the front. They however wished it Cu be understood that they could not have this feeling imposed upon. They should fine him Bs, but at the same time he must understand he was not to play uch tricks. Had it not been for the fact he had h he front they would have sent him to prison, bu_ r wanted to give him an oppor tunity of seeing I tArn r-dee come home.—The magis- trates also expressed their appreciation of the conduct of Booth. A WARRINGTON " ANGLER " FINED FOR TRESPASSING At the Chester County Police Court on Saturday. be- fore Mr H D Trelawy and other magistrates, Daniel Plumpton, 19, Fothergill-street, Warrington, pleaded guilty to trespassing on land in the occupation of Thomas Jeffs, Thornton-Ic-Moors, on the 27th July, in pursuit of game.—Mr F B Mason,appeared for Mr Richard Farmer, solicitor. Chester. who was tenant of the shooting. Mr Farmer was going down Green Turf-lane, and on look- lag over a hedge saw the defendant in a field close to another hedve, stooping down. Mr Farmer went up to defendant and asked him what he was doing, and de- fendant replied that he was gong fishing. He certainly had fishing rods there. On Mr Farmer :coking down at toe hedge defendant muttered something about a piece of string, it had been put there for the purpose of snaring hares, and Mr Farmer found a second one. Defindant was asked to go to Mr Jeffs, but at first he Was very unwilling. Be eventually did so,and Mr Jeffa said be knew the man 'Defendant would not give his name, but subsequently he did. Mr Farmer did not wish to be vindictive, but it was common knowledge that for some time these moo had been in the habit of getting fishing tickets, and instead of going to the Gowey, they went for these hares.—Mr Farmer gave evidence' and defendant was fined lOs and costs or 14 days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>A SSAIILT oos WMIELINGTON POLICE. At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Monday, before Mr Monks (in the chair) and other magistrates, Thomas Line, a labourer, living in Tilley-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in 0 ford-stree, 03 Saturday night, and with assault- ing P.C. Clarke and Detective Forster.—P.C. Clarke was the first witneSt called, and he stated that the °Hance took place at 8 35. The prisoner was drunk and challenged a number of people to" fight. Witness tapped him on the shoulder and asked him to go away, and for a reply the prisoner lou•ted him in the stomach. He closed with the man and they had a tussle in th? street. A man name Eligharn CaO2P to the assistance cf the police, and Lane was removed into Scotland-road on his way to ,he Police Station, when he became very violent and kicked right and left, While in the charge office he used filthy language and struck Detective F rater right and left.— Prisoner, on being asked if he bad anythirg to say. replied" I don'c remember anything about the matter." The Chairman :—ls that any excuse ?—Detective Monaghan informed the Bench that the prisoner was well known to the police. He had been several times before the Benno for assaulting his mother, and had been sent to gaol Fr various terms.—Mr Hayward : He is a bad lot.—The Chairman :He certainly appears to be fond of assaulting people. The magistrates look upon interference will the police as a serious offence. The attack on Foratcr might have been serious. prisoner will have to gl to gaol for two months with hard labour —Bighana was thanked by the Bench for his assistance to the police and was awarded a small sum out of the poor box.</p>
        <p>THEFT Cr BICYCLES AT WARRINGTON-YOUTHS IN TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>- ----- . At the Warrington Po ice Court on Tuesday. before ' Alderman Sutton and other magistrates, two respectably dressed youths named William Hugh Jones (18), and Gorge Carruthera last from Ashhourne, were charged with stediug iwo bicycles from the shop of 'Thomas Daintith, Bridge-street, at 12.30 on Friday. They pleaded guilty.—From the statement of Superintendent Monaghan it appeared that the prisoners went into Mr Daintith's shop at the time named in the charge, and hired two machines for an hour. The second prisoner paid a shilling for the hire and the youths gave addresses in White-street. Nothing more was heard of them, and on Saturday Mr Daintith made inquiries in White-street. He found that the prisoners did not live there and he could discover no trace of them. He re- ported the matter to the police, and it W3s ascertained that at 10 30 on Sa'urday morning the prisoners wenE to a ,hop at Ashbourne, Derbyshire, and offered the machines for sale. They said the bicycles were their own preperty, and that they had come from Stockport. Sorgoant Cheeny brought the prisoners from Ashbourne to Warrington, and when ohar;ecl they admitted the theft. They stated that they had walked Irons Liverpool to Warrington, and being tired faired the machines. They intended to go to London and there dispos,, of the bicycles. One of the machines was punctured in Derbyshire, s 3 they could not go any further. The first prisoner had no parents nut the second had a mother living in Stockport. Recently he bad been employed at Colwyn Bay, and his inkSter Said he had stolen lOs from him.—The magis- trates treated the prisoners as first offenders,and ordered - them to be kept in custody for three days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) Councillor John Roberts and Messrs Hepherd and Thornton. N.S.P.C.C. CASE WITHDRAWN.—Mr Browne mentioned the ciae of Mary Jane Finch, a young woman residing in Knutstord-road, who was charged with neglecting her young child. The case was adjourned some time ago, and Mr Browne now informed the magistrates that the object of the Society had been achieved, and he therefore withdrew the , charge. The principal object at the Society was to prevent I the use of condensed milk for the feeding of babies.--The magistrates agreed to this course. VIOLENT ASSAULT ON AN IRISH LABATRER.—James Hough, 51. liale-str et,was summoned for assaulting Edward Salmon I 313, Winwiok-road, on July 28th.—Mr Steel appeared for the complainant and stated that his client was a young Irish- man fresh from Ireland Some few weeks ago he lodged with the defendant but ultimately went to live at his present address. On Sunday afternoon the landlord and his w:fe went out, leaving the complainant in the house alone. At 2.30 defendant and two friends came in and defendant commenced to quarrel with the complainant. He ultimately made a rush at Salmon,who was poking the fire with a poker. He pulled off his coat and told Salmon he would have to fight. Salmon said he would not whereupon the defendant took the poker fromhim and struck him a blow on the back of the head with it. He tried to commit a further assault upon the complainant but the other men interfered. Complainant had to go to the Infirmary, where the wound was dressed, and he was at present a patient on the books of that institution. He was employed as a labourer at the Water works, but being unable to work owing to the effects of the assault, he lost his situation.—Defendant, who denied having committed the assault, was fined 108 and costs.' WIFE DESERTION.—Arthur Gleave, 8, t honey-street, was summoned for deserting his wife, M era, Elizabeth Gleave—Mr Steel on behalf.of the defendant admitted the desertion but said he was compelled to leave his wife, owing to her filthy and drunken habits. He asked the magistrates to make a separation order with a nominal sum for alimony and to give defendant the custody of the child.—ln- spector Hackett stated that in his opinion the woman was not a fit and proper person to have the custoay of the child —The Bench made a separation :order with an allowance of 4s per week, defendant to have the custody of the child. SATURDbY.--gefore Mr T. Birchen (in the chair), Councillor J Roberts and Mr Hayward. A GERMAN'S STRANGE CONDUCT.—A German named Karl Nagil was charged with being drunk in Lodge-lane at 8.30 the previous night and with discharging a revolver at the same time.—P.C. Muskett said that at the time in ques- tion he was on duty in Bewsey-road near Lodge-lane bridge when he heard two shots fired. fie proceeded along the lane and saw the prisoner who had revolver in his possession. Witness took it from him and found that the bared was warm. There were three" live" cartridges in the revolver and three had been discharged. On being questioned about his conduct prisoner said "Ms fire for fun. If you take my revolver you take me." A number of men were working in a field close by and they wereafraid at the prisoner's conduct. --The Bench decided to fine prisoner 46 (all the money he had in his psissession) and ordered the revolver to be con- fiscated. POLICE OnsvaucrioN —Peter Scu'ly, Town Hill. was charged with obstructing P.C. Reddington whilst acting in the execution' of his duty on Thursday night. It appears that the coustable was taking the prisoner's brother into custody when the prisoner rushed up and commenced shouting • Let him go." fie ultimately got hold of his brother and tried to wreneh him from the officer's grip, but failed to do so.—A fine of 20s with the alternative cf a month's i mpris anment was imposed. VAGRANCY—Thomas Bawkins, of no fixed abode, was charged with being found sleeping on the premises of Messrs Monks, Hall, and Company, without visible means of sub- sistence on Friday night.—Sainuel Lowe, watchman, stated that he was walking round the works on the night in ques- tion, when be saw prisoner in the labourers' cabin. He said that it bethought he (witness) was going to lock him up, he would "put a knife in him." He, however, was bringing him to the palice station, when he met Sergeant Evans, and gave prisoner into custody. MONDAY.—Before Mr Monks (in the chair), Mr Birchall, Alderman Heaton. and Messrs Thornton and Hayward. A RETURNED MILITIAMAN —George Caaning. b private of the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, who returned to Warrington on Saturday from South Africa, was charged with having been drunk in Buttermarket-street on Saturday night. He was the only militiaman brought up and was discharged with a caution. SCENE IN DRTTON-STREET.—George Ogslen, a labourer, living in thatton-street,was charged with having been drunk and disorderly at midnight on Saturday in Dutton-street and assaulting his wife.—P.C. Howard proved the case and stated that prisoner kicked his wife several times while witness had hold of him. He was more like a madman than anything else, and if the police had not come upon the scene the consequences would have been very serious.—A fine of 5s and cost, was imposed. HELPLEeBLY DRUNK.- Michael Cregtan. labourer, Dutton- street was fined 5s and costs for being drunk in Dutton' street on Sunday morning.—Sergeant Williamson, who proved the case, stated that the prisoner was found lying helplessly drunk ma the footpath in front of his house. ASSAULT AND WILFUL DAMAGE —James Donlan, wire— worker. Morris-court was charged with assaulting and beat- ing Annie Donlan, a relative, on the 30th July. and with wilfully damaging a number of windows the property of Thomas Hill,butcher. Winwick-street.—Complainant Donlan stated that the prisoner who had received no provocation, struck and kicked her several times. lie said he •• did not rare for a jury and would do penal servitude for her." The defendant broke all the windows in the house, even the sashes, and a witness estimated the damage at EL—For the assault the defendant was fined 10s and casts and ordered to ray 20s for the Os cage to the windows—in all £35, 60, or go to gaol for a month. ' TUEsDAY,—Before Alderman Sutton (in ,the chair), Mr Birchall, and Mr Young: HER 52ND APPEAR/MCC—Julia Ann Shields, Dial-court.s was charged with haying been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street at an early hour that morning.—P.C. Millington proved the case.—Superintendent Mon sgban said that this was the nrisoner's 52nd appearance before the magistrates. though she is only 31 years of age.—Mr Birchall: I don't think she was in her right mind.—Alderman sutton : It is a pity she cannot be kept in custody penman- ently.—lt was decided to send her to gaol for a month with hard labont BREACHES OF THE PEACE—Mary Ellen Melugh.a married woman, was charged with having been guilty of disorderly conductlin Pieria 'jut-street. on the 27th July. P.O Frank Cooper proved the case and defendant was bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs, 3s 6d.—John Newns. labourer. Church-street. and Minnie Newns, his wife.were charged with a similar offence.in Church-street, on the 29th July. P.O Newey proved the case and the parties were bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs. 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Warrington County Petty Sessions. • ____ WEDNEanalr—Before Messrs If A Frost On the chair) and</head>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions. • ____ WEDNEanalr—Before Messrs If A Frost On the chair) and</p>
        <p>-I Bail.</p>
        <p>REoxideks DRIVERS AT B—Reuben Hewitt and William Connor were summoned for furiously driving horses and traps in Liverpool-road. Sankey. on July 18th—P C Dugdale Fisted that defendants' horses were galloping along the road,and defendants were thrashing the animals with the whipstocks. There was a great deal of traffic on the highway at the time and a number of cyclists were obliged to dis- , mounf. rue defendants seemed to be racing—Defendants ' were e.,cti tined 6s and costs. - NEIGHBOURS AT VARIANCE AT PADDINGTON. —VIOLENT AASAULT,—Geo.,,d J ickson, glass-maker Dog and Partridge- road, Paddington was summoned for assaulting a young mart named James Morris, residing in the same road on the August 3rd. There was a Cross summons against James Morris. and his father Thomas Morris, for assaulting JaC•- son.—Mr T S Steel appeared on behalf of Jackson-- James Morris stated that on Saturday nlgkt be was standing at the back of the houses, when Jackson came up and struck him a blow on the side of the face. Witness had to defend himself.--Hs denied that be or his father kicked Jackson.—On behalf of his oil sat Mr Steel stated that Morris jnn., str,ok Jackson In the mouth and knocked him down, The father then came out and kicked Jackson on the back of the head and under the left eye —The magis- trates dismissed the case against Jackson and fined the Morrises 2e 6d and costs each. THEFT FROM A HATDoCK HOTEL —John Woodacre, a Newton labourer. was charged with stealink three gill glasses. value 6d. from the Waggon and Horses" Hotel. Ha, dock on Saturday afternoon.—John Platt, the landlord, spoke to missing the glasses, and following the prisoner down the road. He asked him if he had got any glasses from the hotel and h e replied that he had not. Ultimately he produced the glasses which had been concealed up his sleeve.—Fined 10s.</p>
        <p>_ THE7T OF Cionmen.—Gerald Fenton was charged with stealing a jacket, pair of trousers, pair of boots, a scud, and a pair of stockings value £1 the property of William 14 lwards. 12. New Boston Haydocc —Prisoner lodged at the house of the prosecutor and last Fri lay decamped with the articles named.—Fined 200 or 14 days.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Reward for Gallant Conduct.</p>
        <p>A brave action was deservedly recognised on Tuesday by the bestowal of the Royal Humane Society's Medal upon John Lowe, lockmaster, of 20 Steps Lock. A short time ago, with great risk Ito his own life he jumped into the lock and res— cued from drowning a cnllcl of about 4 years old. ' There was a clear arop of .13 feet. with 8 feet of water below, at the time, but luckily a rope was thrown to him,and be was drawn to the top having saved the child's life. This brave act was brought to the Society's notice by Mr Bridgwater, and in , the presemca of the staff and employes of the Man- cheater :4 hip Canal Co ,at Howley Quay 3lrsßridg- water made the presentation and commended John Lowe on his gallant conduct. In doing so she said It gave her much pleasure in being asked to under- take that duty, as the ozcasion was one which claimed their sympathy in the great name of hu- manity, and their admiration, for the bravery and courage, in the hope of ,CIViDg the life of a fellow creature entirely forgetful ot self. They were all glad to know he was now none the wore for his I adventure, and that he had been successful in say- ing tne life for which he so readily risked his own. She was very pleased to hand him, in the name of the Royal Humane Society this beautiful testimony of their appreciation of his conduct, for one of the many acts of g,illantry in saving life from drown- ing acd which has hitherto passed without notice. It may be said that this makes the Bth life John Lowe has rescaed from drowning. Mrs Bridgwa,..er tht-ri briefly spoke on the general advantages of swimming, after which, on the motion of Mr Spens- ley, a vote of thanks was passed, for her presence and kind remarks.</p>
        <p>LAWNMOWERS GROUND AND REPAIR-ED. Green, Mowers always in stock•—J. S. GARLLND, Ironmonger Bridge-street, Warriagton.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>ME EXAMINER, FRIIAY, AUGUST 9 1901.</head>
        <p>ME EXAMINER, FRIIAY, AUGUST 9 1901.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB SPURTS.</p>
        <p>The Northern Counties' Championship.</p>
        <p>The premier local athletic festival is that which is annually held in connection with the Warring- ton Rugby Football Club, and the meeting always attracts a large concourse of spectators. Although the atmospherical conditions were not of the most Wourable description, this year's festival, which tool place on the Wilderspool enclosure on Monday afternoon, was attended with gratifying success. I During the vvhoie of the afternoon the sky was overcast, and there was every prospect of a storm but this did not deter local sporting enthu- siasts from assembling in strong force, and by the time announcd for the sports to commence—two o'clock—fully 5,000 spectators surrounded the field. As usual, the most complete arrangements had been made by the committee and officials to whom every credit is due for the assiduous manner in which they worked to ensure the success ot the festival. The following are the names of the offi- cials:—Clerk of the Course, Mr T Bowers ; Starter, Mr T Wainwright ; Judges, Messrs J Evans, W Dil- lon, and D finuion; Referee, Mr J E Warren; Judge of Boeing Competition, Mr J Davies; Committee, Messrs W Nevins, W Hewitt, T Locker, T Davies, J T Thorniley, F Heesom,T Barnes, J T Bate, W Hill, 1 Hackett; Handicapper, Mr W Platt; Hon Treas- urer, Mr T Pemberton ; Hon Secretary, Mr H Ashton, Mr E Pickavance, N.C.U. A very lengthy programme had been arranged, the most important event being the Northern Counties' Athletic Association Championship which aroused great interest. In the other events there was a good proportion of local compAtitors, several of whom carried off prizes. The bicycle races were especially well contested, and the track being in good condition, few " spills " occurred. The Borough Band was in attendance and enlivened the proceedings with a capital selection of music. It was not until ten minutes to seven that the last event was decided and though the afternoon had been very dull and chills, there had been no rain to mar the enjoyment of the spectators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>TUB 1,000 YAIIDS. N.O C.A. CHAIkEPIONSHIP..</head>
        <p>TUB 1,000 YAIIDS. N.O C.A. CHAIkEPIONSHIP..</p>
        <p>This was the principal event of the festival. Several well-known athletes had entered, but three —Joan Baker, of Manchester, E %V Kenyon, Man- chester, and J L Hultne, Salford—did not put in an appearance. From the start J Roberts, Liverpool, took the lead, being closely followed by G W Ellis, of Leeds, and J T Riaimer, of Liverpool. In the last lap he fell behind, G W Ellis. of Leeds, forging ahead, and securing first honours 'uy six yards, and J T Rimmer, of Liverpool, coming in second. The time recorded was 2min. 23secs. The prizes were gold and silver medals respectively.</p>
        <p>100 YARDS HANDICAP. The opening sprint was run off in 10 heats,there being nearly forty competitors. More than usual interest centred in the event, as J Fish, the well-known War- rington footballer competed in the fourth heat. His appearance on the field was greeted with hearty applause. Fish won his heat in splendid style, gaining the first position by 4 yards. He, however, failed to survive the 'second r.,und. In the float F KHamoson, Lower Broughton (8i) and T Underwood (71) Wigan, ran a dead heat for the first place. On trying conclusions a second time, Hampson, by a supreme effort ran in first, only six inches separating him from Underwood. E Green, Liverpool, (si) gained third place. The time recorded in this race was 10 1-53ec. Winners of heats: T Underwood, Wigan ; E Green, Liverpo-d ; N Wilson, UtEnston ; „I Fish, Warrington ; R Wailing, Widne4 ; J Jones, Liverpool ; A Darlington, Altrinchim, and F K Hampson, Lowe. Broughton. The , prizes awarded were : Ist, gent's emir] centre seconds keyless English chronograph, value £lO ; 2nd, case of fish, game,and meat carvers, value £3 ; 3cdoilver-plated four bottle dinner cruet, value El. 220 YARDS HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Thirty-two competitors had entered for this even', and mcQt of them turned out. They included J Fish, of Warrington, who was in the lead for about ball the dis- tance in the fourth heat. He was, however, overtaken at the corner, and so had not the satisfaction of appear- ing in the final. There was a very tight finish, R Wail- ing, of Widnes (14) breasting the tape first. He was only a few inches in advance of J Cowap, of Acton Bridge (16), a foot separating the latter com- petitor from El W Beneion, Warrington (13.) The time occupied in the final was 23 seconds. Winners of heats:— Jrowsp, Acton Bridge; W Wellings,Prescot; R Wailing, Widnes ; El W Bennion, Warrington ; E Bone, Flixton ; L Green, Warrington ; F R Lyon, Chester; C H Cooper, Altrincham. The following priz-e; were awarded ;—lat, case of cutlery c•rnbination,valu £4 ; 2nd, silver-plated tea urn, value £2 , 3rd, silvet-plated hot water jug, value £l.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ONE _MILE NOVICE BICYCLE HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>ONE _MILE NOVICE BICYCLE HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>The first prize in this event was carried off by a War- rington competitor. A Howard (75), who rode well all through the race. He came in abut two lengths in advance of H Wilson, Manchester (75) ; the same dis- tance separating Wilson from J Watkinson, Earlestown I (65). 2 mins. 54secs. was the tllll recorded. There were five heats, the first and second in etch competing in the final. Winners of heats, A Howard Warrington, W Snailharn./Risley'; J Forster, Wigan ; J Watkinson, Earlestown ; S gay, S: Helens ; C Atkinson. Crews; T Adams, Salford ; and H Wilson, Manchester. The prizes were :-Ist,case of of fish and dessert eaters.value £4 ; 2nd, silver-plated fern pot lamp, value £2 ; 3rd, four cup egg frame, value £l. 880 YARDS HANDICAP. The competition in this event wag not very keen. T purges% Widnes (504 ~illy obtained the first placer 3 F Simmondt. Liverfrool,(ss),being second, and F Baker, Manchester (25) third, Five yards separated Burgess and Sitnmends and the respectable distance of ten yards intervened between Simmonds and Baker. The time- keeper recorded 2 minutes 1 second. The following were the prizes :-1, gent's fitted dressing bag ; 2nd, silver-plated salad bowl and servers ; 3rd, aneroid baro- meter, ONE MILE BICYCLE HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Some capital racing was witnessed In this event. The competitors included an old Warrington favourite, T Astill, who turned out in the opening heat. At the outset be rode in grand form and secured first place in the heat. In the final he led for some time, but was unable to maintain the pace and was overtaken. J Watson' of Warrington (170), rode splendidly through- out and reached home first. being followed three lengths behind„;by E Ainsworth, Manchester t 170). The third position was secured by J Forster, Wigan (185), only half a length aeparatioe him from Ainsworth. The time recorded was 2 min 41 sec Winners of heats ; T Astill. Warrington ; A Efaguley, Cadishead ; W J Balshaw, St Helens ; J Forster, Wigan ; J A Watson, Warrington ; A Howard, Warrington ; E Ainsworth, Manchester ; 3 Brelsford, West Gorton ; C E Monks, Risley; and J Shippam, Mincheste r. The prizes awarded were : Ist, silver-plated tea set and oak mounted tray. value £4 ; 2nd, silver-plated spirit kettle and stand, value £2 ; 3, silver-plated tea pot, value 21. 600 YARDS OBSTACLE RACE. The efforts of the competitors in this race created a great deal of amusement. D Gibson, of Warrington,who was virtually • scratch man, easily CAME in first, S Leach, Rochdale (25), and T Jones, Birken- head (75) being second and third respectively. The winners of heats were-9 Gibson (Warrington). W Johnson (Warrington), A Whittaker (Warrington), 9 Leach (Rochdale), A 1?)rd (Warrington), and T Jones (Birkenhead). The priz s were Ist, marble striking clock and pair of bronze horses ; 2nd, silver-plated six bottle dinner cruet ; 3rd, cage ot meat and game carvers.</p>
        <p>TWO MILES BICYCLE HANDICAP. From the spectators' point of view this event was the mos-, interesting of the afterno-m. There were three heats, and the first and second in each raced in the final. A E Whitehurst. of Manchester, who was heavily handi- capped, made a stiff fight for supremacy in the first heat, but was unable to retain either first or , second position. T &amp;still. of Warrington, was also unable to survive his heat. There was an exciting struggle for victory in the final, C E Monk. of Risley (260)., just gaining premier honours. J A Watson, of Warpngton, (260), put on a brilliant spurt towards the finish and was unlucky In not gettina home first. He Was only a wheel behind Monk, H Wilson, of Manchester, (265) being third. The w inners oft heats were C E Monk, Risky ; A Howard, Warringtoo, A Watson, Warrington ; H Wilson, Manchester; E Ainsworth, Manchester ; and J Brels- ford, West Glrton, 'the prizes were, Ist, 14 day 0rm..,1u mounted clock under shade ; 2nd, 6 cup silver. plated egg frame ; 3rd, silver-mounted claret jag. ONE MILE HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>This race wits not at all keenly contested. A large number of competitors set out at the start, but they dwindled down considerably before the list lap was entered upon. C 1 Straw, of Winwick, (120) ran the whole di.tsnce in remarkably good style, and fir ished up first, 20 yards in advance c' T bee, Widnes, (125) who obtained second position, T Hughes, Widnes, (133) came in third. Toe prizes were :-Ist. 4_ OTh gold albert •, 2nd, £2—Case of breakfast cruet and spoons ; 3rd, £1 —Sdver-plated sardine dish. NINE STONE BOXING COMPETITION. Sewn competitors took part in this event. In the first round James Pennington, Patrieroft, beat G Moole, Northwich ; M Jeffers. Warrington, beat Joseph Fair Clough, St Helens ; Mirk Abley, Brmingbalns beat' James Garrigan, St Helens ; and F Thompson, War- rington, boxed a bye. In the second round Pennington beat Ablev, and Thompson beat J-ffers. Thompson met Pennington in the final but the latter competitor acknowledged himself beaten before the first round had been fought. The prizes were let, silver keyless watch ; 2nd, walnut bracket clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
