      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>FARM LABOURER'S WAGES. --- Local Case at Sandbach.</head>
        <p>A FARM LABOURER'S WAGES. --- Local Case at Sandbach.</p>
        <p>An interesting case to farmers and farm labourers was head at Sandbach Petty Sessions last week, when George Henry Clews, of Fearnhead, farm labourer, claimed from Ernest Sullen, farmer, Brereton, the sum of £4 be Bd for wages. Mr Thomas H Granger, of Warrington, solicitor, represented the plaintiff and Mr Hall, of Manchester, appeared on behalf of the defend- ant.</p>
        <p>Mr Granger stated that about a fortnight before Christmas, 1899, Clews saw the defendant at his farm and entered into a contract with him for twelve months from Christmas, 1899, to Christmas, 1900, at £lO 10s per annum; the work that he was engaged to do being simply to look after the cows. A week before Christmas, 1900, a fresh contract was ?ntered into between the parties to commence at Christmas, the work to be done being of a similar character, but the wages being £l5 per annum. On the Bth June last Clew went home tor a few days and feeling unwell, Dr Bennett, of Padgate, was consulted, who toand that he was suffering from influenza. The plaintiff had since that date been atten- ded by the doctor, who now certified that he was suffering from mitral regurgitation of the heart with irregularity and general debility and that he was quite unfit to follow the occupation of a farm labourer generally including such work as plough- ing and it would in his opinion be injurious to his health to resume such work within the next six months. The plaintiff therefore claimed that he was entitled to the wages for the time that he bad actually worked. George Henry Clews the plaintiff, and Hannah Clews gave evidence bearing out Mr Granger's opening state- ment. Mr Hall for the defendant, maintained that it was a yearly contract that the plaintiff had not proved that he was justified in leaving and was not therefore entitled to the wages for the time that he had actually worked and that the contract was not entered into until the 2nd January, 1901, and it was not necessary that it should be in writing as it was to be completed within the year ant called the defendant and Dr Johnson of Sandbach in support of his case. Mr Granger submitted that on the evidence the con- tract was entered into a week before Christmas 1900, and should therefore have been in writing as a contract for a year commencing at a future day, must be in writing and signed by the parties as required by the Statute of Frauds and althou4h under such circum - stances • servant cannot recover damages for wrongful dismissal and the master cannot recover damages for the servants refusal to continue in his service, the servant can recover proper remuneration for service, performed, and quoted the cases " Brittain v Rossiter " and " Taylor v Garnett " in support of his contention. The magistrates gave judgment for the plantiff for the amount claimed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Week ending Saturday, July 27th, 1901.</p>
        <p>During the week, the following births and death were registered : Warrington Registration District, 44 births, 38 deaths. The excess of births over deaths was 6. The birth rate was equal to 355 per 1,000. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 30.6 per 1,000, against 20.3, the average of the previous four weeks, and against 16 8, the corresponding period of last year. The death-rate from the seven cnief zvmotic diseases was equal to 171 per 1,000, against 2.8, the averageorate ot the previous four weeks, and against 2.4 the corresponding period oi 1900. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. Total 27 6 4 1 38. _ .</p>
        <p>. _ The deaths included-19 from diarrhces, 3 from measles, 1 from tuberculosis, 1 from lung disease, 1 from drowning, 13 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Diphtberia 2 cases, ery stpelas 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital week °riling Saturday, 27th July, 7. Meteorology.—The mean temperature was 63 4 Fah.. and therefore 5 6 deg. lower than preceding week. The maximum temperature 82 F., was recorded on the 21st Inst., and the minimum temperature 56 F., on the 22nd Inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall measured 1.52 inch. The general direc- tion was N.W. J. GUEST GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health, (pro-tern.) Prevalence of Diarrhoea.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer of Health (pro tern) wishes to call the attention of the public, to the large and increasing number of deaths from diarrtasa, occuring at the present time, especially among children under one year of age and to remind parents of the urgent necessity of care in feeding babies, and of adopting such precautions as are contained in the leaflet receatly circulated throughout the Borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>HOMING.</head>
        <p>HOMING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Flying Club.</p>
        <p>_ rhis club flew the second young bird race from Gloucester (107 miles) on Saturday, 35 memberi send. ing 294 birds, which were liberated by Mr Timperley at 11.15 a.m.; wind N, light. *Thomas Mason, 1172; and 1165 ; Elgerley and Cliffe, 1153 ;H Clare. 1153 ; *Stringfellow 1152 ; JackFon and Croulden 1149 ; T Jackson, 1148; Bebbington, 1147; B Clare (2), 1146; Jackson and Goulden, 1142 ; Gallop, 1136; Bate. 1134 ; II Clare. 1132 ; Pollard, 1128 ; pticanig, 1128; Bibby, 1124; Suncnck, 1121; Carnet'. ter 1118 ; Hughes, 1114 ;Bate, 1114 • Sinacock, 1108 ; Jackson ' rson and Gou'den, 1108 ; Greaves. 1107 ; Taylor (3), 1103 ; Gallop, 1102 ; Simcock, 1101 ; Stringfellow, 1098; Walker, 1085 ; Howard, 1084 ; Williams and Taylor, 1084 ; Conk and Shoebridge (2), 1082 ; Craven, 1075; Walker, 1072 ; Craven, 1069 ; Blardrnan and Ames (2). 1061 ; Mather, 1055 ; Paine, 1054 ; Hay 8.05. i 1044 ; Daniels, 1037 ; Leicester. 1030 • Healey, 1017 ; Caldwell, 1003; Raybould and Sons, a 993. *Pool winners. Mason wins Mr II J Longton's 103 6d sof cial. T J.ckson's second telegram verified to advan- tage.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Society. This society flew their fourth younv bird race on Saturday, from Swindon, a distance of 129 miles. Ten members sent 53'sbirds, which were liberated by Mr Ashton. Liverpool and District Fedoration Convoyer, at 10 40 a.m. Weather fine, wind north. The following velocities .were attained :</p>
        <p>Marshall, 1,034, yards per minu`e ; Dyer, 1,029 ; Dvor, 1,015 ; Einward, 973 ; Dunbavin, 945 ; Dunbavin, 925; lwards, 900; Whitby. 883 : Shaw and Son 827; Dean, 684. Blar:hall wins Mr Ruby's menial of a pair of squeikera, and Dunbavin wine Mr T W Thorou- goodrB soecial of a pair of squeakers for best average in young bird raJes.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. R. Smith, writing from Swiss Villa, Docking King's Lynn, states that on Saturday evening a carrier— pigeon settled on his window, sneminelv dead-beat. Its ring hears inscription NUOI J W 315, and the owner may have it by applying to Mr W B Mahon, Hare Hotel. Docking.</p>
        <p>When asking for Cocoa, insist on having CADBURY'S sold only in Packets and Tins, as other Cocoas azeoften imbed tuted for the sake of extra profit—Advt. (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Next Year's Cup Competition. Warrington to Meet Barrow. Result of the Draw. A meeting of the General Committee of the Northern Union was held at the George Hotel, Huddersfield, on Tuesday, Mr J U Houghton, St ,Helens, presiding. Mr P Marsh, Tyldesley, resigned his seat on the committee, it being understood that the Tyldesley club is now de- funct. Mr F Hammersley was elected a member of the committee as representative of the Wigan club. The following were elected officials for the ensuing season :—President. Mr J H Houghton, Si Helens ; vice-president, Mr J Cliff ord, Huddersfield ; hon. treasurer, Mr J Nichol, Halifax ; hon. sec., Mr J Platt, Oldham. The following mernbers of the committee were elected as a Professional Sub-Committee for the ensuing season :—Messrs H H Waller, Brighouse ; J Clifford, Huddersfield ; F Lister, Bradford ; J Platt, Oldham ; J H Houghton, St Helens ; E Gresty, Brough- ton Rangers ; and J Welsh, Stockport. The alterations of the Lancashire Senior Competition byelaws were approved as under:—Delete rule 3, rule 15 to be now 14,cross out 'for winning,club of A teams,"&amp;c Rule 17 to be now 16, to read "that referees and touch judges be appointed by the Competition Committee." Rule 18 to be now 17, "that referees' fees be 12s 6d per match, and third-class railway fare, and for neutral judges the fee be 5s and third.class railway fare." Rule 21, now 20, add "after Yorkshire Senior Competition," "or the Northern Rugby League." Rule 22, now 21, same addition as preceding rule. Rule 23, now 22, cross out "for A team matches," Itc. Rule 24, now 23, cross "this rule," &amp;c. Rule 25 alter t 3 read "The cham- pion club of this competition may eachiseason change places with the bottom club of the Northern Rugby League. The club at the bottom of the table at the end of the season shall retire from the competition, but shall be eligible for re-admission." Rule 26 to read "and shall lodge £5 with the secretary," instead of the com- mittee, and cross out "the decision of the committee in all cases shall be final." The Northern League rules were altered as follows :- 21. Delete the words "or Yorkshire." 22. Delete the words "or Yorkshire." 25. Alter to read at the end of the competition each season the clubs holding the lowest number of points shall retire from the league,and the place of such club shall be taken by the champions of the Lancashire Senior Competition The club retiring from the Northern League shall the following season be included in the Lancashire Senior Competition." The I rules of the North Lancashire and District Northern League and North-Western League were approved. The Lancashire Second Competition complained that Werneth and Leigh Shamfoclis had neglected to pay their subscriptions to that body for last am son. The commit- tee ordered their subscriptions to be paid by the first Monday in October. Hull Marlborough were admitted to memtership9f the Northern Union. A vo`e of thanks was accorded to the president for his services during the past season. Northern Union Cup Draw. First Round, March 15th. Warrington v. Barrow. Altrincham v. Carnforth. Featherstone v. Todmorden. Windhill v. Millons. Kirkstal, v. East Moor. Outwood Church v. Morecambe. Hull v. York. Hull Kingston Rovers v. St. Helens. Radcliffe v. Goole. Maryport v. Oldham: Holbeck v. Alverthorpe. Dewsbury v. Manninghim. Kendal Hornets v. Huddersfield. Aspatria v. Rochdale Hornets. Werneth v. Lancaster. Askam v. Widnes. Penrith United v. Whitehaven Recreation. South Shields v. Bradford. Caetleford v. Hebden Bridge. Idle v. Workington. Heckmondwike v. Birkenhead. Keighley v. Broughton Rangers. Halifax v. Swinton. Otley v. Leeds. Salford v. Pontefract. Stockport v. Liversedge. Batley v. Brighouse Rangers. Wakefield Trinity v. Normanton. Hunslet v. With Brow. Leigh v. Runcorn. Wigan v. Bramley, Ossett v. Seaton. Second Round, March 22nd. A.—Outwood Church or Morecambe v. Halifax or Swinton.</p>
        <p>B.—Ossett or Seaton v Featherstone or Todmorden. C.—Askam or Widnes v. Wakefield Trinity or Nor- lanton. D,—Stockport or Liversedg 3 V. Reighley or Broughton angers. E.—Altrincham or Carnforth V. Batley or Brighouse. K—Werneth or Lancaster v. Hunslet or Wath Brow. G.—Heckmoadwike or Birkenhead v. Holbeck or Alverthorpe. H.—ldle or Workington v. Penrith United or White- haven Recreation. I.—D3wshury or Manniricham v Salford or Pont efract J.—Radcliffe or Goole v Kirkstall or Rastmoor. K.—Castleford or Hebden Bridge v Wigan or Beni- tez. - -</p>
        <p>manton,</p>
        <p>Rangers.</p>
        <p>.L.—Leigh or Runcom v Maryport or Oldham. M.—Windhill or Millom v Otley or Leeds. N.—Hall Kingston Rovers or St. Helens v Hall or York.</p>
        <p>0 —Agnates or Rochdale Hornets v Kendal Hornets or Huddersfield: P.—Warrington or Barrow v South Shields or Brad- ford.</p>
        <p>Third Round, March 29th. Q,—Winner of E v winner of P. R.—Winner of D v winner cf N. B.—Winner of IC v winner of L. T.—Winner of 0 v winner of M. U.—Winner of I v winner of J. V.—Winner of 0 v winner of B. W,—Winner of A v winner of H. X.—Winner of C v winner of F. Fourth Round, April sth. AA.—Winner of Q v winner of S. BB.—Winner of R v winner of W. CC.—Winner of V v winner of X. DD.—Winner of T v winner of U. Boni-flan! Round, April 12.</p>
        <p>EE.—Winner of DD v winner of AA. FF.—Winner of BB v winner of CC. Final to be played on April 22tb. Northern League. A meeting of this League was twin on Tuesday at the George Hotel, Huddersfield,Mr J Clifford (Huddersfield) in the chair. The following permits were granted Woodward, Tyldesley, to play with Leigh • J Prater, Tudhoe, James Ritson, Wallsend, to play with Hull ; E Gledhill, Keighley,and H Norton,Elland Free Wanderers, to play with Brighouse Rangers. The " A "team com- petitions were formed as follows, under the, titles of Lancashire Rugby Ccmbinstion and Yorkshire Rugby Combination. The Lancashire Combination is made up as follows :—Swinton. Salford, Broughton Rangere, Oldham, Warrington, Leigh,Runcorn, Widnes, Rochdale Hornets, Birkenhead. Stockport, Werneth, and Fletcher Russell's ; and the Yorkshire Combination, Bradford, Batley, Brighouse Hawn, Hull, Halifax, Hunslet, Huddersfield, and Todrnorden. The vacancies in the latter combination were left over until the next meeting, when fixtures will be arranged. Lancashire Senior Competition. On Tuesday. at a meeting of the Lancarhire Senior Competition, it was decided to admit Hull Kingston Rovers into that Competition.</p>
        <p>Local Steamer in Collision—Engineer Drowned. On Tuesday the captain of the steamship Hope, belonging to the Manchester and Liverpool Trans- port Company, Bishops Wharf,Warrington reported the loss of the engineez, William Totum, of Den- fling-street, Liverpool. It appears the Hope was proceeding past Eastham Ferry to enter the Ship Canal for Manchester, when the outward-bound Norwegioh steamer Roglan struck into her, and carried away the small boat which was being towed astern. The engineer was knocked overboard by the force of the impact and drowned. The Roglan did not stop to render any assistance. Distribution of Long Service Medals at Lymm. The customary church parade of the members, of H (Lymm) Company 3rd V.B.Cheshire Regiment tock place on Sunday. Headed by the battalion bugle band, the company marched to church from headquarters under the command of Captain J E G Groves and Lieutenant T Ridgway. There was a large congregation at church, and the sermon was preached by the Rev G B Thurston, who took for his text the words " Fight the good fight." After service the company marched to the Drill Hall where they were drawn up in the large square in front of the hall. Captain Groves said it was his pleasing duty to ask Mrs Ridgway to present the medals for long service and good conduct to ex- Sergeant C E Ardern and ex-00rpor513 John Wilkin- eon and Aaron Dixon. They had been in the Volun- teer force over 20 years,and he hoped that many of the younger Volunteers present would follow their example and remain in the force, and by their good conduct earn similar medals. Mrs Ridgway then pinned the medals on the breast of each man,at the same time, in a few gracious words, complimenting them on their long service. She and Captain Groves shook hands with the men amidst the applause of the company. Sergeant Ardern is the youngest Volunteer sergeant in England to receive a long service medal, having joined the Volunteers as a bugler at the age of 13, whilst attending the War- rington Grammar School.</p>
      </div>
