      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>DEATH</head>
        <p>DEATH</p>
        <p>OF THE</p>
        <p>EMPRESS FREDERICK.</p>
        <p>HER LAST HOURS at KRONBERG</p>
        <p>A PEACEFUL END.</p>
        <p>The Empre,s Frederick has found release from her sufferings in death. Her Majesty passed away peacefully at a quarter past six on Monday even- ing, at Kronberg, in the presence of all the mem- bers of her family, with the exception of Prince Henry, who was absent Cu naval duty. Early on Monday morning the German Emperor and Empress, with the Crown Prince, arrived at Kronberg, the Kaiser looking tired and worn. He wore the uniform of that Hussar Regiment of which his mother was titular chief, even in this insignificant partieLlar showing his filial affection. During the forenoon he was seen once or twice seeking fresh air in the gardens of the Schloss, con- versing earnestly with the principal Court func- tionaries present. 17p to the last the Empress was conscious of all that was going on around her, and recognised the Kaiser, but immediately after his entrance into the sick chamber she fell into an uneasy slumber, the action of the heart being very feeble and showing a tendency to decrease. She expressed a wish to see the Rev Cameron Waller, British Chaplain at Hom- burg, who was immediately sent for. Mr Waller remained an hour with the Empress and prayed with her. It was the Empress's express desire that as little as possible should be made known to the outside world of her sufferings. She is reported to have said "The world shall not know what I suffer. I will not be commiserated with in my suffering.", All day long knots of people 'gathered', in front of the Castle eager for news, but save the official bulletin nothing was permitted to leak out. On Saturday evening the body will be transported in the coffin from Friedrichshof to the Protestant church at Kronberg in a torchlight procession. The Coffin will be followed by the Royal Family on foot. On Sunday afternoon, at four o'clock, a funeral service will be held, which will be attended by the family, members of the Emperor's househald, a few friends of her Majesty, and a number of privileged persons. The Royal Family will leave on Sunday evening for Potsdam, and the remains of the Empress will be removed there on Monday evening. On the 13th e funeral service will be held in tho mausoleum in the Friedeng Kirche at Potsdam. As it washer 3fajesty's.express desire that there should be no State ceremony on a large scale this service will not be attended by all the members of the Ger- man Royal and princely houses, and it will be as simple as possible under the circumstances. Six weeks of public mourning has been ordered, and all theatres are to be doted until after the funeral. It is the King's pleasure that the bereavement which the Royal Family has suffered shall not have the effect of cancelling or curtailing the programme for the remainder of the Duke and Duchess of Cornwaa's colonial tour. The King will attend the service at Kronberg on Sunday, and he will be accompanied by Queen Alexandra and the Princess Victoria. It is net decided how long they will stay at Kronberg, but when they leave it will be for the Prussian capital in order to be present at the obsequies of the late Erapre,s. By command of the King a memorial service will be held at the Chapel Royal, St James's Palace,next Tueeday for the late Empress Frederick. Orders have been issued to the army that in con- sequence of the death of the dmpress Frederick no bands are to play or bugles sound until further orders: A supplement to the London" Gazette "of Wed- nesday night contains an order for the Court to go into mourning for the Empress: The Court will change mourning on the 29th inst., and resume the half mourning for the late Queen Victoria on Sep- tember 19. Toe "Gazette "also natifies that officers of the navy and the marines are to wear mourning from Thursday until September 19.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>British Princess in a German Home.</head>
        <p>A British Princess in a German Home.</p>
        <p>By her death, at the age of sixty, says the Lcndon Daily Chronicle," Germany has lost her second Kaiserin, England her Princess Royal, and Europe ono of the mo-t remarkable women of her time. The first-born of her royal parents, Victoria Adelaide Mary Louisa, Princess Royal of Great Britain and Ireland, seemed destined to an illus- trious career, yet it proved to be one of which the lustre, to her at least, was dimmed by sorrows and sufferings that fall to the lot of few ordinary mor- tals. "Those in humbler walks of life," wrote the de- ceased Empress in the preface to a popular life of her most unfortunate Consort, " who are denied many et the blessings enjoyed by the rich,to whose lot fall the so-called good things of this world, are apt to imagine that their burden is the hardest to bear, and struggles, and pain, and the tears are only for them." But the life itself of the writer of these words was a proof that the lot of the lofty is some- times one of far greater misery and misfortune than than of the lowly. As Horace singe, " Pale death knocks withimpartial foot,at the huts of the poor and the palaces of kings "—pale death as well as black care, which latter was rarely absent from its appointed place behind the seat of the Empress Frederick from the time when, as a bright and gifted girl of eighteen, the idol of her royal parents as well as of her husband, she made her state entry into Berlin as the wife of Prince Frederick William of Prussia, until the cay wnen, as the broken- hearted widow of the Froperor Frederick, she suc- cumbed to a malady that had been generated by her grief. Born on November 21, 1840, when her royal parents were each only twenty-one, she herself was destined to enter the wedded state when only a little over seventeen. In addition to ordinary instructors, under the care of Lady Lyttleton, she enjoyed the immense advantage of having her studies directed by her father. When still a girl of sixteen, "Vicky "—the name which is always given to her in the Queen's published letters and journals—translated for him a difficult German pamphlet in such a way as to move the admiration of Lord Clarendon. Courtship and Marriage.</p>
        <p>To Prince Frederick William of Prussia, who was nine years her senior, she had become engaged in 115 c, when in her fifteenth year, though the betrothal Was not divulged before her confirmation. The young Prince " Unser Fritz." was on a visit to Balmoral, arid then revealed his love to the Princess Royal by presenting her, in the course of a mountain walk, with a sprig of white heather ; and more than thirty years later, when he had returned to Deeside in the vain search for the recovery of health among the hills, where he had first found his true happiness, he gave the name of " The White Heather "to a steam launch which a gentleman had asked him to christen—thus showing how his thoughts travelled back to the sunny September day when he bad placed the emblem of his love in the trembling hand of his English bride. Differing from most other engagements of the kind, this waa one of pure love on both sides. " It was not polities," said the Prince, "it Was not ambition ;it was my heart." To his aunt of /WPM&amp; he wrote : " Based on deep-seate affection, toy relations to my passionately loved bride are the true prelude to my future domestic happiness. Her qualities of heart and soul, which are highly deve- loped considering her comparative youth, hastened our union, though it bad to be kept a secret till after her confirmation." The ar ecl ding presents were embarrassingly numerous and costly. Six thousand maidens of England had subscribed for a Bible which, in point of type and binding, was a thing of rare beauty. while King Leopold of Belgium gave a dress worth £2,000, and of so delicate a texture that it could be erclosed in a small ivory card box. The bridegroom's r- rfering was a necklace of thirty-two pearls, the finest of which, large as lisz,l-no's. were declared to be inatchlesß. Loving Two Countries. In the cyrs of her adopted countrymen, thrre was a prejudice • tich the Princess hur:ged to rho end, and I that was a fivr:c partiality lia• everything English. Bhe eevetwouli whrily yield to the temptation to belong to foreign nation, and thcugh Moltke once remarked of</p>
        <p>her that " she has shown how one can love two corm- tries, and be true to both— as she loves mother and husband," it otherwise continued to be 1. sub- .;ect of standing reproach to her at Berl.n that he had never sufficiently merged the Princess Royal of England in the Crown Princess of Prussia. The Princess bad not been long at Berlin—where, by the way, she had been received with great popular en- thusiasm as an unknown new-corner of tender years and high degree—before she made it quite clear to all about her, that she had a will of her own, and was determined to exercise it. She felt galled by the strictness and stiffness of Prussian Court etiquette, and was ill at ease in the midst of an official household of ladies of whom onlyone—her secretary—was allowed to be English. But she refused to subject herself either to the Master of C eremonies, or Co the Oberhofmeisterin, making it c.ear to all that, like her own royal mother in England, she meant to manage her domestic affans in ner own way.,.. Having been accustomed at Windsor, with the rest of her brothers and sisters, to keep her own boxes , tidy and orderly, she continued the custom at Berlin, and when begged by her Oberhofmeisterin to refrain from a practice which was derogatory to the dignity of her new position, she simply replitd by locking all her drawers and putting the keys in her pocket.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Motherhood.</head>
        <p>Motherhood.</p>
        <p>Two days after the first anniversary of her wedding, the Princess, now in her nineteenth year, gave birth to the first of her eight children—four sons and four daughters—the Prince, who is now the third German -‘eror. In 1881 the husband of the Princess btcame Prince on the accession of his father, who had - Regent In 1853. after the mental collapse of his brother Frederick William IV., known in England as "King Clicquot." In combating the fierce opposition to Bismarck's scheme for reorganising the Prussian Arms', which was to be the indispensable instrument of Ger- man unity, King William, on the advice of his Minis- ters, had imposed temporary restrictions on the freedom of the Press—a measure which raisnd a great outcry in Liberal circles ; and this outcry swelled to a still vaster volume when the Crown Prince himsclf, in defia..ce of all Prussian precedents, stood forth st Dantzic and pub- _ ..</p>
        <p>• licly denounced the decrees of his royal father as unjustifiable acts of despotism endangering his own rights of auccessioa to the Crown. There can be little doubt that, in taking such an audacious course—which produced a bitter breach between him and his father, and very nearly had the effect of consigning him to prison at Kiistrin—the Crown Prince found the strongest encouragement, if not, iudsed, initiation, in his English wife.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>First Shadow of Sorrow.</head>
        <p>The First Shadow of Sorrow.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the estrangement of this period might have been avoided had the Prince Consort been still alive to guide his daughter. But he had died a few years before, and his Las was the first of those sorrows that were to mark the path of the Crown Princess to the end of her days—sorrows, which included the death of her third son Sigismund from diphtheria during th 3 absence of his father in the Bohemian campaign ; the loss of her fourth boy, Waldemar, from the same cause while his mother's heart was still inconsolable for the death of Princess Alice of Hesse-Darmstadt; and last,and greatest of all, the tragic end of her own beloved husband just after reaching the threshold of that high career for which he and she had waited so long.</p>
        <p>It was from art, more perhaps than from religion, that the Crown Princess, hke her father, sought tied found relief from the woes and worries of her lite. Both as a painter and as a sculptor she achieved so high a degree of excellence as to surprise the recognised masters ot those arts. So long ago as 1860 the Berlin Academy elected her as a member, aad the Art Gallery there, which has now developed into an imposing building, owes its existence and prosperity to her influence and efforts.</p>
        <p>A Struggle against Militarism. Devoted to art of all kinde, she was also a great reader of history, as well as a close student of contem- porary affaire, and in order, above all things, to keep herself au courant with tbe course of events is England shelonce commissioned Mr (afterwards Sir) E Grant- Duff to write her a weekly letter on the politics of her native country—from the Liberal point of view. The politics of Germany she attended to herself, alai even dipped into revolutionary literature to try to make out what the Socialists really wanted. A courtier once expressed his surprise at hearing that the Princess had been reading some speeches by the Socialist deputies Bebel and Hasemilaver. "Oh," she replied, with a bright laugh, " I suppose one may fish for pearls in any waters,but I haven't found any there." The wife of a soldier, whose natural distaste for soldieting pre- vented him from ever becoming a great general—as was truly said of him by his friend Frey tag, the nove- list, the Crown P.incess shared her husband's aversion to militarism, being of the opinion that arts could do more for a nation than arms, in reversal of the maxim, " Magismarte quam arte." Moreover, this natural dis- like of hers to militarism may have been intensified by the knowledge that in a warlike country litre Prussia princes and men were held in much higher account than princesses and women, and that consequ(ntly domestic life was less value.] there than in England. Hence Ai followed that the life of the Crown Princess at Berlin may be almost said to have been a p.2rpetual struggle for the assertion and recognition of the rights and privileges of woman as accorded to her in England —a very different thing from the " Frauenzimmer- politik," or petticoat government, against whims Bis- marck always protested as being most pernicious to the interests of the Prussian State. It also pained the Crown Princess to find that domestic life was less valued in Germany than in England ; and that the Germans might have no cause to regret the wart of a good example in this respect, she herself regulated her own household in .strict accordance with the English model, so that her palace really came to be a kind of enclave of her own native country ; and to such an extent was this anglification of things carried that, when at last the Crown Prince came to the throne as " Friedrich der dritte "—Frederick M.—h was. dubted " Friedrich der Britte." or Frederick the i3iiton by the wanton wits of Berlin. Bismarck Ones com- plained that when at home with her daug,hters the German Empress Frederick only spoke English, ' ss the language of the chosen people," and that her daughters .wrote English letters to their father. It must certainly his owned that the Crown Princess was somf-times a little agkrtsAve with her Anglominia. as when, for example, she once remarked exultingly to Bismarck that " three merchant families in Liwrpool could boast of more silver plate than the entire Prussian nobility." " Yee," retorted Bismarck, " that is</p>
        <p>possibly true, your Royal Highness, but we value our- selves in Prussia for other things besides silver?' On another occasion Bismarck said of the Crown Princess : —" She is a clever woman ; clever in a womanly way. She is not able to disguise her feelings, or at least not always. I have cost her many tears' and she could not conceal how angry she was alter the annexation (of the Elbe Duchies and Hanover): She could hardly bear the sight of me, but that feeling has now (in IVO) somewhat subsided. She once asked me to bring her a glass of water, and as I handed it to her she said to a lady in waiting. "He has cost me as many tears as there is water in this glass."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>' Emieror, Crown P become</head>
        <p>' Emieror, Crown P become</p>
        <p>Samaritan Services.</p>
        <p>From those tears the Crown Princess always found readiest relief in relievirg the sorrows and sufferings o others, and, above all things, in furthering the welfare, I while elevating the statue, of her own sex. " I have always," she said " kept in view the moral and intellectual education of women,the advance of hygienic domestic arrangements, and I have endeavoured to increase the prosperity of women by opening to them fields for gaining their livelihood." But nobler still were the Samaritan services which the Crown Princess ren- dered to the suff-ring and the sick. To her is due the foundation Cm n 1881) of the Victorian House and Nursing School—of which the first iiea had occurred to her in 1867 after the war of the preceding year had shown how insufficiently Germany was provided with nursing agencies—a school a hich was richly endowed by her and her husband out of the handsome sum sub- scribed throughout Germany and presented to them in 18E3 on the oca.sion of their silver wedding—an occa- sion which was otherwise marked by the erection of an English Church—St George's—at Berlin, in which, as it is needless to say, the Crown Princess became a frequent worshipper. Her heal t was always "over the water," and she was fond of drawing comparisons between the disadvantages of life in Prussia and in her own island- home. "AIL" she env said, or rather sighed, to a Scotsman who wag presented to her at the' British Ern. bassy, "I wish we couid only have the hills of Scotland here in Brandenburg!' Never before had the Crown Princess shone forth so much as an angel of gocdness and mercy as during the Franco-German war, when she established herself at</p>
        <p>mburg so as to tend the wounded and at the same time be neater her husband, who used to write to I]..r daily from the front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Family Differences.</head>
        <p>Family Differences.</p>
        <p>The Princess's chronic feud with the Bismarc'is was nothing to her temporary estrangement from her own eldest sou, on the deveh.pment of whose character she bad alwass bestowed so much anxious maternal care. The mother had imbued this eon with all her own strength of character and force of will, arc] the result was cotflict as bitter as that of the fabled crop of dravon's teeth. Polsibly the mother may not have always acted with perfect tact, but it is at least certain that on the tragic death of her consort, she was treated by her sLn with great lack of filial tenderness an,l respect. The world cried out against the son, who cpenly ranged himself on the side of his mother's enemies; who backed up Bismarck, tooth and nail, in his success- inl opposition to his mother's scheme far wedding the ex-Prince Alexander of Bulgaria to her second daughter Princess Vic. oria ; who never lifted a finger, far less uttered.a word, to restrain the Berlin Press from calum• niating the parent wbo bore him in connection with the medical cobtroversies wh.cla raged round the death- loe,d of his father ; who sealed up the New Place at Potsdam with a military cordon the very moment the breath had left his father's body in order to obviate the possibility of his mother removing the diaries and (ther manuscript, of her ill-starred cmsort ; and who other- wise behaved as if he believed all the crud slanders of which she now became the object.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>What Might Have Been.</head>
        <p>What Might Have Been.</p>
        <p>- - - What lines would have be, n pursued by Germany bad the Emperor Frederick lived to control hen l destiny controlled as he himself over was by his English wife, it were bard to say, but the Emperor himself at least had solemnly promised Bismarck that he would with- draw himself from all that foreisn fluence " which even Duke Ernest of Cburg, the uncle of the Empress, complained of in an anonymous pamphlet which he wrote and issin d, entitled : •• Foreign Hands a,hl Co-Regents in Germany." That this Emperor—who, by the way, was never physically strong, in spite of appearances, had to descend to the tomb after a tragic reign of only nine and ninety days; and that his high-minded and accom- Wished consort—who had ea long looked forward to the time when she would be able at his side to shed an addi- tional lustre oo the G-rman lb• one—was then compelled to abandon all her lofty hopes, in circumstances of such peculiar cruelty, was a fate bitter and bard enough to break the braysst neart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[BY A CoRRESPONDENT.] CRICIOET.</p>
        <p>ftn; ifeosr NsBew t Storeroo , their score Yewtorkle- Willows had a visit on Saturday from Eccles to play cif the return fixture. The result was the bwaki sn ga rleBms, a raksa bl againye easy win w chief scorer for Newton, his total being 50. Turton made 37 and Hujit, 22, Eccles fell to pieces, the bowling of Huish, Rylaoce, and Hindley being de- structive.</p>
        <p>The seccni teams met at Eccles, bat Newton sofforeJ defeat. The /scores were, Newton 65, Eccles SS. Unfortunately Wilkinson had to retire From the field owing to an injury, and no doubt this had an effect upon the game.</p>
        <p>Cm Bank Holiday, Newton played Garswood and had to suffer a defeat. Garswood scored 209 aa against Newton 103 stewart for Garewood was a mignty hitter, en 1 en 100, and in the bowling department his share in the attack was of the greatest assistance to his side. For Newton, Hindley made 45, and Harrison was the only other player who made double figures.</p>
        <p>Eariestown visited Whalley Range, on Saturday but were unahlP to prevail aga.lrl6t them. he only compiled 77 as against Whalley Rang,, Alderson was ab,ent from he Earlestown team,and this would affect the at! ack, still. Earleiowo tare been consistently on the losing tide.</p>
