      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, AUGUST 9, 1901 LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, AUGUST 9, 1901 LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>Indian Outpost Surprised.</p>
        <p>Seven Men Killed.</p>
        <p>Rifles and Ammunition Surrendered.</p>
        <p>SIMLA, WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Oa the 6,h inst. a gang of 200 Mahsuds attacked a military post at Kashmar liar and killed the sentry. The garrison was completely surprised and took refuge in the barracks. The Mahsuds demanded their rifles and threatened to set fire to the post. The officer commanding the garrison, seeing that resistance was hopeless, sur- rendered the rifles. The Mahsuds then locked the garrison up in the barracks, looted the post and decamped.</p>
        <p>Oae havildar, three sepoys, and three labourers were killed, white the oaler com.nanding and three wounded. I large quantity of ammuni- tion, thirty rifles,all the kits,accoutrements,rations, and uniforms were Carried off. The Mahsuds sustained no loss.</p>
        <p>the Oceanic in Collision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Sinks a Waterford Steamer.</head>
        <p>Sinks a Waterford Steamer.</p>
        <p>Seven Men Drowned.</p>
        <p>The White Star line steamer Oceanic, which left Liverpool on Wednesday for New York, reported on arrival at Queenstown that she bad been in col- lision in the Irish Channel with the steamer Kin- cora, which sank. Seven oi the Kiucora's mew were drowned. The Bincora trades b3tweea Liverpool, Weetfird, and Limerick.</p>
        <p>The Bincora hat a crew of fourteen on board,and unfortunately, seven ,sf these were drowned. The other seven were brought to Queenstown by the Oceanic, which has her bsw damaged. It is gat( d that the collision occurred during a fog. The White Star Company state that the Oceanic was very little damaged, and has proceeded on her voyage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Collapse of a Racecourse</head>
        <p>Collapse of a Racecourse</p>
        <p>Stand.</p>
        <p>Just as the flve horses were on their way to the post for the St James's Park Handicap at Paisley, the smallest stand which was crowded, collapsed, and many persons were injured. The crowd at the opposite side of the course rushed over, and a wild scene of disorder followed. While fortunately not resulting in any death the collapse caused many injuries, sad several persons were conveyed ED the infirmary. The coJrse was eventually cleared, and It was ultimately decided to proceed with the rack g.</p>
        <p>Yesterday's Cricket. Close of Play Scores. LANCASHIRE V. DERBYSHIRE :—Plav aban- doned for the day. YORKSHIRE V. HAMPSHlRE:—Hacopshire, 204; Yorkshire, 136 for 2. ESSEX v. NOT rs:—Notts, 460 all out. GLOUCESTERSHIRE v. MIDDLESEX :—Glou- cestershire, 229. Middlesex, 42 fru 5. LEICESTERSHIRE v. WORCESTERSHIRE : Leicestershire. 272, Worcestershire, 64 for 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>British Troops Captured.</p>
        <p>The following was iseuei from the War Office on Tburs dayevening From Lord Kitchener to Secretary of State for War.</p>
        <p>Residency, Pretoria, August 8,11 a.m</p>
        <p>A post of 25 men uf Steinacker's Horse, on the Sabi River have been surprised and captured by Boers.</p>
        <p>Forty Boers Captured.</p>
        <p>PRETORIA, WEDNESDAY, Colonel Spens his had a success in the Eleilbron district, and captured forty Boers. General Baden-Powell at the Colonial Office.</p>
        <p>Major-General Baden-Powell visited the office of the Crown Agents for the Colonies on Thursdav afternoon, and later had an interview with Mr. Chamberlain at the Colonial Office. The hero 3f Mafe%ing looked somewhat improved in health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>General French's Operations.</head>
        <p>General French's Operations.</p>
        <p>Enemy Feeling Effects.</p>
        <p>"Times" telegram dated Graaf Reinet, Sugust 5, say:— According to all reports, the viour wjh which General French's operations in Cape C lony are carried on is beginning to tell on the enemy, who in many iastances are short of arms and ammunition, and by no means appreciate being hustled by our columns. N) decisive engagement can be expected.</p>
        <p>In the Colony here, the frequent record of skirmishes, with the losses inflicto I on the enemy must be regarded as a sign that Rood work is being done. Cplonel Hunter-Weston's c )lum^, which left Middl turg last week on s march southward over difficult country came ire touch with the Boers, of whom two wore killed and 901/13 others wounded.</p>
        <p>A smsll force of the enemy undAr L )tter entered Bethesda Road from the west oa Monday, before proceeding eastwards.</p>
        <p>nuche. with a commando which has been in the north-west district, has crossed Orange River to the north.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Presentation of Medals to Warring-</head>
        <p>Presentation of Medals to Warring-</p>
        <p>ton Yeomen.</p>
        <p>At the Bostock Hall Fe e, no Monday, medals (given by the town of Ncrthwich) were presented to the returned men of the 22nd Company Northwich Contin- gent of the Imperial Yeomanry, as a memento of their valiant services in South Africa. Amongst the recipients were several Warrington men, namely .—Bugler Thurgood, PrivateaJ A Prinsepp, A Ai•hton and T Baxter, the medals are of brot z,and enclosed in a case. On one side appears the recipient's name, the arms of Northwich, and the words " Presented by the town of Northwich for meritorious service in South Africa, 1900-1, 22nd Cheshire Company Imperial Yeomanry ' Volunteers," whilst Gn the reverse side is a mounted Yeoman, and an infantry soldier standing at attention. I</p>
        <p>EXA MIN ER PRINTING OFFICE. BEWSEY-STREET, WARRINGTON. AITCTION SM BILLS AND CATALOGIJISS. EXCURSION POSTERS AND HANDBILLS.</p>
        <p>may be had in a towboat%</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>PRINTING OF ALL DESCRIPTIONS</head>
        <p>PRINTING OF ALL DESCRIPTIONS</p>
        <p>ittters VI the Obttor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresBe3 to the Editor.</p>
        <p>Matters intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper aud sent in, if possible not tau r than Thursday. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one. We cannot under ordinAry circumstances unthr- take to rcturn the manuscripts which are not used.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Liberal Aggression.</head>
        <p>Liberal Aggression.</p>
        <p>Sir.—ls it not time the Liberal party exerted itself and cast itself into line of ac'ion ? The present-Jay government affords many weak places which an experi- enced opponent could make party capital of. The gnvernment could very well sing " The mistakes of my life have been many." and it would go well too. It i. a hundred pities that just when the " iron is hot " there shciald be no one to strike hard. Mr Arquith and Sir fl C Bannerman should tour the country and arou:e enthusiasm for the Liberal cause and thus demons:rate to an electoral community the practicability of an active opposition. The War Office for instance should be smitten hip and thigh, and ottisr parts of this" govern- ment of relations " will stand equal investigation and Equal condemna'ion. A Mr Jones speaking at Leigh the other day spoke of Warrington as being " regarded generally as a safe Conservative seat." If organisation and activity were only in evidence Warrington would deme.nstrate that there are surprises elsewhere beside the Transvaal.—Yours, &amp;a.</p>
        <p>EN AVANr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Preservation of part of the Shore of</head>
        <p>Preservation of part of the Shore of</p>
        <p>Derwentwater,</p>
        <p>• Sir.—Many of your readers will be starting f -sr their summPr holidays—some will be coming to the English Lake District, others will be off to Scotch Moor, Wel.h Valley, or British Coast. If it chance that at any timo they feel the beauty and the freedom of foot la SIIMP entrancing scene quicken their pulses and bring the smile to their lips,will the's? think of the possibility they have of securing just such Ves.ure for tens of th -u- -sands of their fellow countrymen in the generatiocs yet to be, by sending a subscripAon or piomise of subscrip- tion towards the purchase of a mile of the western shore of Derwentwater, for which the h ti I Trust t— is now raising the nf cessary 'funds ? We have only a limited time in which the purchaPe- , money (£6,500) can be collected and we need £2,500 still. Is there anyone who cculd wish to perpetuate tne memory of some dear friend by a gift of an acre of the 103 acres we are purchasin ? If so, the way is open. We spend large sums in costly and perishable mono- , merits to the dead, here on the shore of Derwentwater the gift of £6O would purchase a memorial whose , blessing and who charm for toe weary worker on his too short holiday would be perpetual. We have committees at work in Leeds, in Manchester, in Liverpool, in Birmingham, and in li,swick, and nothing has touched me more than letters enclosing 2s or 2s 61 from bona fide working men in our great northern cities, who say, " I had a day's outing on Der- wentwater five years ago, and in memory of it I send you a small subscription, and I will ask my mates and nee what I can do." One man sent £1 with this short note, "I am blind and lam dying, but I once saw Der- wentwater many years ago, and I beg to send you a sovereign for the good objeet." It is for the blind and the dying we appeal. Men who live in the half-darkness of city smoke, and whose souls are withering for want , of communion with that spirit of Nature whose glory is revealed by just such a shore as the shore of Derwent- water. We ask all holiday makers in this August season who feel the good of being where hills and woods and lakes can speak in their unfailing language to the heart, to aid us who are resolved to keep this bit a lakeland shore in its loveliness and calm for the rest and refresh- ment of far-off generations. Since writing the above, I learn from the "Spectator" that a kind friend, who signs himself " Stroller," has offered £lOO on condition that nine others will each give a similar amount. I trust this generous challenge will be accepted. . . _</p>
        <p>Sub&lt;Criptioos should be sent to rnyself,or Miss Oo'avia Hill, 120, Marylebone-road, London, or to the SecretarY. National Trust, 1, Great College-street, Westminster. El. D. RAWNSLEY. Crosthwaite Vicarage, Keswick, Aug. 7th, 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY AUTHORITY. At the Warrington Conn, y Petty Seseitns ota Wednesday, George Sturgess,licensee of the" Hand and Banner," Newton, was granted temporary authority to sell in a tent at the Newton Flower Show on August 24th. BARLBSTOWN TECHNICAL INSTILIIICTION CLASSES. ' The following results of the examination in Machine Construction held in connection with the above classes, have come to hand:—H P Bramah, T C Higgitv, Joseph Rice, A, Ist class. Thomas Benison, A 2nd dabs. W Bowmaker, E Canliffe, R A Eaton, Henry Sweetloye, IS Ist claLs. C Goff, W Herring, L Jones. Henry Large, J E Phail, Thorns itchie, John Wright, 14 2nd class. _ _</p>
        <p>i and</p>
        <p>_ THE EARLESTOwN ANNUAL HOLIDAYS. Next week, the Aunual Works Holidays will take place, and there will be a general cessation of busi- ness. The arrangement arrived at last year was that all the works should close for four days in the third week of August. This year, Messrs T and r Vicars have broken away from the arrangemeno, and will close their premises to-night (Friday) for the whole of next week. The Viaduct Works, The Vulcan Foundry, and Messrs Slee and Co, abide by the arrangement. and will close their establish- merits on Tuesday evening for the remainder of the week. Some of the tradespeople have also come to an understandiag and purpose closing their premises on Thursday, for the day. No doubt in coarse of time, all the tradesmen will fall into line and for one day at least, there will be an entire cessation of business. The employes at the Via- duct Works, having the privilege of travelling at iquarter fare, need not trouble themselves about ex- cursions or cheap fares., but the men at the other works, and their families, will have to depend upon the generosity of the Railway Company in afford- ing facilities for reaching places of interest and popularity at rates cheaper than the ordinary fares. Providing the weather is fine, there will doubtless ba a large exodus of holiday makers. EsaLssTowN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. The 163rd quarterly meeting of this Society Nis held in the co-oper itive Rail, Earlestown on Tues- day evening. Mr D Forrester presided, and there was a large attendance of members. The minutes of the last quartaily meeting having been read and confirmed, the statement of accounts for the . quarter were submitted, showing the cash receipts to be £10,735 2s -lid being a decrease on last q.larter of .£684 lls 7i, but an increase of £1,156 7s NI on the corresponding period last year. The net profits realised were £1,623 Os 2, which the committee prepared to dispose of as follows:—To pay a dividend 9f 3s in the £ to mem- bers. absorbing £1.580 83, the balance of £42 123 20 being added to the Contingent fund, During the quarter 50 new memoers had been admitte I, and '27 had withdrawn, leaving 1,703 on the register,of whom 1.250 had brought in cheeks.— Messrs G Goff and W Clare were re-elected to the Committee of Sfaaagetnent.—Vr Gooding was re-appointed auditor T Swift submitted his report as delegate to quarterly meeting of the Ca-operative Wholesale Society,—After a short dis- cussion the fees of the auditors were increased to .E 2 10s per quarter each. It was decided to issue an invitation for the holding of the District Confer- ence at Earlestown.—This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Sr. HELENS CORPORATION AND MUNICIPALISED TELE.</p>
        <p>PIIONES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the St Helens Parliamentary Co-n- -mittee. a letter was read from Messrs Beariett and Ward Thomas with respect to the desirability either of applying for a local. municipal telephone exchange license or of encouraging a competitive company to put down and work the neces.ary plant. The committee rea..lcud to app-iint a sub-committee to consider the matter and report •hereon. MORE PUBLIC Paaxs FOR Sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The town of St. lielens,which already has within ealy reach of its centre fine public p%rks at Cowley Hill, St. Am.'s, and Tbatto Heath, and a splendid extensive recta .tion ground at B.oundary -road, will shortly add materially to its possessions in that respect. The CDT- poratioa parliamentary and lands committee have had under consideration the desirability of acquiring land from Captain Michael Hughes for the purposes of a park aud recreation .ground at Sutton, and have re- cqmmended that a strip of land about 20 acres in extent bounded on the south side by the Sutton Cricket Cub's Ground, on the west by Marshall's Cross-road, on the east by New-street, and .on the north by agricultural land, he lesB 1d from Captain Hughes on terms discussed in con spondence between that gentleman and the , [own clerk, with the op,ion of purchase later on. . ' Toe committee have rats() visited and inspected the Garkell Park and he Parr It.creation Ground, both of which are now under arrangement, and instructima have been given to the borough enginter on vari..us matters affecting their laying out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>BATHING FATALITY AT WARRINGTON. Courageous Conduct of a Boy and Workman.</head>
        <p>THE BATHING FATALITY AT WARRINGTON. Courageous Conduct of a Boy and Workman.</p>
        <p>Mr S Brighouse, Countv Coroner, cniuct,d an in- quest at toe Warrington Police Station on Saturday morning, relative to the death of George Evans, sged eleven years, son of George Evans, a forgeman residing at 7, Plumpton-street, who was drowned in the Sankey and St. Helena Canal on Thursday of last week. The first witness was Clara Evans, the mother of the deceased, who said she last saw her son alive at three o'clock ,m Thursday afternoon 9 when he went out to play. About five o'clock she was told that he had been drowned in the canal. She had never before known him to bathe in the canal. She had repeatedly warned him not to .to so.</p>
        <p>William Chumley, 13 years of age, residing at 94, Edmund-street, said that at 4.45 on Thursday afte:noon he saw the deceased bathing in the canal.. He was trying to swim across, but seemed to be seized with cramp, and commenced struggling. Witness eadeav cured to rescue him. He jumped into the water withou divesting himself of his clothing and got hold of diceased's foot. He was forced to relinquish his grip, as deceased was pulling him und( r. There were three I others bathing in the canal at the time. In reply to the Coroner witness stated that he could swim, his father having taught him. The Coroner remarked that if all fathers could teach their children to swim, it would be a very good thing. He complimented Chumley upon his couragrous action. P.C. Woods informed the jury that at five o'clock he received information that a boy had been drowned in the canal. He at once proceeded there and after grappling a short time, reecovered the body. At the place in question the canal was ab ,ut 15 yards wide and 7 feet deep. Several persons stated that Chumley had tried to rescue the deceased. A man , named William Melling, employed at Messrs Houghton's wireworks and living at 12, SutLon-street, came upon the scene after the boy had suuk and without divesting himself of any clothing dived into the canal twice but did not succeed in finding the boy. The Coroner thought the conduct of Chumley and Melling ca as deserving of recognition. He shi uld take steps Co have it brought under the notice of the Royal Humane Society. The jury returned a verdict to the effect that the deceased was accidentally drowned. The Coroner again complimented Chumley and awarded him 5s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>DAHLIA QUEEN FESTIVAL AT</head>
        <p>DAHLIA QUEEN FESTIVAL AT</p>
        <p>BURTON WOOD.</p>
        <p>The annual festival in connection with the crowning of the Dahlia Queen took place on Monday at Burton- wood in dull cold weather. In many respeets tho cere- mony is synonymous with the successiul May Queen festival which is annually held at Golborne. A gigantic procession consisting chiefly of children representing all kinds of pretty and grow, gue characters was fund in the field near the Bridge lan, and headed by a bsnd paraded the country lanes, to a field near Collins Gieen St: tion, which had been enclosed by canvas. Upwards of 1,000 children and adults took part in the processir n which included—Flower girls,Nurses,Red Riding Roods, Boy l;lue and Bo Peep, Ally Sloper, and many ccmic char .cters. The Elaydock brass band discoursed numerous selections on the way, the varied costumes, with the numerous banners giving a pretty effect to the gathering.</p>
        <p>- - On arrival- at the field the ft stivities incidental to the Coronation, were procecdod with : such as Dah,ia pole dancing, drilling by Highland Soldiers, &amp;a. The Queen who was conveyrd to the ground in a carriage drawn by a pair of grey horses was Miss Lily Addism of the Pear Tree Hotel, Collins Green, and she was era wned.by Miss Mary Forshaw of Burtonwood, who presented her with a silk sunshade. Prizes were offered to those who had lent their horses and conveyances hr the purposes of the procession, and the judging resulted s follows : GENTLEMAN'S TURNOUT : —l' t prize, Dr Bates, St Helens; 20d, J Miller, Eirlestown.</p>
        <p>The special priza of a set of brasses, given by Mr For- shaw for the cleanest horse on the field was won by Mr Geo Anders, Buttonwood. TRADESMEN'S Tounsour :—lgt pr.v, R F.,rshaw Burtonwood (carter' Unsworth), 2nd pr z, Forshaw ; 3rd priz., Haydock Local Board.</p>
        <p>, -.-• HEAVY:TURNOUT :-13t pr.z., John Fairclough, White- house Farm; 2nd R Fresco', Burtonwood. A good programme of ep3rt9 was afterwards gone through. The officials for the pedestrian amusements were : Judges.—Messrs Charlton, Naylor and Edwards. Starter, J Wightman ; handicappers, J Wigbtman and E Davies; matkaman, E Davirs. The following were the events and the results : , BOYS Rica (under 18 yearn oi.)) :—First heat, 1, J H ll.asworth (Warrlfigtnri) ; 2, W H Smith (Sutton). Second heat, 1, G 13 tir4ll-hs (Stubshaw Cress) ; 2, Thomas Porter (Leigh). Toird heat, 1. W Morgan (Earlestown); 2, G F flaolts (Parr). Final, 1, G H Griffiths; 2, W Morgan; 3, G F Hanks. 100 YARDS FLAT RACE HANDICAP :—First heat, 1, J Qiirin (Earlestown) ; second heat, Ii Whittle (Esrles- town) •' third heat. J Green (Ear!eßtown) ; fourth heat, J 11 Whitlow (Earlestown); fifth heat, J Barrow (Earlestown) ; Final 1, Whittle ; 2, Whitlow ,• 3, Quinn. 100 YARDS THREE-LEGGED RACE :-1, V hiticw and Eaves; 2, Scott and Stephens; 3. Green and Leyland. 80 YARDS 14-STONE RACE :-1, E Scott (Eeriest divn) ; 2, J Leyland ; 3, J Worsley. 100 YARDS Secs RACE *-2, T Porter (Leigh) ; 2, J C Proctor (et Hek us); 3, A Wilding (Burtonwood). 440 YARDS FLAT RACE :-1. G Forbes (St Helens) • 2, J H Whitlow (Earlestown); 3. J .ms Jump (VVinvricit). ONE MILE FLAT RACE :-1, G F Hanks (Parr) ; 2, J F Flynn (Earlestown) ; 3,J Adam 3 (Earlestown). 'Pm F WAR :-1, S Bitcon's team ; 2, W Culshaw's team.</p>
        <p>The evening was afterwards spent in dancing, and there was display of fireworks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>GRAVE CHARGES AGAINST A WIDNES OFFICIAL.</head>
        <p>THE GRAVE CHARGES AGAINST A WIDNES OFFICIAL.</p>
        <p>Sentence at the Assizes.</p>
        <p>At the Uverpoll Ass'zes on Friday last week, before Mr Justice Ridley. Jrho Drury, aged twenty-nine years, bo keeper, was indicted for having at Widnes,b-tween March 29th and April 9th, 1901, being a servant of the Mayor, alderman, and burgesses of the borough of Widnes, feloniously and frandu'ently emb...zzled several sums of money delivered to him on account of his em- pro) era. He was further charged with having, between the dates already mentioned, falsified certain books, the property of his employers, with intent to defraud. Mr Taylor, KC, and Mr Rigby Swift prosecuted, and Mr Tobin defended. Prisoner pleaded guilty to the second indietment of falsifying the bo:ks and denied the other charge. Mr Taylor stated that the prisoner had be n in the service of the Widn's Cotcoration as an assistant collector of rates, his duty being to duly give roc ipts for all sums received by him. As far as could be ascer- tained, the falsification of accrunts had been going on flr about twelve months. Prisoner had made our what purported obe a full statement of all that had been done.</p>
        <p>Mr Tubin,on the prisoner's behalf,pointrd out that he had had the handling of large sums of money,s mething like -£50,000 passing through his handa,whiht his salary W 45 only £ll5. He had every opportunity of com- mitting frauds, and with his small allowance was un- able to resist the temptation placed in his way. There was no suggestion that he drank or was Riven to ex- travagant living. A point which counsel urged in prisoner's favour was that another official of the Cor- poration—though in a superior position to the prisoner —had got !him to go securty for him for borrowed money,and when a demand.was made upon the prisoner he was unable to meet it out of his own pocket,and had recourse to the mot,ey of his employers. All this time there was a project on foot to present the official in question with a money testimonial in consideration of his years of service with the Corporation, and prisoner was relying upon this official being thus placed in a position to refund him the money with which be had met the demand. Unfortunately, however for certain reasons, the proposed testimonial suddenly dropped,and prisoner was thus deprived of any means of repayment. He (counsel) hoped his lordship would take a lenient view of the offence, more particularly as he had given the prosecution every assistance, and had a wife and child dependent on him. Tbe Judge accepted the suggestion that prisonnr had been placed in a position of temptation, whirl, he had failed to resist. The fact that his salary ha o been a small one was, however,no justification for what he had done. The case was a serious one, as prisoner was a trusted servant of the Corporation, and he could not do less than pass a sentence of eight calendar months' imprisonment with hard It hour. In regard to the charge of embezzlement the prosecu- tion offered no evidence.</p>
        <p>Swimming. Before a large crowd, at Leicester, on Tuesday, the hr! -mile A.S.A. championship was decided. The corn- ptitors sa ere .1 A Jarvis, Leicester (holder) ; W A Curwen, Bootle ; D Billington, Bacup ; F Kearsley, Wigan ; and G E Sharp, Leicester. Alter a level start giiiington was the first to show in front, but after going 100 yards Jarvis took the lead, and at the turn was fire yards ahead of Billington. This pair drew away from the others, and Jarvis completed the half distance in 6min., 9 2 ssec., the Bacup man taking Bsec. longer. In the rest stretch Billington made a big effort and drew up somewhat, out Jarvis was swimming well within himself. A hundred yards from home Billiugton made another great, but fut Is, attempt to get on teams, and Jarvis finished ten yards to the good. Time :—Jsrvis, 12inin. 42 2-ssec. ; Billington, 12min. 23 2-sstc ; and Sharp, 13odin. 32iee.</p>
      </div>
