        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAP.O. IT is surprising how many things eome people de lot learn from experience. Tire mills. of the gods grind slowly, as a rule, but they sometimes get a /nave on that paralyzes some of us. IT is a singular fact that the man who knows it all usually disagrees wit% the other man who knows it all. A MONOPOLY is a good deal like by a baby. A man is opposed to it on general princi le' until he has one ot his own. OFTEN THE CASE.—" The minister ,eeighs his words very carefully." "Still, he doesn't seem to realise how heavy his sermons are." AFTER THE ESTRANGEMENT.—Hobson : " you and Chatterton speak nowadays ?" Dobson: " He does—l listen." " Wnv, I'd like to know," said a lady to a judge, " cannot a woman become a successful lawyer?" "Because she's too fond of giving her opinion without pay," answered the judge. "Our of work again, Pat? I thought that old Skinflint gave you a job?" "He did, sor ; but )''ll be kilt afore starve to death for the sake f kapin' aloive, soy." " GEORGIE, I'm glad to see that you are polite al offer sister the oranges first."—" Yee'm— ose then she has to be polite, an' take th' little : " I have called on you seven years, Miss ,Vaite. All this time I have passioeately loved you. To-morrow or the next day I shall probably come up and ask you to marry me. What will you say ?"—Miss Waite (confused): "Oh, this is so sudden !" IT WAS AT TIIE CLUE.—Waiter (at 11 p.m.): "There is a lady outside who says that her hus- band promised to be home early to-night."—All (rising): "Excuse me for a moment." MR. HOOLIGAN: "Shure, 'tis a molglity con- venient arrangement living in wan apartment, Mrs. Finucane. Woi, whin Oi want to go from the dhrawing-room to the shtudio or the kitchen, Oi just stay where Oi am." ON reaching a certain spot the driver turned round on his seat and observed to the passengers : —" From this point the road is only accessible to mules and donkeys; I must therefore ask the gen- tlemen to get out and proceed on foot. THE MAGISTRATE: "Didn't I tell you the last time that you were here that I wanted to see your face in this court no more?" Weary \Vat- kins : "You did, yer onner, and that is exactly what I told the cop. PERKINS: "When I knew Harry he used to paddle his own canoe; but 'how he seems to be a perfect wreck." Jorkins "Yes ; but since then he got married, and his wife is one of those crea- tures that like to rock the boat." HIS DEFINITION. Little Nell: "What is imaitination, Willie?" Little Will : "It is what makes you think a bee's stinger is six feet long." 1 HE RESCUE.—" He shall bite the dust !" hissed the wicked tyrant. "Is there no hope ? " faltered the heroine. "Hark !" exclaimed the hero. " M•Ahinks I hear the clatter of hoofs. Perhaps it is a watering•cart." BANK DEFAULTER: "I would give ten years of my iite to get out of this scrape." His Lawyer: " That's about what you will get if I don't succeed in getting you off." FARMER HORNBEAK " You know how old Zcb Flint hain't had no faith in banks, an' has been keepin' his money in a teapot in the barn, where he could go an' gloat over it whenever he felt like it?" Mrs. Hornbeak "Well, what has hap- pened, Eery ? " Farmer Efornbeak (drily): "We. al, Zeb, hain't got no faith in barns now." MILLICENT: "How, long did your trip to Rome occupy ?" Madeline: "Oh, a week altogether— there and back." Millicent: "And you saw everything ?" Madeline : "Oh, yes; you see, there were three of 'us. Mother went to the picture-galleries, I examined the monuments, and father studied local colour in the cafes." LORD GLAsGOW was once travelling by rail, and tendered a five-pound note to the booking-clerk for a ticket. " Put your name on it," said the youth, and Lord Glasgow indorsed it as requested, putting simply the name "Glasgow," as is cus- tomary, with nobles, and handed the note back. " Here, hi I you stupid man !" cried the clerk ; "I wsnt to know who you are, and not where you're going to." HUSBAND (breathlessly)—"l am to start on a trip in two hours, and you can go with me if you I can get ready." Wife—" Certainly I can. It won't take me more than 10 minutes to pack, and ' that will leave me one hour and 50 minutes to dress." "Now, children," said the Sunday school teacher of the juvenile class, "our lesson to-day tells us of the powers possessed by kings and queens. Can any of you name a still greater power?" " Yes'm ; I can," replied one little fellow. "'What, Willie? " asked the teacher. "Aces," was the unexpected answer. DOLLIE—" I wonder why Love is represented always with wings?" Mollie—" They are for him to use in flying out of the window when Poverty comes in at the door. Don't you know that much ? " NuwED-" According to you, I never told you a single truth before we were married." Mrs. Nuwed—" Oh, George, you weren't quite so bad as all that. Don't you remember you always used to say you were unworthy of me?" MISTRESS : "Not going to marry that sweep after all, Jane? Why, I thought it was all set• led." Jane: "So it was, mum • but the Net is, I saw him with a clean face for the first time last night, and I can't marry him! You've no idea how ugly he is when he's washed, mum." TALK to woman about religion, she sighs ; talk to her of love, she simpers ; talk to her of art, she yawns ; talk to her of science, she goes to sleep ; but talk to her of dress, and she will open her eyes and give you the entire attention of her ears. HE KNEW THE SEX'S WAYS.—Miss Shopper: "Do you think you will be able to match this ribbon ?" Draper's Young Man (with a sigh ,of re- signation) : "After you have looked at everything else I shall." A DOUBTFUL Slay.—" I used to think my hus- band loved me," said Mrs. M'Bridie, with a sigh. "And doesn't he?" inquired her bosom friend. " Well, he insists upon our living within his in- eome." A RICH but ignorant lady, who was rather am- bitious in her conversational style, in speaking of a friend said—" He is a paragram of politeness." " Excuse me," said a wag sitting next to her, " but do you not mean a parallelogram ? " "Of course I do," immediately replied the lady ; "how could I have made such a mistake ?" " WHEN are you going to settle this bill?" " We've had a settlement already." " When?" "The last time you called." "How so?" "Didn't I tell you then that I meant to settle the bill ?" "Yes." "Very well, then; wasn't that a settle meant ? " MINNIE: "Mr. Billie, if I should consent to be your wife, are you sure you would never object to my dressmaker's bills, never refuse to buy theatre. tickets when asked, never—er -in fact, never object to any expenses which I might wish to incur ?" Mr. Billie "Darling, I swear it r Minnie: "Well, Mr. Billie, if you can't hold on to your money any better than that I must say 'No.'" "On, papa I " exclaimed little four-year-old Ned. "Look what a bright star ! " Yes." replied the father, "and it three times as large as our earth." "Oh, no, it isn't," said Ned. " Why do you doubt it?" asked his father. "Because if it was it %.•ould keep the rain oil." SUNDAY-SCHOOL teacher (illustrating the "still, email voice")—" Wtat is it, dear children, that makes you feel so uncomfortable and unhappy after you have done something which you ought nut to do?" Dear child—" A liekin.' " FARMEB'S Wi`e (of vinegary visage): " Navy you get away from here quick, or I'll call my huslia .." Tramp: " Your husband ain't at home.' Farmer's Wife : "How do you know?" Tramp : " Well, mum, I've alters noticed that when a man is mart ed to a woman wot looks like you he's never at ha.ne 'cepting at meal times." LITTLE Elmer: "Pa, what is an extemporaneous ker ?" Professor Broadhead: "One who can ently about nothing without any previous I tion."</p>
